HT_DATA.load("detail", {"kpk2tdsPh0A":{"wy":"Held up as a legendary deep dive into game internals, explained lucidly and with humor, and a gateway to the channel.","q":{"x":"This guy literally has a PhD in SM64. It's insanely complicated, and surprisingly lucid.","i":18074928,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":40335588,"author":"RadiozRadioz","month":"2024-05","story":"Super Mario 64's unopenable door has been opened [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep knowledge of game internals presented approachably","quote":"I recommend this channel in general. He has extremely deep knowledge of the inner workings of the game","text":"I recommend this channel in general. He has extremely deep knowledge of the inner workings of the game, and presents it in an approachable way. Other classics: A 3 hour 45 minute deep dive into the game's infamous invisible wall bug (useful for understanding the linked video): [LINK] His most famous video, a summary of the extremely involved and complex methods required to beat a particular level using the lowest number of A button presses - in this case, 0.5 : [VIDEO]"},{"id":36726365,"author":"keepamovin","month":"2023-07","story":"Super Mario 64 – Watch for Rolling Rocks – 0.5x a Presses [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious and brilliant 12-hour speed build-up","quote":"I love how there's so many super super smart people on the internet!","text":"I kind of lost it at the point where he \"builds up speed for 12 hours\". AHAHA [VIDEO] His mind is so clear. I love how there's so many super super smart people on the internet! :)"},{"id":24017436,"author":"ruytlm","month":"2020-08","story":"Spherical Geometry Is Stranger Than Hyperbolic","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the greatest weird game explanations","quote":"one of the greatest weird explanations of video game things ever","text":"At 5:45[0] in that video, he makes a reference to one of the greatest weird explanations of video game things ever: the explanation[1] of how to beat the Watch for Rolling Rocks level in Super Mario 64 with only half an A-press, which is highly worth a watch if you like this stuff about geometry. And if that was interesting, I also recommend the history of any percent speed runs for Super Mario Sunshine.[2] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":18074928,"author":"kchoudhu","month":"2018-09","story":"'Super Mario Bros.' speedrunner hits nearly inhuman 4:55 world record","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insanely complicated yet lucid explanation of SM64","quote":"This guy literally has a PhD in SM64. It's insanely complicated, and surprisingly lucid.","text":"Did you ever see this one? [VIDEO] This guy literally has a PhD in SM64. It's insanely complicated, and surprisingly lucid."},{"id":14451548,"author":"joshguthrie","month":"2017-05","story":"Jailbreaking Super Mario World to Install a Hex Editor and Mod Loader [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Famous video that provoked a riot in gaming circles","quote":"Pannenkoek2012 is both genius and crazy awesome at the same time.","text":"Pannenkoek2012 is both genius and crazy awesome at the same time. Its video \"0.5x A Key Presses\" provoked a riot in video game circles some times ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12558905,"author":"baconomatic","month":"2016-09","story":"Super Mario 64 – 1996 Developer Interviews","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Takes speedrunning to the extreme","quote":"If you're at all interested in speedruns, this is a great video that takes it to the extreme for Super Mario 64","text":"If you're at all interested in speedruns, this is a great video that takes it to the extreme for Super Mario 64: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11767863,"author":"meanduck","month":"2016-05","story":"Flappy Bird Clone Code Injected into Super Mario World for SNES by Hand","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing thought process behind the half A press","quote":"the amount of thought process went into it is just amazing.","text":"This[1] was mentioned in its other thread. Not related to programming but the amount of thought process went into it is just amazing. [1] \"SM64 - Watch for Rolling Rocks - 0.5x A Presses (Commentated)\" : [VIDEO]"},{"id":29370034,"author":"bspammer","month":"2021-11","story":"Floating point visually explained (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Absolute classic with useless but entertaining knowledge about PUs","quote":"this video is also an absolute classic and very entertaining","text":"I highly recommend watching this video, which explains floats in the context of Mario 64: [LINK] Games are a great way to explain floats because they're so visual. Specifically, check out 3:31, where the game has been hacked so that the possible float values in one of the movement directions is quite coarse. (If you're curious what a PU is, and would like some completely useless knowledge, this video is also an absolute classic and very entertaining: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":24457794,"author":"devindotcom","month":"2020-09","story":"Super Mario Bros. 3 in 3 Minutes – World Record Speedrun Explained [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Equally interesting look into the game's hidden internals","quote":"be sure to watch the \"0.5 A presses\" Mario 64 speedrun video, which is equally interesting","text":"This was interesting. I used to whine about doing this kind of glitched run, but it's just a different category of speedrun. Amazing to have that level of precision, though of course he must have done this hundreds, maybe thousands of times to get it right. If you like this kind of explanation be sure to watch the \"0.5 A presses\" Mario 64 speedrun video, which is equally interesting as far as getting into the hidden guts of the game: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23666396,"author":"dx87","month":"2020-06","story":"GNU GCC does not round floating-point divisions to the nearest value","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth look at half-press star and parallel worlds","quote":"here is an in-depth video where someone talks about getting a star with half a button press.","text":"For anyone else interested in similar tricks, here is an in-depth video where someone talks about getting a star with half a button press. It starts out straightforward enough, but eventually gets really interesting when they start talking about parallell worlds that you can only access by abusing bugs to reach unrealistic speeds. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19935703,"author":"roryokane","month":"2019-05","story":"YouTube “Let's Play”s are preserving video game history","stance":"rec","why":"Most culturally referenced gameplay video; explains parallel universes","quote":"It’s quite interesting, though, especially to a programmer.","text":"There are Let’s Plays that I think are better than others, but I don’t think any of them, even the best of them, are popular enough to be part of gamer culture. However, the gameplay video that I think comes closest to leaving “a mark in history” is this single video by pannenkoek2012: [VIDEO] “SM64 - Watch for Rolling Rocks - 0.5x A Presses (Commentated)”. That’s the only video I’ve seen referenced in comments on videos of completely different games. The references are to “parallel universes” and “0.5 A-presses”, terms that are explained in this video. I don’t know if you could call this video a Let’s Play, though, since the commentary is recorded after the gameplay, it’s not part of a larger playthrough of the whole game, and the video has custom-made animations to aid in explaining the game, not just raw gameplay footage. It’s quite interesting, though, especially to a programmer. Among popular Let’s Play series, one I recommend is the Achievement Hunter Minecraft Let’s Play series: [LINK] . (Yup, they snagged the username “LetsPlay”.) In each episode, members of the Achievement Hunter group of players play some custom-made activity together in Minecraft. I don’t like the personalities of the players/commentators that much, but the immense creativity of the challenges and the players’ occasional funniness make it worth it. After 100 episodes it started getting repetitive though. A few other lesser-known Let’s Plays I recommend: • Cryaotic’s Undertale playthrough, Pacifist "},{"id":14440331,"author":"MrJagil","month":"2017-05","story":"Jailbreaking Super Mario World to Install a Hex Editor and Mod Loader [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Great breakdowns of SM64","quote":"Pannenkoek2012 does great breakdowns of SM64","text":"Pannenkoek2012 does great breakdowns of SM64: [VIDEO] If you like this sort of stuff, reddit.com/r/speedrun can be fun to visit."},{"id":11391878,"author":"CM30","month":"2016-03","story":"The Rest Is Advertising – Confessions of a Sponsored Content Writer","stance":"rec","why":"Example of in-depth fan-made game coverage","quote":"You're not gonna find anything as in depth at this on most sites","text":"Depends what kind of info you're looking for. Are you looking for information on the current middle east geopolitical situation? Then not really. Same deal if you're looking for some sort of longform investigative journalism like the recent one about one of the Truecrypt founders. But if you're a web developer or programmer looking for information about the latest Javascript frameworks or what not, then yes. You're going to find better information in blogs and on social sites that on a mainstream news site or publication, because the former cares about the specific subject and the latter has to try and cover a wide range of things to less of a degree. And in gaming... well, the 'professional' journalism is terrible quality to begin with. The only sites that cover anything remotely interesting are fan or volunteer run, and the most interesting sources for gaming related media are indeed social media sites. You're not gonna find anything as in depth at this on most sites: [VIDEO] Even for more normal 'news' stuff, there are usually tons of free and fan run alternatives. For example, no one tries to get news about Pokemon from a mainstream source, because they've got other things to focus on and don't know a lot about gaming in general. Instead, they go to one of the many fan run sites, like Bulbanews or Serebii.net. This holds true for most gaming related niches, where there'll be a few sites that provide it directly (hours ahead of the mainstream media) then a flood of copycat"},{"id":36729104,"author":"hn92726819","month":"2023-07","story":"Super Mario 64 – Watch for Rolling Rocks – 0.5x a Presses [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I could see how this could be confusing. SM64 does distinguish between before a release and after a release, so technically the game itself doesn't care. I think what they were trying to say is that there really isn't any benefit in speedrunning the game to keep track of releasing (and this can be confirmed in the video too: [VIDEO] ). I think it's possible that if the next level requires .5 a presses but, for example, you are falling with a wing cap in the current level, you have to decide between bumping that half a press into a full a press or losing time when falling with the wing cap. I just don't think it's a common case though because you could use a different route where the held A won't affect you. It looks like in the A Button Challenge (ABC), there are only 5 half a presses: [LINK]"},{"id":25228655,"author":"chungus_khan","month":"2020-11","story":"1.5 is the midpoint between 0 and infinity in Ruby","stance":"reference","why":"How Mario 64's coordinate system works","quote":"This video goes into something vaguely related with how the coordinate system works in Mario 64","text":"This video goes into something vaguely related with how the coordinate system works in Mario 64: [VIDEO] There's a lot of interesting problems that arise when making a 3d game vs a 2d one."},{"id":24457532,"author":"zamadatix","month":"2020-09","story":"Super Mario Bros. 3 in 3 Minutes – World Record Speedrun Explained [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"One in particular caught people's attention to the point it became a meme","text":"The 0 exit speedrun for Super Mario World uses pretty much the exact same method (koopas and all) as well [LINK] SMB3 also has an interesting tool assisted speedrun (non-human doing the inputs) where fast inputs on the second controller cause an overflow on the input buffer allowing for the game to be beaten basically instantly from the start screen. If you like this because of the interesting stuff done to abuse the game The Legend of Zelda Ocarina of Time has pretty much been a history of \"and then a quicker way to corrupt the game was found\". It's not always about going fast either, challenge runs also get a lot of very interesting results from a lot of dedication. Super Mario 64's A Button Challenge (try to beat the game with as few A presses as possible) has tons of outrageously complex tricks with amazing explanation videos. One in particular caught people's attention to the point it became a meme [VIDEO] though it's not actually the most technically impressive to come out of that endeavor."},{"id":21786356,"author":"ranger207","month":"2019-12","story":"The Outer Worlds: Fixing the “game thinks my companion is dead” bug","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's also the source of the infamous \"0.5x A Presses\" video.","text":"You might enjoy speedrunning, especially older games like those for the Nintendo 64 or the original Playstation. In addition, there's the Super Mario 64 0x A Button challenge, an effort to beat the game while avoiding the use of its main mechanic. It's not a speedrun, but the themes of exploiting every glitch possible is the same. It's also the source of the infamous \"0.5x A Presses\" video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15642766,"author":"zzazzdsa","month":"2017-11","story":"A look at inner workings of Joycon and Nintendo Switch","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the (very impressive) Watch for Rolling Rocks in 0.5 A presses video","text":"For a specific example of something like this being important, remember the (very impressive) Watch for Rolling Rocks in 0.5 A presses video from a couple years ago? [VIDEO] (For the uninitiated, this video presents a TAS of Super Mario 64 which beats the level Watch for Rolling Rocks without pressing the A button outside of keeping it held from entering the level.) It turns out that the route presented in the video actually fails console verification, since the crazy things he does trigger some annoying FPU crashes on console, but not on emulator. There is a happy end, though: a fixed route was published after this was discovered, and it passes console verification just fine (assuming the inputs dont desync over the 13 hour run)"},{"id":15328687,"author":"chrsstrm","month":"2017-09","story":"How I Fixed a 10-Year-Old Guitar Hero Bug Without the Source Code [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Beating a level with under one A press","quote":"discussing how to complete a level in Super Mario 64 with less than 1 full A press","text":"This video [0] has definitely made the rounds here before, discussing how to complete a level in Super Mario 64 with less than 1 full A press and in the process discovering parallel universes in the map. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27818762,"author":"kyle-rb","month":"2021-07","story":"Tesla’s $16k Quote for a $700 Fix Is Why Right to Repair Matters","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you're using the shift key, then \"Tesla\" is 6 keypresses whereas \"TSLA\" is only 4.5 (.5 since you don't technically have to release shift, which you can take advantage of in certain cases [0]). [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Dxnr2FAADAs",1],["OP4CKn86qGY",1]]},"jbkSRLYSojo":{"wy":"HN commenters send it as a short antidote to cynicism, showing long-term global progress and the shrinking gap between rich and poor countries.","q":{"x":"Spend five minutes and watch this. Amazing presentation my Hans Rausling","i":36398576,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36398576,"author":"sethammons","month":"2023-06","story":"Are You a Late Bloomer in Work or Love? Maybe You’re Right on Time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing five-minute presentation on rising global prosperity","quote":"Spend five minutes and watch this. Amazing presentation my Hans Rausling","text":"Spend five minutes and watch this. Amazing presentation my Hans Rausling, economist, on rising global property. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35889667,"author":"SeanAnderson","month":"2023-05","story":"Helion’s fusion power purchase agreement with Microsoft","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Antidote to cynicism about the world's trajectory","quote":"I always liked watching this when I was feeling especially cynical about the overall trajectory of the world","text":"It's admittedly a 12 year old YouTube, but I always liked watching this when I was feeling especially cynical about the overall trajectory of the world: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34561628,"author":"somethoughts","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Why Is Everything Declining?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reassuring perspective that human progress continues slowly in the background","quote":"anytime I feel similar to the OP, I watch something by Hans Rosling [1]","text":"This is just personal but anytime I feel similar to the OP, I watch something by Hans Rosling [1] or Anna Rosling [2]. Although 10 and 5 years old respectively, I feel the general sentiment is still true - that human progress is slow, in the background and might be ebbing and flowing in your specific sub-population. And when compared to the present, media narrative driven present its hard to see the improvements happening across the global population. It helps me put stuff in perspective - of course mileage may var (and maybe it's just helping me delude myself into getting out a funk but it works!). [1] Hans Rosling's 200 Countries, 200 Years, 4 Minutes - [VIDEO] [2] See how the rest of the world lives, organized by income - Anna Rosling Rönnlund [LINK]"},{"id":36385484,"author":"sethammons","month":"2023-06","story":"Goodbye, Twilio","stance":"rec","why":"Counterexample to zero-sum thinking, showing rising prosperity","quote":"the following amazing video from Hans Rausling, economist, on rising prosperity?","text":"Can your zero sum hypothesis explain the following amazing video from Hans Rausling, economist, on rising prosperity? [VIDEO]"},{"id":30908496,"author":"sethammons","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: How can I start thinking optimistically?","stance":"rec","why":"Short optimistic outlook on long-term global economic and poverty trends","quote":"Here is a talk from Hans Rosling talking about long term global economic and poverty trends that gives an optimistic outlook.","text":"There are lots of things to worry about. There are lots of promising possibilities. Here is a talk from Hans Rosling talking about long term global economic and poverty trends that gives an optimistic outlook. 4min. [VIDEO] And while that is promising, the question for me comes down to happiness which is different than prosperity. I'm just starting to think more about that."},{"id":28870852,"author":"FredPret","month":"2021-10","story":"You Are Jeff Bezos","stance":"rec","why":"Classic perspective on progress over the last century","quote":"Here's that classic video by Hans Rosling giving us some perspective on the progress over the last century","text":"Fair point. In defense of my moral model, it's backed up by this idea: are things perfect? No. Is the system working? Yes, and astonishingly well. Is there a better way to get from here to there, where \"there\" is some improved state of prosperity and development? No. Human nature is what it is, and our systems must exploit our nature rather than combat it. It's all based on measurable positive outcomes, rather than some theoretical idea that being eye-wateringly rich is somehow bad - morally bad, or bad for society. That idea is rooted in the old, pre-growth, pre-industrial paradigm, when all wealth was seized by force in some way. Today, some wealth is from violence, but the overwhelming majority is new money that was created out of nothing but good old human intelligence. Here are some references backing up my idea that the system works. Here's one focusing on the most important metric, human development: [LINK] This one is from the World Bank. You have to go into the metrics and countries. There are metrics that aren't of interest here, but some are, like access to clean cooking fuels, or electricity. But it's easy to see that most of it is pointing in the right direction. [LINK] Here's a book about all the things that ordinarily fuel existential pessimism: [LINK] Here's a book about how innovation progresses exponentially, and what it means for the masses: [LINK] Here's that classic video by Hans Rosling giving us some perspective on the progress over the last century: [V"},{"id":20144255,"author":"peteforde","month":"2019-06","story":"It's Cold in the Information Age","stance":"rec","why":"Rosling showing the world is getting better by most measures","quote":"The late, great Hans Rosling was superb at breaking down these dimensions in video form","text":"To quote David Mitchell (the comedian, not the author) it would be tempting to take this kind of thing seriously if it wasn't for a damnable sense of perspective. Some thoughts: 1. Much like how the entire centrist media machine feeds off the insanity of the current US administration, somehow this article is at the top of HN. We put it there. (Well, 42 of you did, as of the time I wrote this.) 2. Perhaps the best-kept secret in the world is that it is getting better by almost every measurement. The late, great Hans Rosling was superb at breaking down these dimensions in video form (eg. [VIDEO] ) but if you want the real deal, I recommend Steven Pinker's excellent \"Enlightenment Now\". 3. I couldn't care less that Intel has \"stalled\" since you can now buy a Raspberry Pi that pwns my first computer by an order of magnitude or three and costs less than $25. Meanwhile, Apple is refocusing on Mac hardware again because so many \"poor\" people have phones now that they can see a sales ceiling... this means that a huge percentage of the planet has a significant computer on them all of the time. 4. Not only does age and wisdom allow you to observe tech over many cycles, but the older I get the more I realize that tech is nothing outside of its relationship to politics, culture, and ourselves. Look at how young children just assume everything is a screen, now. Tech is now an important aspect of the daily political conversation. In just 15 years we've gone from a society that rents VHS mo"},{"id":12271986,"author":"Swizec","month":"2016-08","story":"The fight to cheat death is heating up","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the gap between rich and poor countries shrinking over 200 years","quote":"Over the past 200 years, the difference between 1st world and 3rd world has all but vanished.","text":"Not utopia compared to what? My great grandmother had to have 8 kids so that she could have 4 or 5 adult children. That was less than 100 years ago. My grand parents were the children of subsistence farmers without running water or electricity. They went to high school, worked in factories, and could get broadcast TV in their living room. Electricity and running water became a base default. My parents work in an office pushing papers around. Even the society of their youth seems alien in its antiquatedness of both social mores and amenities. I get paid to think things and can reasonably expect to live essentially disease free until my 70's. Achieving 90 in relatively good health and vitality doesn't seem out of the question. Seems pretty utopic to me. What not-utopia are you talking about? EDIT: Watch: [VIDEO] Over the past 200 years, the difference between 1st world and 3rd world has all but vanished. We still have a ways to go, but no utopia is perfect. Even in the movies."},{"id":12272141,"author":"Swizec","month":"2016-08","story":"The fight to cheat death is heating up","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the gap between rich and poor countries shrinking over 200 years","quote":"Over the past 200 years, the difference between 1st world and 3rd world has all but vanished.","text":"The people who grow my coffee beans also live much better than they used to. We're enjoying a before unseen period of peace and prosperity. Globally. Watch: [VIDEO] Over the past 200 years, the difference between 1st world and 3rd world has all but vanished. We still have a ways to go, but no utopia is perfect. Even in the movies."},{"id":11508241,"author":"tim333","month":"2016-04","story":"Business in Africa: 1.2B opportunities","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Africa catching up toward the end of the animation","quote":"Hans Rosling's thing is interesting showing Africa catching a bit towards the end","text":"It took the west a long time to figure out the stable government structures. I saw one theory that civilisation started around the Med because it was easy to get about by boat and was slow in Africa because it was pretty hard to get about before jet aviation. I figure that now they've got internet they'll catch up. Hans Rosling's thing is interesting showing Africa catching a bit towards the end [VIDEO]"},{"id":8674422,"author":"btilly","month":"2014-11","story":"Mean People Fail","stance":"rec","why":"Vivid demonstration of how remarkable long-term progress has been","quote":"See for a vivid demonstration of how remarkable it is.","text":"No, something has changed and PG understands it while you don't. As [LINK] explains, what you are missing is that what people really want is wealth, not money. And wealth is things of value created by people. As productivity improves, we can create more wealth. One form of that wealth is that we can make more efficient use of fundamentally scarce resources. Which admittedly does not increase them, but does decrease pressure on them. The aggregate effect of these trends over time is truly remarkable. See [VIDEO] for a vivid demonstration of how remarkable it is."},{"id":6040231,"author":"btilly","month":"2013-07","story":"High-fructose corn syrup prompts considerably more weight gain than sugar (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Short view of global progress showing poor countries improving","quote":"For perspective, take 5 minutes to watch","text":"I am making a statement about the reasoning that results in current policies. I'm not saying that the policies are well-executed (indeed I think they are not), merely that they have something more than just \"reward a well-connected lobby\" justifying them. As for African poverty and famine, you're right that measures that protect farmers in rich countries will hurt farmers in poor ones. However the #1 cause of African famine remains corruption and war inside of Africa. And on current economic trends, we'll see economic powerhouses emerging in Africa in near decades. So as sad as our neglect of the interests of over a billion Africans is, it does not seem to be a permanent boot in the face. For perspective, take 5 minutes to watch [VIDEO] . There are - today - African countries that are better off than any country in the world was 70 years ago. If others continue on trends that have happened elsewhere, things are going to improve fairly dramatically. I agree with you that our agricultural policies create problems. But I'm optimistic about what the future holds for people who are impacted."},{"id":3180046,"author":"KingOfB","month":"2011-11","story":"Visualizing how a population grows to 7 billion.","stance":"rec","why":"Animated visualization of 200 years of life expectancy by country","quote":"a far cooler visualization in my book!","text":"Here's another visualization focusing on the last 200 years. Doesn't focus on population growth as much as life expectancy, but a far cooler visualization in my book! Starts about 30 seconds in: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2151277,"author":"thret","month":"2011-01","story":"How to Make Trillions of Dollars","stance":"rec","why":"Concise data visualization of global development and capitalism's gains.","quote":"Concisely sums up everything good about free-market capitalism.","text":"Hans Rosling's 200 Countries, 200 Years, 4 Minutes [VIDEO] Concisely sums up everything good about free-market capitalism."},{"id":35843878,"author":"btilly","month":"2023-05","story":"US crypto mining used more power last year than all the computers","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for worldwide economic progress helping the poor","quote":"","text":"I'm bored, so I'll respond. But where to begin? First, the most basic role of money is to be a medium of exchange. With reasonable overhead and in acceptable time, people need to be able to exchange money for goods and services. Crypto fails this in every practical way. If I want to buy pizza with bitcoin, my overhead exceeds the value of the pizza, the average time to make the purchase is longer than the average time to order, have it made, delivered, and get eaten by my family. (Have you ever SEEN hungry teenagers tear into pizza.) Also if people started trying to buy pizza with bitcoin, you'd quickly run into the problem that the maximum number of transactions per second on the bitcoin network is a fraction of the number of pizzas sold per second. Yes, yes. This is all improvable. I've heard that for years. But these issues are not new, and haven't gone away. OK, what next? You are making a big deal out of the fact that there are poor people in horrible third world countries who want to use crypto to escape authoritarian oversight. At best this is a red herring, I lean towards calling it disingenuous. A far more important use case for crypto is crooks trying to get paid for their ransomware attacks without themselves being caught by law enforcement. And the third world has benefitted far more from payment networks like M-PESA than all of crypto. Besides, the single best way to help the poor people in horrible third world countries is to continue the worldwide economic prog"},{"id":20609849,"author":"bsder","month":"2019-08","story":"The big little lie of the TV kitchen island","stance":"reference","why":"Visualization of wealth growth over time","quote":"Here is a Hans Rosling visualization of wealth","text":"> Three hundred years ago, full-time wives did about 60 hours/week of housework. This is a woeful underestimate before labor saving machinery. Here is a talk about the $3500 Shirt and the fact that women spun thread in any idle time: [LINK] Here is a Hans Rosling visualization of wealth: [VIDEO] I remember a story about historian couple (sadly I can't find it) who tried to live like the 1880's. The husband basically came home completely exhausted every single day. The wife, however, literally broke down because the housework was completely relentless. She was completely busy maintaining the household every single waking moment. Big families weren't just because of lack of birth control."},{"id":13267191,"author":"stretchwithme","month":"2016-12","story":"As Groundwater Dwindles, a Food Shock Looms","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of improving global conditions","quote":"","text":"I think it makes sense for a community to own its local roads, the last mile. But I don't think government control makes sense for highways, except perhaps where there is no competition. There are many ways to get to most places. Even with a monopoly, there is a price level where the road makes the most money. You can't raise prices above that without losing money. Even then, it is possible for communities to have contracts with road providers that pay a provider for each mile travelled but that give the community the proceeds of the surge pricing. And certainly communities have to agree to connect to and use a road for it to be profitable. And we certainly don't want to have a situation like that privately owned bridge connecting the US and Canada where there are no other bridges nearby. But I pay an even higher bridge toll to drive to Queens. I'm all for maximizing the benefits. That is the reason we should have to pay to use roads, especially at peak times. Because we waste a lot of time on them when they are free. It's not about collecting the most money for the government. It's about maximizing the flow of traffic when demand is highest. And yes, its hard to eat in places without strong markets and property rights. And things have improved greatly over the last 30 years where people now have more rights and markets set the prices for food rather than political decisions. [VIDEO] The more accurate prices are, the more sensible our decisions can be and the more opportunity"},{"id":8756861,"author":"Pamar","month":"2014-12","story":"ELI – A System for Programming with Arrays","stance":"reference","why":"Example of choreographed data presentation, inspiring gesture-based data queries","quote":"This video gives an idea of what I'd like to have","text":"Ok, well - maybe I'am going offtopic now but what I'd like to have is a _query language_ based on gesture. I.e. something to analyze data with. This video [VIDEO] gives an idea of what I'd like to have, but of course in this case the data \"transformation\" are pre-scripted and the speaker goes through a sort of coreography. Imagine being able to play with data interactively by moving stuff around in space around you."},{"id":7147404,"author":"tim333","month":"2014-01","story":"The world’s rich stay rich while the poor struggle to prosper","stance":"reference","why":"Data showing poor countries have developed over time","quote":"I think if you actually look at the data numerically then Gates wins","text":"So Kay says “... rich countries got rich and why poor countries stay poor” and Gates says that's false. I think if you actually look at the data numerically then Gates wins eg. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1974810,"author":"chroma","month":"2010-12","story":"200 Countries, 200 Years, 4 Minutes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The original video link ( [VIDEO] ) was submitted 5 days ago ( [LINK] ). Then a slightly different url ( [VIDEO] ) was submitted 10 hours ago ( [LINK] ) It seems quite unfair to me that the person who submitted this content first, and did so without linking to their own blog, gets none of the recognition and karma. While 100% of sthomps submissions ( [LINK] ) are links to his own blog, where he mirrors or links to the original content. I just said it ( [LINK] ) but I'll say it again: I think this is a flaw in HN's ranking scheme. If the original link isn't upvoted quickly, it never gets on the front page. After that, the rewards (recognition, traffic, and karma) go to people who mirror the original content or simply link to it from their own websites."}],"r":[["aXQ2lO3ieBA",1],["ICp2-EUKQAI",1],["u4L130DkdOw",1],["wVp5koAOu9M",1],["RWTic9btP38",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["QWveXdj6oZU",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["62tIvfP9A2w",1],["SWEts0rlezA",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["IkdziSLYzHw",1],["7k1ehaE0bdU",1],["YMqgiXLjvRs",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["baFaEvBywGc",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1]]},"d-7o9xYp7eE":{"wy":"HN treats it as the canonical video to link whenever talking to police or interrogations come up.","q":{"x":"If you don't know why, you must watch this","i":27412734,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27412734,"author":"lmilcin","month":"2021-06","story":"Tips for Interviewing over Zoom","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why you should never talk to police","quote":"If you don't know why, you must watch this","text":"No, not really. Advice for police interrogation is: never talk to the police. If you don't know why, you must watch this: [VIDEO] Also, I never said this is my advice. In general I like to volunteer information during interview when I am the candidate. I think it lets me control the situation a little bit better and move to ground that I have much more expertise in. I am just saying that I can't fault candidate for not doing this."},{"id":15360537,"author":"Chaebixi","month":"2017-09","story":"King County rolls out Miranda rights tailored for young people","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Relevant whenever talking to police comes up","quote":"This video is always relevant when the subject of talking to police comes up","text":"> If you do want to talk to me, I can tell the juvenile court judge or adult court judge and Probation Officer what you tell me. It's missing the explicit warning that \"what you say can and will be used against you in the court of law.\" I think they should add a clause to the end stating \"and they can use this information against you to get you in trouble.\" This video is always relevant when the subject of talking to police comes up: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48558879,"author":"digitalPhonix","month":"2026-06","story":"Never talk to the police","stance":"rec","why":"Police officer also advises not talking to police","quote":"here's a US police officer saying the same thing","text":"If they wanted to do that, your chances of \"I talk, and it screws me\" are pretty high anyway. Also, don't take my word for it - here's a US police officer saying the same thing: [VIDEO] (skip to the second half) He wouldn't talk to police."},{"id":46095913,"author":"lclc","month":"2025-11","story":"Stopping bad guys from using my open source project (feedback wanted)","stance":"rec","why":"Law professor James Duane on why not to talk","quote":"It's pretty shocking.","text":"Indeed. As to why, see this video with Regent Law Professor James Duane: [VIDEO] It's pretty shocking."},{"id":39266824,"author":"throwaway19091p","month":"2024-02","story":"WA House bill would make it illegal for police to lie during interrogations","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on how innocent statements get used against you","quote":"I highly recommend checking out this lecture when you get a chance","text":"> They can use \"I don't remember\" ? They absolutely can, and will use that in a court of law. Any comment that you make that seems completely fine from your perspective can quickly be turned around to lock you up. I highly recommend checking out this lecture when you get a chance: [VIDEO] Police are not generally concerned with getting the right person, they're just concerned with getting a person."},{"id":38663095,"author":"bacheaul","month":"2023-12","story":"Suspects can refuse to provide phone passcodes to police, court rules","stance":"rec","why":"Law professor gives startling reasons to invoke the Fifth","quote":"Regent Law Professor James Duane gives viewers startling reasons why they should always exercise their 5th Amendment rights when questioned by government officials.","text":"> Don't talk to the police: Regent Law Professor James Duane gives viewers startling reasons why they should always exercise their 5th Amendment rights when questioned by government officials. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31441809,"author":"mulmen","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: I found a pretty extreme data leak and I'm not sure what to do","stance":"rec","why":"Do not talk to police; contact a lawyer","quote":"Do not talk to the police. You have nothing to gain and everything to lose. Contact a lawyer.","text":"This is terrible advice. Do not talk to the police. You have nothing to gain and everything to lose. Contact a lawyer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27859819,"author":"codegeek","month":"2021-07","story":"Illinois first state to tell police they can't lie to minors in interrogations","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on not talking to police","quote":"In case people are not aware yet, please watch this video about","text":"In case people are not aware yet, please watch this video about \"Don't talk to the police\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":20309500,"author":"dandellion","month":"2019-06","story":"Gallery Owner Arrested After Placing Spoon Sculpture at Purdue Pharma (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Lawyer explains never speaking to police","quote":"You might find this interesting, where a lawyer explains why should never say a single word to the police, under any circumstances","text":"You might find this interesting, where a lawyer explains why should never say a single word to the police, under any circumstances: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19937578,"author":"andybak","month":"2019-05","story":"Hertz bug leads to people being erroneously arrested and jailed","stance":"rec","why":"Innocent people also should not talk to police","quote":"Rewatch the original video. It covers exactly this.","text":"> The \"don't talk to police\" mantra is for when you know that you've done something wrong Not true. Rewatch the original video. It covers exactly this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19955654,"author":"conanbatt","month":"2019-05","story":"Seattle police use family DNA, latest science to identify killer 52 years later","stance":"rec","why":"Famous video on why to consult a defense attorney","quote":"A famous video about this topic is here","text":"> I've been part of the group who got asked to give a DNA sample in the Nicky Verstappen [1] case. This was voluntarily, and I reluctantly did this Great story and thanks for sharing this. I want to answer why I think this is a bad idea however. First, I would talk to a legal defense attorney before doing this. A famous video about this topic is here [VIDEO] . 1) The police might have told you it was for one case but it was for another, as the police can lie. 2) The DNA test itself might give a false positive on you or your family members, putting your family to a harsher scrutiny by the police that would have otherwise, and with more judicial justification. In the investigatory zeal they might find other lesser crimes they might want to prosecute just to not be empty handed. In the U.S., they might have charged a family member on a false positive with a ridiculously overblown crime. People have killed themselves over that prosecutorial technique ( [LINK] ) 3) The police could fiddle with the samples and plant evidence if they thought you or someone else did it. (Steven Avery case) 4) The lab might fake results if they are in tandem with the DA ( [LINK] ) You are gambling with your life and the life of your family members when you make that choice. And if you were so sure a family member of yours did not do it, then the police could have come to the same conclusion on their alibis and physical state of your family members. Even if you believe non of that is \"likely to happen\""},{"id":17870694,"author":"mhkool","month":"2018-08","story":"Tesla Directors, in Damage Control Mode, Want Elon Musk to Stop Tweeting","stance":"rec","why":"Lawyer and cop say never speak to police","quote":"is a very serious message from a lawyer and a cop not to say a single word since it will be used against you.","text":"The video titled 'don´t talk to the police' [VIDEO] is a very serious message from a lawyer and a cop not to say a single word since it will be used against you. The same is true for Musk and twitter."},{"id":16633148,"author":"sigstoat","month":"2018-03","story":"‘Testilying’ by Police: A Stubborn Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Advice to stop talking to police","quote":"Record, record record. even better, stop talking to them.","text":"> The police are 100% your enemy once they start questioning you about anything. Record, record record. even better, stop talking to them. [VIDEO] > Catch them in one lie, and you get off scot free. they're not leprechauns, there are no magical rules in effect."},{"id":45151237,"author":"FollowingTheDao","month":"2025-09","story":"AI surveillance should be banned while there is still time","stance":"reference","why":"Old video on not talking to police, extended to surveillance","quote":"","text":"I cannot understate how afraid I am of the use of AI Surveillance. The worst thing is there is nothing you can do about it. It does not matter how private I am online, if the person I am sending things to is not privacy conscious and, say, uses AI to summarize emails, then I am in the AI database. And then there is just the day to day data being scraped, like bank records, etc. I mean a PARKING LOT in my town is using AI cameras to track and bill people in a parking lot! The people of my town are putting pressure on the parking lot owner to get rid of it but apparently the company is paying him too much money for having it in his lot. Like the old video says \"Don't talk to the Police\" [1], but now we have to expand it to say \"Don't Do Anything\", because everything you do is being fed into a database that can possibly be searched. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44928173,"author":"komali2","month":"2025-08","story":"\"None of These Books Are Obscene\": Judge Strikes Down Much of FL's Book Ban Bill","stance":"reference","why":"Classic video on anything said being used against you","quote":"I guess it's time to trudge out the classic","text":"> You can have a conversation about politics You are lucky, you must live in a nice country with normal laws and normal cops. I can't imagine any other reason why you'd just offer this up... Even an American with a thin blue line flag on their house would warn against talking politics with a cop. Check out the videos of the statsi troops going door to door in DC, fawning to locals about how they're there on trump's orders to \"keep them safe.\" Armed to the teeth. You think it's a good idea to try to have a polite political conversation with men like that? You are blessed! For your own safety, never come to America, stay in peace and comfort elsewhere, I genuinely fear how your kind hearted openness will land you into awful trouble in the USA. If you do come, enjoy a day sitting in a courtroom. Watch how the simplest, most meaningless conversation with a cop is read out by the court in a toneless transcript without any context and how a judge will grant probable cause on it, or consider it in sentencing to their detriment. Bon homanie about the slow construction of a local freeway? In court that's lack of attachment and local loyalty, disaffected personality, antisocial tendencies. Complaints about the EU? General disrespect of authority, likelihood to stir up trouble, lack of respect for law and order. I guess it's time to trudge out the classic: [VIDEO] Anything you say will be used against you . > I'm sure you can show me hundreds of cases, when there are millions of policem"},{"id":26942473,"author":"syshum","month":"2021-04","story":"Reliability of police mobile phone evidence questioned after hack","stance":"reference","why":"Support for never talking to police","quote":"So the better plan, for your own personal safety, is to NEVER TALK TO THE POLICE","text":">> Giving specific details like the place and time that you saw a movie at a theater along with any receipts or ticket stubs, would be a huge factor in preventing future law enforcement / prosecutor interactions. This is only true if the cops are actually looking for the Truth, not just a way to close the case as fast as possible. You seem to have faith that the cops / prosecutors are attempting to find the truth, unfortunately I do not share your faith in the system. So the better plan, for your own personal safety, is to NEVER TALK TO THE POLICE [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20197291,"author":"twright0","month":"2019-06","story":"Why people confess to crimes they didn’t commit","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"To be frank, this is bad advice [EDIT: parent wasn't giving advice, so instead this should read as something like \"Talking to police without your attorney present is a very risky idea...\"] that probably literally every criminal defense attorney in the United States will disagree with (I am not a lawyer, however, to be clear). A better framing perhaps is that there are two scenarios if you are being interrogated by the police: (1) The police have enough evidence to charge you with a crime (2) The police do not have enough evidence to charge you with a crime They do not have to tell you (and can actively lie!) which scenario you are in, and these scenarios are only loosely correlated with whether or not you actually recently committed a crime. In scenario (1), they're going to charge you with a crime no matter what you say - they already have enough evidence. And anything you say to the police in this scenario can only be used as evidence against you to further strengthen the police's case, it cannot be used in your own defense in court. So it's better to remain silent. In scenario (2), if you remain silent, they cannot charge you with a crime - they don't have enough evidence and you're not giving them any (asserting your right to remain silent is not evidence). If you answer their questions, you might accidentally give them enough evidence to charge you with a crime, like contradicting your own story because your memory is fallible. The police are allowed to lie to you, ask y"},{"id":19654748,"author":"giancarlostoro","month":"2019-04","story":"Tracking Phones, Google Is a Dragnet for the Police","stance":"reference","why":"Why not to speak to police without a lawyer","quote":"I sure wont be talking to you without a lawyer no matter what[0].","text":"This also showcases how innocent until proven guilty is not honored at all. Don't throw me in jail just because you're investigating me, throw me in jail if I run off out of town or do something sketchy to interfere with the investigation. If I am not guilty I wont be running out of town, but I sure wont be talking to you without a lawyer no matter what[0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17787801,"author":"candiodari","month":"2018-08","story":"They’re Falsely Accused of Shoplifting, but Retailers Demand Penalties","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You should look at this from a legal perspective. Now I understand the \"real\" situation, and that everybody should just magically know it, and the world should be just and true and ... Fact is ... it isn't. You don't know if people are playing fair or not, and that means 1) at some point you make a guess if they are ... or not. There are going to be false positives and false negatives. 2) there will be disputes about this So we need a justice system. Ok, now let's see how people react once they get into the situation where there's an actual dispute about whether they stole or not. That dispute can happen regardless of whether you stole or not, and either side may be fraudulent, uncooperative, overworked or absent, just to name some basic problems. a) they never have any real legal knowledge, so they are not capable of playing by the rules of the justice system, even if they want to. Nobody does even an introductory course (even though, you're actually sort of legally required to know all laws) b) they don't have a lawyer, and they refuse to pay for this. So they don't know the law, nor are they willing to pay for having someone explain the law to them and give them advice about their options. c) they also don't want to pay taxes to have an extensive police force. So elected officials (presumably with your approval) have instituted \"pay per conviction\" rules, to measure police force effectiveness. So what situation is a cop and, later, the assistant prosecutor in ? They have s"},{"id":13494419,"author":"CiPHPerCoder","month":"2017-01","story":"Ask HN: Do you keep a personal journal?","stance":"reference","why":"Journal contents can be used against you in court","quote":"If you record in your journal anything that can be perceived to place you near a crime","text":"> Thus not keeping a journal because its contents might be used in a court of law is just a sad state of affairs. [VIDEO] If you record in your journal anything that can be perceived to place you near a crime, whether or not you're guilty or the association is a stretch of the imagination, then your words can be used against you without your right to invoke the 5th amendment. If the journal was seized in the execution of a search warrant, you lose the 4th amendment protections too. The winning move is to not play."}],"r":[["xzYgiOC9cj4",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["ovD0aLdRUs0",1],["j-kj2qwJa_E",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1]]},"443UNeGrFoM":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the one long programming video worth watching, a provocative and influential take on C.","q":{"x":"the only 2 hour YouTube video I'll ever recommend","i":34106174,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34106174,"author":"suprjami","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Learn C in 2023?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Watch after learning C basics.","quote":"the only 2 hour YouTube video I'll ever recommend","text":"[LINK] If you have lots of time: [LINK] If you can't be bothered reading a whole book: [LINK] Exercises: [LINK] and [LINK] Once you have syntax and basic algorithms down well, watch this, the only 2 hour YouTube video I'll ever recommend: [VIDEO] Both r/cprogramming and r/C_programming are active, also full of lazy students trying to get people to do their homework. If you come by, describe your problem well with code. Say you're learning for yourself, not for school. Together C & C++ is a good Discord if you prefer live chat: [LINK]"},{"id":34107434,"author":"MonkeyClub","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Learn C in 2023?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Top notch C work from Steenberg.","quote":"the video is heavily recommended","text":"> Once you have syntax and basic algorithms down well, watch this, the only 2 hour YouTube video I'll ever recommend: > [VIDEO] Hard +1 on this! I think Eskil Steenberg work in C is really top notch - the video is heavily recommended, as is an overview of his work in general, he does some awesome stuff with C."},{"id":26018552,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: Best tech talks/videos of 2020-1?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Big influence on the commenter.","quote":"I seriously loved How I Program C","text":"Hi Eskil from Australia :-) I remember you posted that on the page from 40 days ago also hehe. I have too many ideas myself already, so that one wasn't so useful to me, but was interesting. However, I really liked Drama in games [0] and I seriously loved How I Program C [1]. It's been a big influence. Thank you! [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46205855,"author":"breckinloggins","month":"2025-12","story":"Brent's Encapsulated C Programming Rules (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Long, somewhat controversial talk on C practices the commenter mostly agrees with","quote":"Long and definitely a bit controversial in parts, but I find myself agreeing with most of it.","text":"Other resources I like: - Eskil Steenberg’s “How I program C” ( [VIDEO] ). Long and definitely a bit controversial in parts, but I find myself agreeing with most of it. - CoreFoundation’s create rule ( [LINK] ). I’m definitely biased but I strongly prefer this to OP’s “you declare it you free it” rule."},{"id":44404911,"author":"ykonstant","month":"2025-06","story":"Untangling Lifetimes: The Arena Allocator","stance":"rec","why":"Classic talk defending C, provocative but worth considering","quote":"I am sure the video above will cause immediate disagreement (I think it goes too far on some topics), but I urge people to consider the ideas contained within.","text":"The author's defense of C reminds me of this classic youtube video: [VIDEO] ... I am sure the video above will cause immediate disagreement (I think it goes too far on some topics), but I urge people to consider the ideas contained within. (I seem to have mis-posted this to another thread?)"},{"id":36151600,"author":"59nadir","month":"2023-06","story":"I think Zig is hard but worth it","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what laser focus on one language and a dogfooded code mountain yields.","quote":"I think \"How I program C\" by Eskil Steenberg is an interesting window","text":"For what it's worth I think this is an excellent choice. Back in 2019 I was deciding whether I wanted to pursue Zig full-time and one of the upsides that I determined was that once you reach critical mass writing all of your code for tools and things in Zig you end up with things that are really exactly what you need and with a very high baseline for speed, flexibility, and so on. Right now I'm considering the same thing but with Odin and for many of the same reasons that I had for Zig; it's an excellent language to write foundational code in and once you do, you end up being able to build significantly more understandable, reliable, stable and consistent things. This, but on a company level, is a real multiplier. Once you adopt a couple of Python scripts and that's OK, you give up the possibility of wielding this sharp spear. Edit: I think \"How I program C\" by Eskil Steenberg is an interesting window into what you can get if you laser focus on a language and environment and allow yourself to build up a mountain of code that you dogfood: [VIDEO] At some point I will likely soft-retire and at that point it's exceedingly unlikely that I'll bother with using other people's libraries except a few key ones that I think are decent, and at that point there really is no reason I'd sit down and write these fundamentals in anything but a lower-level language. Odin, Zig or something like it would pretty much be the only thing on the table."},{"id":26913006,"author":"quelsolaar","month":"2021-04","story":"Beej’s Guide to C Programming [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Long talk with in-depth thinking about pointers.","quote":"it goes in to dept about how to think about pointers.","text":"Try watching this: [VIDEO] Its long and is filled with other stuff about C programming but it goes in to dept about how to think about pointers. Good Luck!"},{"id":26313269,"author":"quelsolaar","month":"2021-03","story":"Thoughts Around Naming Variables","stance":"rec","why":"Longer explanation of C style including naming.","quote":"A much longer explanation of how is use C that goes in to more depth about naming can be found here","text":"As a C programmer i have two ways I go about selecting names. For anything uniqe, I use long post fix names. I think of them as a directory path to the functionality. So instead of: create_object(); delete_object(); I do: project_module_object_create(); project_module_object_delete(); I then do the same with files, so the above would probably reside in a file called: project_module_object.c This makes everything easy to find, you know where things belong and you can search for partial names to find instances of interfaces. For local variables, I have a small set of variable names that all have specific meninges like i, j, k, f, sum, found, best, length, count, that I always use in the same way. They therefore become very easy to read since I know their use. If something doesn't fit the limited set of common variables, I revert to long post fix names. A much longer explanation of how is use C that goes in to more depth about naming can be found here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23760425,"author":"quelsolaar","month":"2020-07","story":"Finally releasing my 15 years of C libraries as open source","stance":"rec","why":"Steenberg's own programming style and philosophy.","quote":"For more on my programing style, and philosophy watch this","text":"All code written in C89 Written from scratch, without any dependencies. All operating system interaction is warped and ported. FreeBSD License. -Forge: stdlib with math, text, A*, geometry math, memory debugger and much more. -Imagine: Threads, timing, setting, lib loading. -Betray: Platform layer, with plugin support, VR, Multi-touch, controllers and more. Win32 , Linux and limited MacOS support. -Relinquish: Rendering library, wrapping OpenGL supports and hides a wide range of extensions. -Seduce: 3D UI toolkit, text rendering and editing, fonts, 3D manipulators, cameras. Mouse / multi-touch / multi-user / controller support. -Testify: Networking and serialization. TCP, UDP, Files, NAT Holepunching, local/global service discovery. -Hxa: asset file format, FBX and PNG implementation. Graphics processing. -Json parser. These libraries have been used extensively in software production for all my applications including LOVE, Exo, Stellar, MoF, and Loq Airou (source included). You can find out more about these at: [LINK] . For more on my programing style, and philosophy watch this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13883253,"author":"signa11","month":"2017-03","story":"Principles for C programming","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by Eskil Steenberg on how he programs in C.","quote":"is, imho, pretty good as well.","text":"this video: [VIDEO] , from a talk by eskil-steenberg titled 'how i program in c' is, imho, pretty good as well."},{"id":46215617,"author":"sph","month":"2025-12","story":"Dependable C","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of a C programmer far from vibe coding","quote":"","text":"The author of this website couldn't be further from any notion of vibe coding: [LINK] [VIDEO] Vibe coders follow the herd (Python, JS, Rust), they don't write any C."},{"id":36758598,"author":"ryu2k2","month":"2023-07","story":"The C Programming Language: Myths and Reality (1)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows object inheritance isn't hard to implement in C.","quote":"Object inheritance is actually one that isn't too difficult or complex to implement","text":">You don’t have inheritance [..] Modern PL features are more or less wrappers around comparatively complex C code. Object inheritance is actually one that isn't too difficult or complex to implement: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36763954,"author":"jll29","month":"2023-07","story":"The C Programming Language: Myths and Reality (1)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows object inheritance implemented in C.","quote":"Object inheritance is actually one that isn't too difficult or complex to implement","text":"> Modern PL features are more or less wrappers around comparatively complex C code. Object inheritance is actually one that isn't too difficult or complex to implement: [VIDEO] True, and if you implement it as a preprocessor, that's exactly how C++ started in 1979 (Stroustrup's \"C with classes\" Cfront pre-processor: [LINK] )."},{"id":36222928,"author":"gavinhoward","month":"2023-06","story":"I hate almost all software","stance":"reference","why":"Example of building one's own software stack from scratch.","quote":"","text":"Once I have an actual working piece of software, I will announce it on HN. This framework is not meant to be shared really; it's more building my own software stack from scratch, kind of like [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36341639,"author":"gavinhoward","month":"2023-06","story":"The Stupid Programmer Manifesto","stance":"reference","why":"Model for building your own stack.","quote":"","text":"Boy, I feel this. I am also a 0.5x programmer. I write dead-simple C; I don't even touch web development, which I consider more complex than C development with Valgrind. In fact, I've spent nearly 3 years writing a build system. That's how slow I am, and it's made some acquaintances laugh at how long I've worked without result. But in reality, I'm not just building a build system. I'm also building my own stack, a la [1], including replacing as much of libc as I can and changing the OS API's I use. I think that soon enough, this stack and this simplicity will become my superpowers and make me a 10x programmer. Being a stupid programmer means you won't be clever. And if you're not clever, you will be able to debug it, even though it is twice as hard to debug than to write. [2] Good debugging is a superpower in itself. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":33569731,"author":"suprjami","month":"2022-11","story":"Size is the best predictor of code quality (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Steenberg's idea of code width as a quality indicator.","quote":"Eskil Steenberg has talked about *width* of code as a more likely quality indicator.","text":"> It's generally a good thing to write code which takes up more space if it's clearer to understand what's going on. Eskil Steenberg has talked about *width* of code as a more likely quality indicator. Consider the difference between: func(int i, void* p) And: modulename_objectname_operation(int count, void *ptr) Reference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22021330,"author":"bordercases","month":"2020-01","story":"We’re approaching the limits of computer power – we need new programmers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The fundamental tradeoff is between control and abstraction. Better control typically means going closer to machine/operational semantics, better abstraction typically means going to denotational semantics. Compilers are what mediate between these two domains, but tend to become more bloated as they have to accommodate both more diverse hardware and more numerous languages. This helps the working programmer ignore the problem of writing good code but only for so long. It only delays the inevitable as the returns from clever compilation can't go on forever, and in fact these returns become more volatile as hardware architectures become more complex (typically through more cores or extra caches, incurring synchronization costs). Thus for maximum performance through binaries one would have to practice tweaking compiler settings which just creates another layer of abstraction and defeats the point of having this step automated for you. Programmer training in particular needs to become both more comprehensive and more specialized. More comprehensive means knowing how each layer of abstraction gets built up from the most common machines (like x86). More specialized means filtering out a lot of people who were trained-for-the-tool and facilitating more cross collaboration between those that can program in a domain but not program for performance. This might mean better methodologies for prototyping across domains or experimentation with organizational structures to complement such m"},{"id":16663755,"author":"quelsolaar","month":"2018-03","story":"C for All","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"If you have too much time on your hands","text":"This is the wrong way to go about it. The syntax is great, what is needed is updates to reflect modern hardware: -Vector types. (With arbitrary sizes not just up to vec4 for like on GPUs) -New operators like clamp, and other intrinsic. (See GLSL and modern instruction sets) -Min/max: |< >| -Dot: -Cross product: >< -Swizzle: a.3.2.1.0 -Qualifiers for warping behavior. -Standard library with cash management hints. -Define qualifiers for padding of structs to be defined. I would also argue you could change some things in the spec to make it easier for compilers to optimize, like making the calling a switch without a catching case undefined behavior. As for the syntax itself, I could find being able to type multiple break commands in a row to get out of more then one loop useful, but its not a big thing. I would probably drastically restrict the power of the pre-processor too. (If you have too much time on your hands: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":14597811,"author":"jcalabro","month":"2017-06","story":"Why Python Is Not My Favorite Language (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Steenberg's point about wanting control in the end.","quote":"In the beginning you always want Results. In the end all you want is control","text":"A comment I find myself returning to whenever this conversation comes around is from Eskill Steenberg: \"In the beginning you always want Results. In the end all you want is control\" [0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1]]},"r3snVCRo_bI":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it whenever a thread asks why clones of or alternatives to YouTube, Reddit or Twitter fail or turn toxic.","q":{"x":"Folding Ideas (on YouTube) had a solid video about VidMe, which is a case study in how NOT to do this","i":21507171,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21784757,"author":"MrRadar","month":"2019-12","story":"An account is now required to read Reddit comment threads?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why alternatives to popular platforms attract toxic users first.","quote":"made a very insightful video about the problem of setting up \"alternatives\" to popular social media platforms","text":"Any \"Reddit alternative\" will have a hard time gathering users as long as Reddit exists (and doesn't alienate a critical mass of their users ala Digg). Dan Olson of the YouTube channel Folding Ideas made a very insightful video about the problem of setting up \"alternatives\" to popular social media platforms (having lived through several painful platform transitions himself): [VIDEO] The tl;dw is that if your \"alternative\" platform doesn't launch with unique and valuable features of its own to attract users away from the original platform then you will only attract toxic people who get kicked off the original platform as the first users of your \"alternative\" platform (because they are the only ones really in need of an \"alternative\"). This creates a self-reinforcing pattern where non-toxic users are repulsed from your alternative platform by the toxic users so growth only comes from more toxic users. You can clearly see this in the dynamic between Reddit and Voat."},{"id":15349550,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-09","story":"Google pulls YouTube off the Amazon Echo Show","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Very good explanation of why cloning YouTube fails.","quote":"For a very good explanation of why, see Folding Ideas video essay \"VidMe or Why Platforms Aren't Your Friends\"[1].","text":"> Vid.me Unfortunately, simply cloning youtube isn't a competitor without also cloning youtube's bankroll. For a very good explanation of why, see Folding Ideas video essay \"VidMe or Why Platforms Aren't Your Friends\"[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36166600,"author":"6D794163636F756","month":"2023-06","story":"Reddit Is Killing 3rd-Party Apps, the Best Way to Read the Site","stance":"rec","why":"Competitors without features attract those banned from the original.","quote":"A competitor that can't sell itself on features will inevitably gain traction most with those banned from the original platform.","text":"Folding ideas has a video on vidme that goes into this. A competitor that can't sell itself on features will inevitably gain traction most with those banned from the original platform. Currently that means a new platform will have very angry, very extreme voices. That is a terrible way to start a platform. Here is the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31159120,"author":"Qub3d","month":"2022-04","story":"Twitter set to accept Musk's $43B offer – sources","stance":"rec","why":"Alt platforms had inherent problems before political bent.","quote":"somewhat related and worth a watch: Folding Ideas' 2017 video essay about YT alternatives.","text":"That's true, but its a deeper problem with that. The moderation is a sort of \"in protest\" form, with lots of deliberate \"blind eyes\" and slow action so they don't get deplatformed by the app stores etc. Beyond the moderation, these sites have the problem that they are inherently set up as a sort of opposition haven -- \"we're twitter for people banned from twitter!\" This results in a phenomenon Hank Green calls the \"worst people problem\"[0]. I'm not a huge fan of the judgemental term, but the core idea is similar to pointing out the inherent contradiction of, say, an anarchists' society. You end up with a userbase that is defined by its desire to flaunt rules and reject any normative standards. [0]: [LINK] (somewhat related and worth a watch: Folding Ideas' 2017 video essay about YT alternatives. Alt platforms have had inherent problems even before the more recent political bent: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":29232996,"author":"oriki","month":"2021-11","story":"YouTube takes down independent court livestreams","stance":"rec","why":"Covers creator versus platform relationship and VidMe's userbase problem.","quote":"a Folding Ideas video[1] that covers the discussion of the creator vs platform relationship overall","text":"I mean, not just perception issues, but also the fact that you're inherently setting yourself up to build a platform with a core userbase of \"People banned from [the more popular version of the platform they're cloning].\" You build VidMe and the first people interested in VidMe are people that got kicked off of YouTube, which it turns out are rarely oppressed free speech advocates and much more often trolls and similarly unwanted individuals. If your userbase is entirely toxic, nobody wants to stay there, and your platform never really succeeds because of that. It's worth noting that I'm loosely parroting the content of a Folding Ideas video[1] that covers the discussion of the creator vs platform relationship overall. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21507171,"author":"etchalon","month":"2019-11","story":"YouTube says it has ‘no obligation’ to host anyone’s video","stance":"rec","why":"Case study in how not to build a YouTube alternative.","quote":"Folding Ideas (on YouTube) had a solid video about VidMe, which is a case study in how NOT to do this","text":"It would if it had a legitimate new take on the video hosting problem and wasn't just \"YouTube, but for free speech\", which would immediately become \"YouTube, but for Nazis\". Folding Ideas (on YouTube) had a solid video about VidMe, which is a case study in how NOT to do this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19662522,"author":"anitil","month":"2019-04","story":"YouTube is trying to reward “quality” content","stance":"rec","why":"Early adopters of new platforms are most toxic for the original.","quote":"There's a great video that goes through this phenomenon (in the context of Youtube vs VidMe)","text":"There's a great video that goes through this phenomenon (in the context of Youtube vs VidMe) [0]. It points our exactly that the people who adopt a new platform tend to be the people most toxic for the original. I have no idea how you get around this. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19091760,"author":"anitil","month":"2019-02","story":"Reddit is raising a huge round near a $3B valuation","stance":"rec","why":"Issues with secondary services and toxic early adopters.","quote":"There's a great video by Folding Ideas which talks about the issues with secondary services","text":"There's a great video by Folding Ideas which talks about the issues with secondary services ( [VIDEO] - this is in the context of video platforms) One big 'bootstrapping' problem is that the first to adopt a new platform are the people who are _too toxic_ for the original."},{"id":19126886,"author":"8note","month":"2019-02","story":"Ask HN: Alternative HN-Like Communities?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why absolute free speech sites don't flourish.","quote":"I think this is a good description of why an absolute free speech site will never flourish","text":"I think this is a good description of why an absolute free speech site will never flourish: [VIDEO] what you describe is basically just Reddit, but with the people nobody wants to talk to on Reddit ... so mostly just the people you don't want to talk to on Reddit talking to each other,which has no mass appeal"},{"id":32107859,"author":"sascha_sl","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: How have you deGoogled your life?","stance":"reference","why":"Made the point that alternative platforms polarize quickly.","quote":"Folding Ideas made this point (about VidMe) half a decade ago","text":"No, you really don't. You get an exaggeration of the margins of society (very often one specific direction) that is discriminated against elsewhere, unless you're also the biggest and most profitable platform. And that's YouTube. You saw the same effect with Gab. These spaces become polarized very fast based on who is already there and what the admins believe is adequate moderation. Odysee seems more right leaning. I don't see queer content creators on there, for instance, despite YouTube heavily demonetizing and recommendation-banning them too. This is why I really want federated spaces, because they can serve different margins of society without becoming isolated islands. Folding Ideas made this point (about VidMe) half a decade ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30033765,"author":"8note","month":"2022-01","story":"YouTube deactivates channels linked to Oath Keepers","stance":"reference","why":"VidMe as example of competitor needing incentives for creators.","quote":"","text":"Unlikely. A YouTube competitor has to offer worthwhile incentives to creators who can also be on youtube Eg. Vidme: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25174969,"author":"8note","month":"2020-11","story":"Reddit is slowly killing the old interface? native image galleries are invisible","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on building alternatives to established platforms.","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] If you're going to make an X alternative, you have cater to it's core audience. In Reddit's case, it's the power users (folks who post all the content) the moderators, and the commenters. If you don't give them stuff Reddit won't, you won't do well. A new platform has to be significantly better for all of those groups or it doesn't have a chance"}],"r":[["Erp8IAUouus",2],["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1]]},"996OiexHze0":{"wy":"HN repeatedly points people to it as the clearest way to finally understand OAuth, to the point that one team made it required viewing for new hires.","q":{"x":"it was a requirement for new joiners to watch this in their first week","i":40060108,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40060108,"author":"willsmith72","month":"2024-04","story":"Learn OAuth by building a client with Node.js","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Learn OAuth from zero.","quote":"it was a requirement for new joiners to watch this in their first week","text":"at my old company we worked with auth a lot, so it was a requirement for new joiners to watch this in their first week. i still recommend it if you want to learn oauth from 0 [VIDEO]"},{"id":35719545,"author":"butisaidsudo","month":"2023-04","story":"Why is OAuth still hard in 2023?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clarified all OAuth concepts after confusing docs.","quote":"This video was a huge help though for explaining all of the concepts","text":"I read so many docs when I was trying to implement OAuth and got more and more confused. This video was a huge help though for explaining all of the concepts: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23852496,"author":"sakisv","month":"2020-07","story":"Everything you need to know about OAuth 2.0","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nice, simple, hour-long OAuth explanation.","quote":"It's about 1h long, but it's really worth it.","text":"This also has a very nice and simple explanation of the whole thing: [VIDEO] It's about 1h long, but it's really worth it."},{"id":47099176,"author":"scandox","month":"2026-02","story":"What Is OAuth?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains OAuth from the original problem it solved.","quote":"A classic explainer from almost a decade ago.","text":"[VIDEO] A classic explainer from almost a decade ago. This explains it from the point of view of the original problem it was designed to solve."},{"id":38331757,"author":"krantic","month":"2023-11","story":"Ask HN: Any comprehensive courses on Auth?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among free OAuth/OIDC learning resources.","quote":"","text":"I have not seen a course that covers all of the things you are asking for. The best courses on the oidc/oauth and saml I have seen were the paid ones here: [LINK] On linkedinlearning this one was quite ok: [LINK] Free ressources check: - [LINK] -OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect (in plain English): [VIDEO] [LINK] -OAuth/OpenID by Nat Sakimura(chairman openid foundation) [LINK] For the active directory topic I don't know good ressources"},{"id":38348387,"author":"langur","month":"2023-11","story":"Ask HN: Any comprehensive courses on Auth?","stance":"rec","why":"Insightful explanation of OAuth and OpenID Connect.","quote":"I found Nate Barbettini's video on OAuth and OpenID Connect incredibly insightful for understanding these topics.","text":"I found Nate Barbettini's video on OAuth and OpenID Connect incredibly insightful for understanding these topics. He explains everything so well- [VIDEO] . Additionally, I'm part of the ZITADEL team, an open-source project that's free to download or use in our cloud offering. So, you can always tinker around with it as some others have already suggested. Our blog dives into various security topics, ranging from OAuth, OpenID Connect, and Single Sign-On, Authentication, Federation to emerging issues like Passkeys. We also discuss real-world Identity Management problems and solutions seen by ZITADEL users— [LINK] . For any specific security-related queries, feel free to join the conversation on our Discord chat: [LINK] . We're always discussing and sharing insights on these topics."},{"id":21358017,"author":"manigandham","month":"2019-10","story":"An Illustrated Guide to OAuth and OpenID Connect","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth walkthrough of OAuth and OpenID Connect.","quote":"Also this video is a great in-depth walkthrough","text":"Seems there's still confusion so I'll copy/paste my old comment: OAuth 1.0/2.0 are a set of protocols and standards allowing applications to identify users and get access to their data using existing profiles. OAuth is mostly focused around authorization with claims (which are just key-value pairs) and authentication was an afterthought. OpenID is another standard designed to let people authenticate across the web, launched with a lot of hype in the early web 2.0 days but never took off. OpenID Connect (OIDC) is a new standard based on OAuth 2 + OpenID to provide both authentication and authorization in a single flow. OIDC/OAuth is all based on tokens, which are JWTs containing a bunch of claims that can be validated against the server that issued them. There are ID tokens (for the user info) and Access tokens (for accessing an API on behalf of that user), but some services like social logins don't provide any access tokens. Other providers might have rate limits, or dont have fine-grained permissions, or you maybe you have completely internal APIs that you need tokens for. Also this video is a great in-depth walkthrough: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Erp8IAUouus",1],["wrwgIjBUYVc",1],["NGFhc8R_uO4",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["clnozSXyF4k",1]]},"HI09kat_GeI":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend the series as engaging, substantial physics with just enough math for non-specialists.","q":{"x":"Just enough math to be interesting but nothing requiring a math degree.","i":42991165,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42991165,"author":"mperham","month":"2025-02","story":"Noether's Theorem Revolutionized Physics","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Physics series with just enough math","quote":"Just enough math to be interesting but nothing requiring a math degree.","text":"Sean Carroll’s Biggest Ideas in the Universe YouTube series is fantastic. Just enough math to be interesting but nothing requiring a math degree. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31447161,"author":"Trasmatta","month":"2022-05","story":"PBS SpaceTime","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great physics series by Sean Carroll","quote":"series is really great","text":"I'd also recommend Sean Carroll. His \"Biggest Ideas in the Universe\" series is really great: [VIDEO] I'd also recommend his podcast, Mindscape."},{"id":29523975,"author":"ericbarrett","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Long-form info-dense videos on YouTube you would recommend to anyone?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Intro to modern physics with accessible math","quote":"an intro to modern physics, including homotopy and quantum field theory","text":"The Biggest Ideas in the Universe by Sean Carroll, an intro to modern physics, including homotopy and quantum field theory. He does some math but you'll follow if you've finished high school. Don't miss the Q&A videos in between the 26 regular episodes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26507712,"author":"Diederich","month":"2021-03","story":"Reality as a Vector in Hilbert Space","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Substantial yet approachable physics series with some math","quote":"his presentation is engaging, approachable but also fairly substantial.","text":"The author is [LINK] Shortly after Covid19 started last year, he started a series of videos called \"The Biggest Ideas in the Universe\", playlist here: [VIDEO] I've watched quite a lot of this so-called 'popular' hard science material on youtube, and this series was by far the best I've run across. His approach is a bit different from most others, and his presentation is engaging, approachable but also fairly substantial. Specifically, he doesn't elide all of the relevant math, but doesn't get too deep at the same time. You'll see a lot of integrals, derivatives and graphs. For example, he talks about an hour on the big idea of 'Change', and another \"Space\". Perhaps even more interesting are the followup Q&A videos, which are as long as and perhaps even more detailed than the source. Do give the linked six minute intro video a watch."},{"id":44912398,"author":"jackdoe","month":"2025-08","story":"Death and What Comes Next (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"Helps laypeople ask better quantum mechanics questions","quote":"dont miss the Q&A videos","text":"Ignoring the moral question in the story, many people are satisfied by saying \"haha funny, the cat is both dead and alive\" and they just move on. Reading Pratchett for almost 30 years, it has fueled quite an interested in quantum mechanics in me, he subtly introduces QM teasers in almost every book I read. Particularly Mort. As I dropped out of highschool, I didnt have the luck to properly study physics, but in my 30s, I my existential crisis hit, as they say, a man dies twice, and the stupid half dead cat wouldn't leave me alone. And one night the omnipotent youtube algorithm recommended Susskind's lectures to me, then Feynman, Shankar, .. it just kept going. Now I watch and re-watch them but take screenshots and send them to claude when I am confused (which is still most of the time). If you are a layman like me and just want to know what is up with the cat, I encourage you to watch Sean Carroll's lectures (dont miss the Q&A videos) and it will for sure allow you to ask at least better questions. I love that cat so much. --- Sean Carroll: [VIDEO] Richard Feynman: [LINK] and [LINK] Leonard Susskind: [LINK] Ramamurti Shankar: [LINK] Barton Zwiebach: [LINK] Carl Bender: [LINK] If you struggle with calculus, I found Herb Gross to be partucularly good Herb Gross: [LINK] Of course watch 3blue1brown, alphaphoenix, styropyro etc as well"}],"r":[["-kFOXP026eE",1],["VMj-3S1tku0",1],["pyX8kQ-JzHI",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["vT1JzLTH4G4",1],["MFRWDuduuSw",1],["kTKn1aSOyOs",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["ZXtimhT-ff4",1],["frAEmhqdLFs",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["l7rce6IQDWs",1],["DYvhC_RdIwQ",1],["1b-bijO3uEw",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["rFqL9CkNxXw",1]]},"US8QG9I1XW0":{"wy":"Recommended as a practical demonstration of how functional thinking improves design without going off the deep end.","q":{"x":"Here is a great talk demonstrating that by following a simple functional approach, your code can naturally fall into a “pit of success","i":24918556,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":27881398,"author":"Xunjin","month":"2021-07","story":"Habits of Expert Software Designers (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Among Seemann's amazing functional programming videos","quote":"His videos about functional programming are amazing (Mark Seemann). \"Pit of success\" is one of the best.","text":"His videos about functional programming are amazing (Mark Seemann). \"Pit of success\" is one of the best. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24918556,"author":"adamkl","month":"2020-10","story":"A Theory of Software Architecture","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows simple functional approach naturally leading to pit of success","quote":"Here is a great talk demonstrating that by following a simple functional approach, your code can naturally fall into a “pit of success","text":"I spend a lot of time thinking about these sorts of topics (actually, I just taught a 4 hour session yesterday that used most of the terms in this article), working with newer, less experienced developers, and trying to figure how to distill the essence of \"architecture\" down to something simple that everyone can start with. This is what I’ve started telling people: Use mostly functions, try to make most of them pure. I think that can get people (even new devs) 80% of the benefits (testability, composability, loose coupling, and the ability to reason about code) of more complicated, prescriptive architectures (Hexagonal, Onion, Ports & Adapters, Clean, etc) with a minimal amount of ramp up. Of course, this isn't the solution to every problem (it obviously depends on the domain you are working in, for me its webDev and backends), but I think maybe its a good way for people to start. Edit: Here is a great talk demonstrating that by following a simple functional approach, your code can naturally fall into a “pit of success”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43583546,"author":"tubthumper8","month":"2025-04","story":"Why F#?","stance":"rec","why":"Long talk helpful for adopting a functional mindset","quote":"which is long but good especially to adjust to a different mindset","text":"\"pit of success\" from the previous comment might refer to this video, which is long but good especially to adjust to a different mindset [VIDEO]"},{"id":29575105,"author":"kirse","month":"2021-12","story":"F# Good and Bad","stance":"rec","why":"Seemann's talk on functional pits of success","quote":"Mark Seemann has a good talk on this called the \"functional pits of success","text":"Mark Seemann has a good talk on this called the \"functional pits of success\" -> [VIDEO] Of all the languages I've used nothing is quite as fun as refactoring an F# app down to nothing. Expressive power is king in F# with the whole .NET ecosystem behind it."},{"id":25888734,"author":"adamkl","month":"2021-01","story":"Software engineering topics I changed my mind on","stance":"rec","why":"Great example of benefits of functional thinking","quote":"Here’s some great examples of the benefits of functional thinking without going off the deep end.","text":"> So while FP is just another tool, it's also a framework for thinking that helps people make better choices I’ve been getting more into functional programming this past year (via Clojure, though my day-job is mostly JavaScript/TypeScript) and this is the big takeaway for me. Type theory and monads may have their place, but thinking about separating logic from side-effects is, I think, the most valuable aspect of functional programming. That is a thought process that you can apply in pretty much any language that will give you the biggest bang for your buck. Edit: Here’s some great examples of the benefits of functional thinking without going off the deep end. 1. Boundaries by Gary Bernhardt - [LINK] 2. Pits of Success by Mark Seemann - [VIDEO] 3. Grokking Simplicity by Eric Normand - [LINK]"},{"id":18043510,"author":"pwm","month":"2018-09","story":"Functional core, imperative shell (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on keeping pure core and impure shell","quote":"Here is a good talk about these ideas","text":"The goal is that everything in the core is pure data types and functions. Now, depending on your problem domain, your core can be small or huge compared to the shell. Your question was all about IO/effects. They are impure and belong to the shell. If what you work on does mostly IO then you will have a lot of impure code lying around by definition. Still, the idea is that you should keep the amount of time spent in impure land minimal, ie. drop into pure structures/computation as soon as possible during program execution and only dip out of on occasions when you have to do IO. Here is a good talk about these ideas: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32831054,"author":"kirse","month":"2022-09","story":"Scala isn't fun anymore","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"doing at least one app in F# will vastly improve your C# and how you structure applications.","text":"5yrs ago I was roughly the same, depends how much you commit up front. I had minimal FP experience, so I bought \"Domain Modeling Made Functional\" [1] by Scott Wlaschin and used it for side projects / class libs first. Took about 6 months to experience the full mental shift. Even if you eventually decide you can't commit a team to F#, it's highly worth it because doing at least one app in F# will vastly improve your C# and how you structure applications. [2] I personally just got addicted to the expressive power, it's hard to replicate with any other ecosystem given the full power of .NET under the hood. Giraffe is also a great place to start if you know ASP.NET Core. [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":28337008,"author":"scns","month":"2021-08","story":"The problem of effects in Rust (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"falling into the \"Pit of success\" [2]","text":"Yeah, me too. They published quite a lot of papers using it [0]. The datalog integration is pretty nice too, haven't gotten to learning Prolog yet, but seeing how concise a sudoku solver can be implemented in it was an experience. (edit) you can import Java, Kotlin and Scala in it too, those are automatically Impure. Would enable purely functional architecture [1] and falling into the \"Pit of success\" [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24981198,"author":"kirse","month":"2020-11","story":"Dark’s new backend will be in F#","stance":"reference","why":"Mark Seemann's functional pits of success","quote":"Mark Seeman calls these the \"Functional pits of success","text":"good default behavior and how it nudges programmers to favor predictable and composable solutions Agree. Mark Seeman calls these the \"Functional pits of success\" [VIDEO] I think the toughest thing about F# is that it doesn't offer as many Google-able easy solutions, so I'm stuck using it for utility and client libs at work. Trying to get a whole team onboard is a non-starter. It takes a good 6-12 months to adjust the mindset and most people are just looking to come into work, find their tough questions on StackOverflow and head out for the day."},{"id":21173170,"author":"truculent","month":"2019-10","story":"Learning Haskell is no harder than learning any other programming language","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I'm perfectly happy to fall into the pit of success[1].","text":"> I don’t have that talent, focus and skill. Same. I'm perfectly happy to fall into the pit of success[1]. 1. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qvNCVYkHKfg",1],["8bZh5LMaSmE",1]]},"9eyFDBPk4Yw":{"wy":"HN uses it to illustrate physical latency limits and why performance and distance matter in computing.","q":{"x":"Admiral Grace Hopper Explains the Nanosecond\" is a good illustration of this (segment is only 2m)","i":29600583,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":34351025,"author":"dmoreno","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite illustration in Computer Science?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Makes the cost of inefficiency tangible","quote":"For me it changed my mind on wasting time on less efficient algorithms.","text":"My favorite is more a small presentation than an image: Admiral Grace Hoper Explains a Nanosecond. [VIDEO] For me it changed my mind on wasting time on less efficient algorithms."},{"id":29600583,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Two-minute segment illustrating how long a nanosecond is","quote":"Admiral Grace Hopper Explains the Nanosecond\" is a good illustration of this (segment is only 2m)","text":"> Light is slow, not exactly 1 foot per nanosecond but it's a reasonable mnemonic. \"Admiral Grace Hopper Explains the Nanosecond\" is a good illustration of this (segment is only 2m): * [VIDEO] * [LINK] Full lecture: * [LINK]"},{"id":27593668,"author":"klyrs","month":"2021-06","story":"Your CPU may have slowed down on Wednesday","stance":"rec","why":"Hopper's lesson on caring about performance","quote":"the industry has collectively decided that Grace Hopper is an old codger who doesn't need to be listened to.","text":"Speaking as somebody who never stopped optimizing like its the 90s: there's definitely a learning curve, but when microbenchmarking becomes a reflex, you learn your language in depth and the burden drops quickly. It only seems esoteric to you because the industry has collectively decided that Grace Hopper is an old codger who doesn't need to be listened to. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22831458,"author":"lozf","month":"2020-04","story":"Captain Grace M. Hopper: The Mother of COBOL (1981)","stance":"rec","why":"The famous nanosecond wire demonstration","quote":"For those that haven't seen it","text":"> The nanosecond wire alone should be a compelling argument. For those that haven't seen it: [VIDEO] (Of course, I expect that most here have, but xkcd 10,000 and all that.)"},{"id":22066553,"author":"dougmwne","month":"2020-01","story":"A low-mass planet candidate orbiting Proxima Centauri","stance":"rec","why":"Grace Hopper shows how far light travels in a nanosecond","quote":"Learn from Admiral Grace Hopper","text":"Yes it is and no it isn't. Learn from Admiral Grace Hopper: [VIDEO] The speed of light in fiber optic cable is about 2/3 the speed of light, which is why SpaceX's Starlink will have a potential latency advantage over oceanic fiber optics. It also gives good perspective on what's being thrown away when you add a single ms of latency through a router or a display. 186 miles at the speed of light. Many on HN don't get this, but this is why cloud gaming is perfectly feasible if we're running with low latency displays and inputs, low distance to the edge compute, and few routing hops."},{"id":44599750,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2025-07","story":"When is tech not hype? Tulips, toilets, trains and tabs","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates physical limits on latency via the nanosecond wire","quote":"Latency is limited by physics ( c ) and distance","text":"Latency is limited by physics ( c ) and distance; see Admiral Hopper's nanosecond illustration: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":41639305,"author":"ForOldHack","month":"2024-09","story":"In 1870, Lord Rayleigh used oil and water to calculate the size of molecules","stance":"reference","why":"Hopper's physical demonstration of how far light travels in a nanosecond","quote":"So light travels only 0.3 m / nano second, or 11.802 inches.","text":"The speed of light, because it is so fast, is the most precise physical constant known : 299 792 458 m / s. Less than 7/1000ths away from 300,000,000 m / s. I am not going to sweat this in the least. So light travels only 0.3 m / nano second, or 11.802 inches. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33696297,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-11","story":"How Precision Time Protocol is being deployed at Meta","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how long a nanosecond is","quote":"Adm. Grace Hopper elegantly illustrates 'how long' a nanosecond is","text":"So they state: > One could argue that we don’t really need PTP for that. NTP will do just fine. Well, we thought that too. But experiments we ran comparing our state-of-the-art NTP implementation and an early version of PTP showed a roughly 100x performance difference: While I'm not necessarily against more accuracy/precision, what problems specifically are experiencing? They do mention some use cases of course: > There are several additional use cases, including event tracing, cache invalidation, privacy violation detection improvements, latency compensation in the metaverse, and simultaneous execution in AI, many of which will greatly reduce hardware capacity requirements. This will keep us busy for years ahead. But given that NTP (either ntpd or chrony) tends to give me an estimated error of around (tens of) 1e-6 seconds, and PTP can get down to 1e-9 seconds, I'm not sure how many data centre applications need that level of accuracy. > We believe PTP will become the standard for keeping time in computer networks in the coming decades. Given the special hardware needed for the grand master clock to get down to nanosecond time scales, I'm doubtful this will be used in most data centres of most corporate networks. Adm. Grace Hopper elegantly illustrates 'how long' a nanosecond is: * [VIDEO] How many things need to worry the latency of signal travelling ~300mm?"},{"id":32322953,"author":"BenoitEssiambre","month":"2022-08","story":"Use one big server","stance":"reference","why":"Hopper on hard latency limits of distance","quote":"As Admiral Grace Hopper would point out","text":"I'm glad this is becoming conventional wisdom. I used to argue this in these pages a few years ago and would get downvoted below the posts telling people to split everything into microservices separated by queues (although I suppose it's making me lose my competitive advantage when everyone else is building lean and mean infrastructure too). In my mind, reasons involve keeping transactional integrity, ACID compliance, better error propagation, avoiding the hundreds of impossible to solve roadblocks of distributed systems ( [LINK] ). But also it is about pushing the limits of what is physically possible in computing. As Admiral Grace Hopper would point out ( [VIDEO] ) doing distance over network wires involves hard latency constraints, not to mention dealing with congestions over these wires. Physical efficiency is about keeping data close to where it's processed. Monoliths can make much better use of L1, L2, L3, and ram caches than distributed systems for speedups often in the order of 100X to 1000X. Sure it's easier to throw more hardware at the problem with distributed systems but the downsides are significant so be sure you really need it. Now there is a corollary to using monoliths. Since you only have one db, that db should be treated as somewhat sacred, you want to avoid wasting resources inside it. This means being a bit more careful about how you are storing things, using the smallest data structures, normalizing when you can etc. This is not to save disk, disk is che"},{"id":32329063,"author":"faeriechangling","month":"2022-08","story":"Use one big server","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">As Admiral Grace Hopper would point out ( [VIDEO] ) doing distance over network wires involves hard latency constraints, not to mention dealing with congestions over these wires. Even accounting for CDNs, a distributed system is inherently more capable of bringing data closer to geographically distributed end users, thus lowering latency."},{"id":30706079,"author":"BenoitEssiambre","month":"2022-03","story":"How our free plan stays free","stance":"reference","why":"Hopper reminds us distance matters in computing","quote":"distance matters in computing","text":"I would add that software infrastructure can run incredibly fast and scale incredibly well on modern hardware if you're a bit careful about resource usage. Traditional relational DBs like Postgresql are very scalable on modern hardware. If you take the time to craft a normalized schema with low redundancy being careful about keeping data small, you can achieve performance and resource efficiency and cache efficiency hundreds of time better than bloated distributed document nosql based systems. You can also get better transactional integrity, reduce reliance on queues (use load balancers instead) and get more instant and more atomic error propagation and edge case resolution. You can really build something lean and mean to a point that would be physically impossible in distributed systems or systems that involve multiple hops over potentially congested networks (as Admiral Grace Hopper likes to remind us, distance matters in computing [VIDEO] ). As far as I know, normalized relational databases are still the best at efficiently using multiple levels of physically near caches to their full potential. Most applications don't need to scale horizontally and can easily fit on single servers (that can scale to dozens of cores and terabytes of ram). I hear arguments that it's the engineers that are expensive so it's ok to be wasteful with hardware if it saves engineering time but in my experience the type of engineers that are good at optimizations are often good at engineering in ge"},{"id":27594382,"author":"cogman10","month":"2021-06","story":"Your CPU may have slowed down on Wednesday","stance":"reference","why":"Software companies often do not care about performance","quote":"Pretty reminiscent of Grace Hopper's nanosecond lecture","text":"Pretty reminiscent of Grace Hopper's nanosecond lecture [1] Certainly there are reasons why a feature might slow down a system beyond the branching (for example, if it requires you to load other resources)... but really, the issue is that as pointed out in the youtube video. Software companies very frequently do not care about performance. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1]]},"o0fG_lnVhHw":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a rare look inside a rocket factory and cites it for manufacturing details like welding methods.","q":{"x":"If you haven't watched Smarter Every Day's ULA factory tour I highly recommend it.","i":25389240,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25389240,"author":"tastyfreeze","month":"2020-12","story":"Starship SN9 damaged in an accident in the high-bay","stance":"strong_rec","why":"ULA factory tour including materials improved by computer modeling","quote":"If you haven't watched Smarter Every Day's ULA factory tour I highly recommend it.","text":"A lot of rocketry advancement would not be happening if SpaceX didn't enter as a competitor. For that reason alone SpaceX should be lauded. It helps that SpaceX is pursuing the dream of a space faring civilization that was promised decades ago. Relativity Space looks cool. But until they launch they will sit in the stack of neat tech to maybe be excited about. I am excited by humans doing things to advance the species. I don't care much who is doing it. But, I have been burned by too much vaporware to get excited by marketing pieces. If you haven't watched Smarter Every Day's ULA factory tour I highly recommend it. There is even a nice bit about how their materials have improved as computers were better able to model stresses. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46399201,"author":"tiffanyh","month":"2025-12","story":"Former ULA President and CEO Tory Bruno Joins Blue Origin","stance":"rec","why":"Tory Bruno guest-hosts how rockets are made","quote":"There’s a great “Smarter Every Day” video on how rockets are made and the guest host was Tory Bruno.","text":"There’s a great “Smarter Every Day” video on how rockets are made and the guest host was Tory Bruno. [VIDEO] Two things I recall from this video from years ago 1. Tory really seemed to be a true expert. Not a typical “CEO”. 2. Seems like a genuinely nice guy, see this moment when Tory recognized Dustin’s dad [LINK]"},{"id":40825807,"author":"AdamJacobMuller","month":"2024-06","story":"Navigating Starlink's FCC Paper Trail","stance":"rec","why":"ULA also gave a factory tour","quote":"Also literally everything Destin/SmarterEveryDay puts online is worth watching.","text":"In fairness, ULA has done that too. [VIDEO] Also literally everything Destin/SmarterEveryDay puts online is worth watching."},{"id":28058295,"author":"polytely","month":"2021-08","story":"Everyday Astronaut: Starbase Factory Tour with Elon Musk","stance":"rec","why":"ULA facility tour","quote":"Smarter Everyday did a tour of the ULA facilities [0], which is also worth checking out if you like this kind of thing.","text":"It's so cool to get a tour like this, fascinating stuff. The part at 13:30 seems to be some really valuable knowledge, when he says: if you are not occasionally adding things back in, it means that you are not eliminating stuff aggressively enough. Smarter Everyday did a tour of the ULA facilities [0], which is also worth checking out if you like this kind of thing. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23372690,"author":"725686","month":"2020-05","story":"Crew Dragon Docks with ISS","stance":"rec","why":"ULA factory tour showing friction stir welding on Atlas","quote":"Smarter Every Day has a very interesting video[1] inside the factory of United Launch Alliance","text":"Smarter Every Day has a very interesting video[1] inside the factory of United Launch Alliance in which, among a lot of other things, shows the friction stir welding used in their Atlas Rocket[2]: [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35283433,"author":"enragedcacti","month":"2023-03","story":"Relativity Space launches first 3D-printed rocket in historic test","stance":"reference","why":"Shows large CNC-milled rocket parts with tool markings","quote":"Many of the huge parts in this SmarterEveryDay video at ULA have tool markings e.g. at 8:40.","text":"I'm far from an expert but it seems like at least some companies use cnc milling very extensively which I can't imagine is all that fast either. Many of the huge parts in this SmarterEveryDay video at ULA have tool markings e.g. at 8:40. I'm also skeptical of 3d printing being as big of a boon as they pitch but I also wouldn't be super surprised if it nets an improvement in speed for some parts. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23751992,"author":"rtkwe","month":"2020-07","story":"1:60 scale Boeing 777 made from manila folders","stance":"reference","why":"Shows ULA rocket skin providing structure","quote":"Destin from Smarter Every Day did a tour of the ULA factory where you can see the skin that provides a lot of the structure for their rockets.","text":"Doing a model rocket would be tougher there's a lot of bits they just couldn't show him due to ITAR restrictions. Also beyond the engines rockets are a LOT of empty space, there is some interesting structure in the skin but most of the volume is just empty. Destin from Smarter Every Day did a tour of the ULA factory where you can see the skin that provides a lot of the structure for their rockets. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22454202,"author":"gpm","month":"2020-03","story":"SpaceX’s Starship SN1 prototype blows up during pressure test on its Texas pad","stance":"reference","why":"ULA uses resistance welding for stainless upper stages","quote":"ULA stainless steel (<1mm thickness) upper stages are resistance welded instead","text":"> Ideally you would use a turntable to rotate the cylinder while welding the sections together with a sub-arc machine. [LINK] Uh, at least in the video that steel is pretty thick. Starships skin is slightly less than 4mm thick... while in that video they have a 2cm deep groove, would the same technique actually work? For what it's worth, ULA stainless steel (<1mm thickness) upper stages are resistance welded instead [VIDEO] That said, I think a lot of the decisions right now are based on \"what can we quickly experiment with\" not \"what is a good long term plan\". Building a 9m turntable (diameter of the rocket) would take a long time."}],"r":[["AD5aAd8Oy84",1],["mScpHTIi-kM",1],["3VZSJKbzyMc",1],["RUwS1uAdUcI",1],["uUmIdq0D6-A",1],["1ffBJ4sVUb4",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["HrUy1Vn2KdI",1],["5d6SEQQbwtU",1]]},"re96UgMk6GQ":{"wy":"Recommended as a funny, accessible history of Haskell that is valuable even for people interested in language design in general.","q":{"x":"It's a funny talk and it's worth watching in full","i":21172415,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21172415,"author":"modernerd","month":"2019-10","story":"Learning Haskell is no harder than learning any other programming language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny history of why monads came to Haskell, with accessible examples","quote":"It's a funny talk and it's worth watching in full","text":"Yes, I didn't include the papers below in my parent response because, “just read these dense academic papers to understand what monads are and why Haskell has them” doesn't tend to be good advice for Haskell beginners. But learning why monads came to be included in Haskell is useful if you want to dive deeper into this stuff: Philip Wadler's original paper on Comprehending Monads [PDF]: [LINK] Philip Wadler and Simon Peyton-Jones' paper on Imperative Functional Programming [PDF]: [LINK] Simon Peyton-Jones mentions both papers and talks about why monads came to be part of Haskell in his talk on the history of Haskell here, with examples that are a little more accessible than the above papers: [VIDEO] [introduction to purity and then monads starts around 30:07] It's a funny talk and it's worth watching in full, but here's a nice soundbite: “So what did we do for IO? Well, we just didn't have any. [audience laughs] So the joy of being an academic, right, is you can design a language — in 1990, I beg to inform you — that had no input/output. A Haskell program was simply a function from string to string. That was what it was. … But this was a bit embarrassing…” He goes on to talk about other ideas they explored to create effects, why they settled on monads, and why he wishes now they had called them something like “workflows” (as F# later did[1]) to make them sound less intimidating. (Simon and Philip will both be at Haskell Exchange 2019 in London this coming week if anyone else,"},{"id":41162510,"author":"ddellacosta","month":"2024-08","story":"Why does everyone hate Haskell, jazz, and pure math?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Haskell team cares about industry users, not just ivory tower","quote":"it's worth watching a bit of this talk","text":"Also, it's worth watching a bit of this talk (link at a particularly relevant time to start) because while Haskell is an \"ivory-tower language,\" it is not _just_ an ivory tower language, and the people working on it are very much not just \"a merry bunch of category theorists who had little interest in software engineering.\" [VIDEO] This talk is seven years old btw. Caring about industry users in Haskell is not new."},{"id":17907511,"author":"Tarean","month":"2018-09","story":"Why I never finish my Haskell programs","stance":"rec","why":"Peyton Jones jokes about rich languages being complex and poorly documented","quote":"From a great talk by Simon Peyton Jones","text":"> I'm from Microsoft and when you see Microsoft documentation it often says, you know, x y or z is a rich something, right. In this case Haskell - or ghc's version of haskell is a rich language. What does this mean? Sounds good, doesn't it? But it always means this, right. That it's a large, complex, poorly understood, ill documented thing that nobody understands completely. From a great talk by Simon Peyton Jones [VIDEO]"},{"id":16781167,"author":"superice","month":"2018-04","story":"Are unsound type systems wrong?","stance":"rec","why":"Haskell's approach to evolving a sound type system","quote":"The whole video is very interesting even if you know nothing about Haskell, but are just interested in language design in general.","text":"Unsound typesystems are not necessarily a problem, but consider the problem the other way around: given a sound typesystem, how do we improve this in such a way that a) the average Joe will understand it b) it decreases the overhead of writing the types c) the type system helps the programmer write better programs Haskells approach to this keeps the constraint of soundness: [VIDEO] The whole video is very interesting even if you know nothing about Haskell, but are just interested in language design in general. To summarize the part of the video I just linked: Their approach so far has been to try and design the type system in such a way that you reduce programmer pain while not sacrificing soundness at all. It is a painful constraint for language designers, but it allows you to gradually add functionality to the type system from observing real world examples. The goal can be described as: Design a typesystem such that every working program can be typed in both a sound and correct way, while minimizing the amount of programs that can be typed, but are not working as intended."},{"id":14552255,"author":"arianvanp","month":"2017-06","story":"Rust as a gateway drug to Haskell","stance":"rec","why":"Easy-to-follow history of why laziness led to monads in Haskell","quote":"It is very easy to follow and a fun insight into the academic history of modern functional languages","text":"SPJ also noted that the original success of haskell was due to their pursuit of laziness. Because laziness has no place in a system with side effects, their pursuit of laziness and functional programming is what led them to discover the usefulness of monads for describing it in the first place. If they were pursuing a strict language instead, then they were allowed to take shortcuts, because sneaking in side effects is a lot easier. YEs maybe the next haskell will be strict, but getting to the point to make that decision required laziness. SPJ made a great lecture about this. It is very easy to follow and a fun insight into the academic history of modern functional languages [VIDEO]"},{"id":29402047,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2021-12","story":"Julia 1.7 Highlights","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's because Haskell is like the programming language equivalent of The Gig That Changed The World[0], although I guess Algol60 also has a strong claim to that title. Let me explain by quoting these paragraphs Roger Ebert's review[1] of 24h party people: > As the film opens, Wilson is attending the first, legendary Sex Pistols concert in Manchester, England. (...) Wilson is transfixed by the Pistols as they sing \"Anarchy in the U.K.\" and sneer at British tradition. He tells the camera that everyone in the audience will leave the room transformed and inspired, and then the camera pans to show a total of 42 people, two or three of them half-heartedly dancing in the aisles. Sounds like the average language designer entranced and inspired by their first time grokking Haskell. > Wilson features the Pistols and other bands on his Manchester TV show. Because of a ban by London TV, his show becomes the only venue for punk rock. Turns out he was right about the Pistols. They let loose something that changed rock music. And they did it in the only way that Wilson could respect, by thoroughly screwing up everything they did, and ending in bankruptcy and failure, followed by Sid Vicious' spectacular murder-suicide flameout. The Sex Pistols became successful because they failed; if they had succeeded, they would have sold out, or become diluted or commercial. I saw Johnny Rotten a few years ago at Sundance, still failing, and it made me feel proud of him. I could rephrase that last sent"},{"id":15040500,"author":"zengid","month":"2017-08","story":"John Carmack on Functional Programming (2013) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Laziness led to discovery of monad-based IO","quote":"","text":"The thing that's interesting about Haskell is that lazyness had a payoff of leading to the discovery of monad-based IO [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15082991,"author":"Ace17","month":"2017-08","story":"D as a Better C","stance":"reference","why":"Peyton-Jones on early success freezing a language's definition","quote":"What happens if [a language] become successful too quickly? You can't change anything!","text":"That's not so simple, because large adoption at a too early stage causes the language definition to freeze before it reaches maturity. Simon Peyton-Jones from the Haskell community explaining exactly that: \"What happens if [a language] become successful too quickly? You can't change anything!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":14437659,"author":"pron","month":"2017-05","story":"Principles of TLA+","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> An example is not being able to prove the termination of executing arbitrary programs The theorem states that you cannot prove a program's termination any faster than running it. You're not violating any theorems. > So, Im wanting it peer reviewed. But it has been peer reviewed. Here is the publication [LINK] in this journal [LINK] > So, such methods should allow us to achieve the same amount of complexity going upward using same abstraction and shortcut techniques. First, I think you're misinterpreting (and exaggerating) what has actually been achieved. Looking at Hyper-V[1], 30KLOC of (preexisting and tested) C code have been verified in a year (out of a total of just 100KLOC), but only function-by-function, rather than global correctness properties. They wrote some global invariants, but didn't verify them. As I understand, such similar verification of various layers has been done, but no global end-to-end work. Whether low-level or high-level, there has been no composition across abstraction layers. Second, such methods would not allow us to go \"upward\", because there's a reason why people concentrate on the lower level. The state space is smaller there. Finally, I think you're also misinterpreting DeepSpec's work. It is at the forefront of verification of a certain kind, but if you look at their papers, when they talk about end-to-end verification, they're discussing programs that are 3KLOC C. These programs are smaller than many JavaScript libraries. Also, Coq is not "}],"r":[["UF8uR6Z6KLc",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["72y2EC5fkcE",1],["0ifChJ0nJfM",1],["sPhpelUfu8Q",1],["-k3mVnRlQLU",1],["bAxRYrpbnuA",1],["HnOix9TFy1A",1],["pXOK-ZVJMaU",1]]},"LRhbcDzbGSU":{"wy":"HN recommends it to anyone interested in text adventures, Infocom, MUDs and early computer gaming history.","q":{"x":"Recommend watching “Get lamp” documentary to fans of MUDs, text adventures","i":19393534,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":46473235,"author":"anonymousiam","month":"2026-01","story":"Adventure 751 (1980)","stance":"rec","why":"Great documentary by Jason Scott about Adventure.","quote":"Jason Scott (of textfiles.com) made a great documentary video","text":"Jason Scott (of textfiles.com) made a great documentary video about the Adeventure game. I picked up a copy at DEFCON about 15 years ago. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31742079,"author":"petercooper","month":"2022-06","story":"Enjoyed Jason Scott’s BBS documentary","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary about text adventure games that the commenter enjoyed.","quote":"documentary about text adventure games called \"GET LAMP\" which I enjoyed a lot","text":"He also did a documentary about text adventure games called \"GET LAMP\" which I enjoyed a lot: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19393534,"author":"freediver","month":"2019-03","story":"Iconoclast: a Gothic/Cyberpunk MUD RPG, Online Since 1996","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary for fans of MUDs and text adventures.","quote":"Recommend watching “Get lamp” documentary to fans of MUDs, text adventures","text":"Oh the MUDs! Props to Forest’s edge and TempusMUD. Text is the ultimate medium because it directly connects with our imagination. I remember jumping out of my chair after reading a line of text on my screen. Recommend watching “Get lamp” documentary to fans of MUDs, text adventures and those born after: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18639744,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2018-12","story":"Great Adventure-Game Puzzles","stance":"rec","why":"History of text adventures with interviews of Infocom developers.","quote":"If you like text adventure games, check out Jason Scott's documentary GET LAMP","text":"If you like text adventure games, check out Jason Scott's documentary GET LAMP about the history of text adventure games, including interviews with some Infocom developers: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":8678804,"author":"grimgrin","month":"2014-12","story":"First Infocom Z-machine implemented in hardware","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary featuring many Infocom employees.","quote":"Recommended if text games are of interest to you.","text":"Many Infocom employees are present in the documentary Get Lamp: [VIDEO] Recommended if text games are of interest to you."},{"id":22996444,"author":"Eremotherium","month":"2020-04","story":"textfiles.com","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among Jason Scott's works.","quote":"","text":"He also gave us the brilliant BBS: The Documentary [LINK] GET LAMP: The Text Adventure Documentary (shot in 1080p) [VIDEO] and (apart from his many talks) his podcast \"Jason Scott talks his way out of it\" which is just gem after gem of documented tech ephemera. [LINK] It's baffingly hard to quantify how many people he (and all the other great people at the Internet Archive) has affected with his bullheaded conservation efforts."},{"id":14067533,"author":"mgkimsal","month":"2017-04","story":"A History of Interactive Fiction (2006)","stance":"reference","why":"Screening of the Get Lamp documentary at a Google event.","quote":"there's a few versions of \"get lamp\" documentary online","text":"Related: there's a few versions of \"get lamp\" documentary online ( [VIDEO] ) or you can get the movie directly from getlamp.com. These histories (the maher link above, the getlamp doc, etc) bring back a lot of great memories. EDIT: the youtube link above looks like it's actually the getlamp movie being shown at a google event, with an audience (audience sounds in there now and then)."},{"id":10640527,"author":"jamesk_au","month":"2015-11","story":"Get Lamp","stance":"reference","why":"Non-interactive version of the documentary with maker commentary.","quote":"You can watch the non-interactive version of the documentary with some commentary by its maker","text":"You can watch the non-interactive version of the documentary with some commentary by its maker in this Google Tech Talk: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["N8elxpSu9pw",1],["y71lli8MS8s",1],["63vqob-MljQ",1],["eRkgK4jfi6M",1],["HQYsFshbkYw",1],["gPlpphT7n9s",1],["Myq_Hc_39aI",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1],["zU7Lww-sBPg",1],["h6fcK_fRYaI",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["coFIEH3vXPw",1],["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["stxVBJem3Rs",1],["m64SWl9bfvk",1],["wICOlaQOpM0",1],["ELeZAKCN4tY",1],["lfo8XHGFAIQ",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1]]},"TlLIOgWYVpI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a fun, informative survey of easy-to-implement dungeon generation methods from the author of the Rust roguelike tutorial.","q":{"x":"My favorite from that collection on map generation is by Herbert Wolverson","i":29814690,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29814690,"author":"kruffin","month":"2022-01","story":"Simple Dungeon Map Generation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Walks through 5 or 6 easy-to-implement map generation techniques","quote":"My favorite from that collection on map generation is by Herbert Wolverson","text":"My favorite from that collection on map generation is by Herbert Wolverson: [VIDEO] He goes over 5 or 6 different techniques that are easy to implement."},{"id":33300618,"author":"arandomhuman","month":"2022-10","story":"A celebration of roguelike games","stance":"rec","why":"Fun, informative procedural generation talk from a community developer","quote":"I recently saw a really fun and informative talk on procedural generation","text":"I recently saw a really fun and informative talk on procedural generation from their YouTube channel by a well known developer in the community [VIDEO] . There's tons of interesting talks there if (traditional) roguelikes or their development really pique your interest."},{"id":32068955,"author":"chungus","month":"2022-07","story":"Roguelike Tutorial in Rust (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk on procedural map generation by the tutorial author","quote":"has done an interesting talk[0] on procedural map generation","text":"This is what I used to learn Rust, in tandem with The Book. I would read a chapter of The Book, then do a chapter of Herbert's Roguelike Tutorial. Very well written and fun material, that guides you deep into a tricky space with very little previous knowledge required. The maintainer is also very responsive to questions and pull requests. And has done an interesting talk[0] on procedural map generation at the annual 'Roguelike Celebration' conference[1] which is happening in October. (and for which the Call For Papers is still open) [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":28867202,"author":"rudedogg","month":"2021-10","story":"Hands-On Rust: Effective Learning Through 2D Game Development and Play","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on procedural generation by the Rust book author","quote":"I watched one of your talks about procedural generation[0] and really enjoyed it, thanks!","text":"Congratulations on releasing your book. I watched one of your talks about procedural generation[0] and really enjoyed it, thanks! Mentioning this here since the tutorial linked above has a lot of procedural generation content [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AqDdWEiSwMM",1],["3LVeEjsn8Ts",1]]},"Vjm8P8utT5g":{"wy":"HN points to it as the standard demonstration of arbitrary code execution on a Game Boy, and of glitches and bugs being turned into a working computer.","q":{"x":"One of the crazier uses of ACE in Gameboy Pokemon games exploits","i":32635760,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32635760,"author":"stepupmakeup","month":"2022-08","story":"Tetsuji: Remote Code Execution on a GameBoy Colour 22 Years Later","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Genuine, unedited Pokemon Yellow exploit, replicable on an emulator.","quote":"One of the crazier uses of ACE in Gameboy Pokemon games exploits","text":"One of the crazier uses of ACE in Gameboy Pokemon games exploits is this \"speedrun\", by MrWint: [VIDEO] ([TAS] Pokémon Yellow - Arbitrary Code Execution) I've timestamped the part where the actual exploit begins, after all of thesetup. It's worth mentioning that this isn't faked or edited in any way, and it's possible to download the input data and replicate on a emulator yourself."},{"id":45423374,"author":"alex-moon","month":"2025-09","story":"Bad Apple but it's played inside Super Mario Bros","stance":"rec","why":"Pokemon Yellow exploit runs several different games.","quote":"There is an amazing video of a similar exploit in Pokémon Yellow","text":"There is an amazing video of a similar exploit in Pokémon Yellow where they basically run a bunch of different games: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40287980,"author":"Dwedit","month":"2024-05","story":"Hackers discover how to reprogram NES Tetris from within the game","stance":"rec","why":"Something beautiful running on the console after the exploit.","quote":"you can create something really beautiful for the console to run afterwards. Pokemon Yellow","text":"Sometimes even after you've come up with the exploit, you can create something really beautiful for the console to run afterwards. Pokemon Yellow: [VIDEO] Super Mario World: [LINK]"},{"id":30216929,"author":"alexb_","month":"2022-02","story":"Accidentally Turing-Complete","stance":"rec","why":"Shows bugs hacked into alternate Game Boy instructions.","quote":"Good example: (skip to 1:20 for craziness)","text":"It mentions Pokemon Yellow, but really any code run isn't really \"run in the game\" more so that it uses bugs to hack together alternate instructions for the game boy to execute. Good example: [VIDEO] (skip to 1:20 for craziness)"},{"id":31962393,"author":"schroeding","month":"2022-07","story":"Games Done Quick “Ocarina of Time” beta showcase","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Arbitrary Code Execution TAS like Triforce% or this Pokemon Yellow one[1] are more or less demos","text":"Arbitrary Code Execution TAS like Triforce% or this Pokemon Yellow one[1] are more or less demos that target both the console and the \"host game\". I would say even some non-ACE ones like this Brainage TAS[2] are demos :D [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":24616562,"author":"DougBTX","month":"2020-09","story":"Show HN: A SQL database implemented purely in TypeScript type annotations","stance":"reference","why":"Tetris injected into Pokemon Yellow via runtime code injection.","quote":"Tetris implemented in Pokémon Yellow via runtime code injection","text":"At the end of the day, if you look at a low enough level in the stack, there are no types. In a very concrete way, types are a high-level idea, and if the high-level stops matching the low-level, then bugs crop up. See for example Tetris implemented in Pokémon Yellow via runtime code injection: [VIDEO] Their compiler missed that! So I think there's good reason to switch from talking about types as \"assumptions\", and think of them as compile time \"assertions\". Something lower-level in the stack could break the high-level code, but that doesn't make the high-level code useless, just imperfect."}],"r":[["hB6eY73sLV0",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1]]},"06e-PwhmSq8":{"wy":"HN people recommend it for its interesting, if contested, theory of cancer's root cause, noting the content matters more than the delivery.","q":{"x":"This video [1] is worth a watch to learn a bit more about cancer as a metabolic disease.","i":29578605,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":33601342,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-11","story":"Nicotinamide riboside uptake linked to metastasis and NAD+ metabolism","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on cancer as metabolic disease.","quote":"This doctor is not a great speaker but it is the content that matters.","text":"As does glucose and L-Glutamine. Cancer cells literally live off of glucose, specific amino acids and fermentable fuels. What is the FDA's stance on banning sugar? I would prefer we focus on eliminating molecules from the diet that feed cancer cells and damage mitochondria. I believe we should also cycle up/down molecules that trigger an up-regulation of NRF2/AMPK to literally digest the senescent and especially the damaged cells prior to them becoming cancerous. I am not really a fan of NR/NMN anyway. Both are more expensive than NAM and my body already converts NAM to NR/NMN, it's just a lot slower than superloading NMN directly, not to mention NMN alone will only activate the Sirt genes but other nutrients are required for repair. Excess NMN is converted back to NAM for storage and when there is too much we just urinate it out. But I am no biologist and the folks pushing these supplements will not respond to my inquiries. On a related note take some time to watch a video on Cancer as a metabolic disease. [1] This doctor is not a great speaker but it is the content that matters. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":29578605,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-12","story":"No limit to maximal lifespan in humans: how to beat a 122-year-old record","stance":"rec","why":"Explains cancer as a metabolic disease.","quote":"This video [1] is worth a watch to learn a bit more about cancer as a metabolic disease.","text":"There are some other compensating controls that can mitigate cancer but it takes some willpower which I suppose is an entirely different topic all together. This video [1] is worth a watch to learn a bit more about cancer as a metabolic disease. [1] - [VIDEO] [video]"},{"id":29667313,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-12","story":"The roots of the National Cancer Act","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting theories on root cause of cancer.","quote":"Compelling theories anyway.","text":"I find this video [1] interesting as it pertains to the root cause of cancer. Compelling theories anyway. [1] - [VIDEO] [video]"},{"id":28658087,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: Blogs/YouTube Channels on Cancer?","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on cancer as metabolic disease, in a list of resources.","quote":"It's just interesting content on your topic.","text":"Sorry to hear your are going through this. Here are a few I have found interesting. I am not a doctor and by no means is any of this something I am suggesting. It's just interesting content on your topic. - Prof. Thomas Seyfried - 'Cancer as a Metabolic Disease: Implications for Novel Therapies' [VIDEO] - A New Cancer Strategy [LINK] - The MOST Important Cancer Prevention [LINK] - Antiangiogenic Therapy [LINK] - Sulforaphane and Its Effects on Cancer, Mortality, Aging, Brain and Behavior, Heart Disease & More [LINK] - Q&A with Dr. Jed Fahey on Sulforaphane, Moringa and Chemoprotection [An authoritative discussion!] [LINK] - [LINK] Also watch this site if your doctors run out of ideas. Look for newer and more targeted methods. [LINK] Some people that were written off by doctors had some success with prolonged fasting. Also important would be to get opinions by many doctors inside and outside of your medial group."},{"id":25791839,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-01","story":"Why cancer cells waste so much energy","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on cancer as metabolic disease.","quote":"I think their research is very promising.","text":"Here is a video on \"Cancer as a Metabolic Disease: Implications for Novel Therapies\" [1] by Prof. Thomas Seyfried that may be related. I think their research is very promising. [1] - [VIDEO] [video]"},{"id":37141401,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2023-08","story":"M. Levin – Bioelectrical signals reveal, induce, and normalize cancer [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Asks how it relates to Levin's work.","quote":"","text":"How far along has this technology and science evolved? Have we repaired birth defects in humans yet? Can we use this to make an adult human repair itself in ways otherwise thought not possible yet or perhaps very soon? How many people are working on this? The reason I ask is that despite the video having just been uploaded I recall watching this some time ago. I may have even saved it. How far are we from cracking the bioelectrical code ? Is there a website that is tracking the progress and accepting donations? I would also like to see Michael Levin's views on the work by Prof. Thomas Seyfried on cancer as a metabolic disease [1]. Specifically does Thomas's work reference the mechanical structures that Michael is talking about as an augmentation rather than conflicting concepts? [1] - [VIDEO] [video][44 mins]"},{"id":34818382,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2023-02","story":"Arginine – The Nutrient That Cancer Cells Crave","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for cancer cells' fuel use.","quote":"","text":"My understanding is that cancer cells short circuit the krebs cycle [1] and survive off fermented fuels like glucose and most of the amino acids, l-glutamine being the most prevalent and available. Most of the population have an over-abundance of glucose. [1] - [VIDEO] [video][unpopular opinion]"},{"id":31225994,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-05","story":"Ancient Genes for Symbiosis Hint at Mitochondria’s Origins","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in passing on mitochondrial damage.","quote":"","text":"I am not an expert in this area but I believe it may be related to the primitive mechanism the cells are capable of activating and reverting to when the mitochondria are damaged. [1] [1] - [VIDEO] [video]"},{"id":29508762,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-12","story":"More than half of high-impact cancer lab studies could not be replicated","stance":"reference","why":"Asks experts about its claim cancer is metabolic.","quote":"","text":"Speaking of reproducible science, I am really curious what cancer experts take is on this video [1] and their thoughts on cancer not being a somatic genetic disorder but rather that being a downstream effect of a mitochondrial metabolic disorder. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":27193970,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-05","story":"Share of Population with Cancer by Country","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for cancer as metabolic disease.","quote":"","text":"Another possibility could be what people consume and how much of it, if we start to accept that cancer may be a metabolic disease. [1] [1] - [VIDEO] [video]"},{"id":25427050,"author":"PIKAL","month":"2020-12","story":"A newly discovered body part changes our understanding of the brain (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Related to metabolic view of disease.","quote":"","text":"“ meaning we’ve been wrong about some basic ways in which the brain and immune system are connected.” I’ve been screaming this at my doctors for years. For those who don’t want to read the article, the quote sums it up. They discovered new lymphatic vessels in the brain in 2016 where previously the brain wasn’t thought to have any. 2 years ago I had a stressful life event. I became schizophrenic because of it. I accidentally discovered that ketosis cures the schizophrenia. It turns out a doctor at Harvard has been sounding the alarm about this and nobody is listening to him [1]. After researching these things somewhat deeply, it appears that almost all diseases that involve the death or inactivity of neurons (Alzheimer’s, schizophrenia, autism, etc) are sometimes caused by “inflammatory signals,” as I call them, which interfere with the metabolism of glucose. Note the way I worded that — there’s more than one way for those neurons to become deactivated. What we currently think of as being a single disease might be many diseases, many different types of pathology that ultimately lead to the silencing of the same general population of neurons and thereby causing the same symptoms. The point is that the metabolic angle doesn’t explain all neural pathology, so keep that in mind when ketosis is found to only put 20% of schizophrenics into remission in the next five years. There is currently exactly one study looking at the effect of ketosis on schizophrenia, in Finland, and it kee"},{"id":24591365,"author":"vwat","month":"2020-09","story":"As Their Numbers Grow, Covid-19 “Long Haulers” Stump Experts","stance":"reference","why":"Related to metabolic disease discussion.","quote":"","text":"The idea that people are imagining this, as other commenters have put forward, is nonsense. The idea that people are just getting old and drink too much soda isn’t right either although it’s close. The reason why people report similar or overlapping symptoms is because they all have a problem with their immune systems. Overactive immune systems tend to cause a certain set of symptoms. The immune system is very complicated, very broad in its jurisdiction and very poorly understood. It is scientifically proven that certain chemicals (common food additives for example) cause no immune (allergic) response at all whatsoever, but cause an immune response to other chemicals. So the immune system is not just complex as a monolith but also has inputs that interact with one another, leading to a combinatoric level of complexity. When you also consider that immune function and mediation (as seen with some food additives, for example) is observed in emotional stress — something that is as close to being unquantifiable as anything could be — one can see that it might be the case that this is a disease that has evaded medical science until now. Chronic fatigue syndrome was observed in gulf war vets and was so common among them that it was coined “gulf war syndrome.” One researcher has hypothesized that this is due to an immune response which takes the form of cell deactivation via ATP signaling which he has named “cell danger response.” Meanwhile, it is widely appreciated that autism is no"}],"r":[["zz4YVJ4aRfg",1],["-f-CFQxaUY4",1],["sWN13pKVp9s",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["9nXop2lLDa4",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["aDejwCGdUV8",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["xc8epam4NyY",1],["G0Cp7DrvNLQ",1]]},"URJ_qSXruW0":{"wy":"HN considers it the go-to link for conveying the chaos of Space Station 13 in any related discussion.","q":{"x":"No SS13 discussion is complete without linking to SsethTzeentach's infamous absurd-sounding review","i":29109264,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":35510626,"author":"Aromasin","month":"2023-04","story":"Ever-expanding animation of the life of the 796th floor of a space station","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Conveys the chaos of Space Station 13 better than the commenter could","quote":"This YouTuber really expresses it better than I ever could","text":"Reminds me of a game I used to play called Space Station 13. It's a completely unique game. Imagine a giant game of Among Us, but with the engine complexity of Dwarf Fortress. Most servers are high level roleplay, where each player has a role (doctor, janitor, captain, clown, etcetera) and the rounds play out until chaos ensues (theme changes each round, but it could anything from station terrorists to a wizard causing bedlam). This YouTuber really expresses it better than I ever could: [VIDEO] Everyone has their goals to achieve each round, but it's general havoc. The OP animation really channels the feeling of playing the game."},{"id":29109264,"author":"ajvs","month":"2021-11","story":"Space Station 14 – Open-source remake of Space Station 13","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Infamous, absurd-sounding SS13 review","quote":"No SS13 discussion is complete without linking to SsethTzeentach's infamous absurd-sounding review","text":"No SS13 discussion is complete without linking to SsethTzeentach's infamous absurd-sounding review[1] or MandaloreGaming's slightly less infamous but more informational review[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":35519370,"author":"LoganDark","month":"2023-04","story":"Ever-expanding animation of the life of the 796th floor of a space station","stance":"rec","why":"Absurd, vivid description of Space Station 13 chaos","quote":"Welcome to Space Station 13. Just wow.","text":"> This YouTuber really expresses it better than I ever could: [VIDEO] From the video: > You can try taking a painkiller instead, but, it wasn't a painkiller, it was LSD. Having a bad trip? Don't worry, there's a security officer nearby to help! But he can't respond because he was murdered and replaced by a genetically modified monkey wearing his uniform. Hallucinating? Keep calm and focus on what's real. Unfortunately for you, the supermassive black hole expanding towards you is not a hallucination. It is, in fact, very real. _ {THE EMERGENCY SHUTTLE HAS BEEN CALLED}_ Welcome to Space Station 13. Just wow."},{"id":25712669,"author":"ajvs","month":"2021-01","story":"Games on GitHub – A list of popular/awesome video games hosted on GitHub","stance":"rec","why":"Popular review sharing stories of crazy events in SS13","quote":"Here's a very popular review which shares stories of some of the crazy events that can happen","text":"I'm always surprised by lists like these that fail to mention Space Station 13, the most actively developed 10+ year old FOSS game on GitHub. There are numerous forks of it, with tgstation being the most popular one [1]. SS13 is multiplayer round-based role playing game, where players assume the role of a crewmember on a space station. Together they must keep the station running smoothly, whilst dealing with antagonistic forces who threaten to sabotage the mission. Since most stations have dozens of job roles, each with content enough to last you hundreds of hours it's no surprise why people such as myself have spent thousands of hours on it and thousands play this every day. It has systems as varied as atmospherics, fully destructible environments, surgery, genetics, science, engineering, etc etc and the amount of content and interaction possible means that no two rounds are the same. Here's a very popular review which shares stories of some of the crazy events that can happen [2]. Unfortunately it's all built on the proprietary engine BYOND. However there are fully libre successors on new FOSS engines which are in-progress to match the massive amount of content SS13 has already accumulated, such as SS14 [3] and Unitystation [4]. I'm not a hardcore FOSS enthusiast about games so I prefer the amount of content SS13 has for the time being, but in time I intend to play and contribute to one of the successors instead in the future. They both have active servers if you're interes"}],"r":[["3VZSJKbzyMc",1],["15R2pMqU2ok",1]]},"ibqiet6Bg38":{"wy":"Praised as a remarkable piece of social engineering, spoofed caller ID plus pretexting, that produced direct evidence of the poisoning.","q":{"x":"But the best part is how he did it: caller ID spoof + social engineering.","i":25868124,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28191993,"author":"sam_lowry_","month":"2021-08","story":"The best books on assassinations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Navalny pranks his would-be killer into revealing details of the attempt.","quote":"Navalny calling his killer and pranking him into telling all kinds of nasty details of Navalny's own killing attempt is mind-bogging","text":"This is a bit off-topic, but Navalny calling his killer and pranking him into telling all kinds of nasty details of Navalny's own killing attempt is mind-bogging [1]. I am sure this is now part of all secrets services of the world 101 trainings. Full audio with English transcript: [2]. There is also a bit of background told by Navalny later on, e.g. the name Maxim Sergeevich Ustinov was not really random. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] P.S. fivebooks.com seems to be spamming HN quite regularly."},{"id":25566455,"author":"0x38B","month":"2020-12","story":"Russia gives Kremlin critic Navalny an ultimatum: Return immediately or jail","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Navalny pranks an FSB agent into confessing details of his poisoning; English subs.","quote":"the video of Navalny calling one of the FSB agents who poisoned him is definitely the best prank of 2020","text":"Related: the video of Navalny calling one of the FSB agents who poisoned him is definitely the best prank of 2020 [1]. (Video has English subs) Watching Navalny grill Konstantin Kudryavtsev about why they failed to kill Navalny (flight quickly grounded, quick medical aid on the ground) was unreal and the best spy moment of 2020 for me. One thing that's been making a difference is Navalny's Anti-Corruption Foundation's \"Smart Voting\" system, which helps people vote out \"United Russia\" members and bring in opposition or at least non-United-Russia candidates [2]. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":25868124,"author":"wyldfire","month":"2021-01","story":"Novichok Nerve Agent Poisoning","stance":"rec","why":"Phone call using spoofed caller ID extracts evidence of poisoning cover-up.","quote":"But the best part is how he did it: caller ID spoof + social engineering.","text":"In case you missed it: Navalny found conclusive evidence of a cover-up for his poisoning [1] [2] [3]. But the best part is how he did it: caller ID spoof + social engineering. IMO exploiting the authoritarian nature/culture was really clever. Every time Kudryavtsev demurs about revealing more details, Navalny (as Ustinov) calls him by his full name. To me, it sounds like he's threatening him or reminding him of their relative position in the hierarchy. Each time, Kudryavtsev continues to spill the beans and give up co-conspirators. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":26925160,"author":"awb","month":"2021-04","story":"Dutch MPs had a call with deep fake imitation of Navalny's Chief of Staff","stance":"reference","why":"The actual call where Navalny impersonated an official to uncover his poisoning.","quote":"And here’s the call itself","text":"> And with old-fashioned telephone presumably has had this impostor problem since forever. Nalvany himself impersonated a Russian official via telephone to uncover details of his own assassination attempt: [LINK] And here’s the call itself: [VIDEO] It’s an incredible story and yes it does still happen."},{"id":26614890,"author":"reducesuffering","month":"2021-03","story":"Ask HN: What was the biggest contributor to your happiness in the past year?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence that an FSB agent admitted the poisoning.","quote":"I don't know how you can dispute them literally catching the FSB agent admitting it","text":"I'm fully aware of Navalny's history, all politicians are flawed in some ways, and Navalny is even moreso. Do you think that assassination is acceptable for that? The evidence for the poisonings is ironclad by any world measures except RT. Remember, this is about the Russian government's abhorrent \"evil\" behavior. Your whataboutism is a weak defense. I don't know how you can dispute them literally catching the FSB agent admitting it: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lkgszkPnV8g",1],["gwvA49ZXnf8",1]]},"XOR8mk0tLpc":{"wy":"HN commenters post it as a favorite scene about IBM's reputation and sales psychology.","q":{"x":"one of my favorite scenes from Halt and Catch Fire","i":38142495,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":47056726,"author":"danielvaughn","month":"2026-02","story":"Halt and Catch Fire: TV's Best Drama You've Probably Never Heard Of (2021)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Showcases Lee Pace's charismatic marketing guru performance.","quote":"a great example and one of my favorite scenes from the show","text":"Lee Pace's performance in that show is one of my all time favorites. It's incredibly hard to play a charismatic marketing guru because in some sense, you're not acting. In a given scene, the character might be trying to convince people around him of some crazy idea, but if he hasn't convinced you, the viewer, then the entire illusion falls apart. So he really has to do in real life what he's pretending to do on screen. edit - a great example and one of my favorite scenes from the show: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38142495,"author":"danielvaughn","month":"2023-11","story":"When to Shut Up: A Visual Guide (With Included Algorithm)","stance":"rec","why":"Scene where a marketing guru tells an engineer when to shut up.","quote":"one of my favorite scenes from Halt and Catch Fire","text":"This reminds me of one of my favorite scenes from Halt and Catch Fire, when the marketing guru tells the sales engineer (in no uncertain terms) when to shut up: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18330501,"author":"j1vms","month":"2018-10","story":"IBM’s Old Playbook","stance":"rec","why":"Halt and Catch Fire pilot sales call scene against IBM.","quote":"Think you'll get a kick out of this scene from Halt and Catch Fire","text":"Think you'll get a kick out of this scene from Halt and Catch Fire: [VIDEO] [0] [0] Sales call scene with Joe and Gordon in Halt and Catch Fire: Season 1 Episode 1 [1] [LINK]"},{"id":41084550,"author":"udev4096","month":"2024-07","story":"The New Internet","stance":"reference","why":"Clip of the IBM safe-choice dialogue.","quote":"Here's the clip at 1.51 minutes, if anyone's interested","text":"> You know what, nobody ever got fired for buying AWS. > That’s an IBM analogy. Wow, this dialogue comes in the first episode of halt and catch fire. I didn't know this was a real thing Here's the clip at 1.51 minutes, if anyone's interested: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38961894,"author":"jyunwai","month":"2024-01","story":"Google lays off Bay Area workers, closes Mtn View child care center","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates the feeling behind the term Big Blue.","quote":"","text":"The term did come back a bit in 2014 with the great television series \"Halt and Catch Fire.\" The metonymy of \"Big Blue\" was regularly used in the series (especially in the pilot) to describe IBM as a huge entity that a forcefully-made new division of a smaller company was competing against, to develop a personal computer in the 1980s. This scene in the pilot about a salesman trying to convince another company's executives to choose his products over IBM's describes the feeling behind the term very well: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XRr1kaXKBsU",1]]},"iSVHp6CAyQ8":{"wy":"Frequently recommended as a good, approachable overview of ASML and semiconductor equipment.","q":{"x":"Good video on ASML and semiconductor fabrication equipment in general","i":32357218,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30881019,"author":"tux1968","month":"2022-04","story":"Why the World Relies on ASML for Machines That Print Chips [Video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how EUV 13nm light is created in ASML machines.","quote":"The entire video is pretty fascinating","text":"The entire video is pretty fascinating, but this direct link to the timestamp where they explain how the EUV 13nm wavelength light is created, is particularly incredible: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39453286,"author":"tim333","month":"2024-02","story":"Japan bets $67B to become a global chip powerhouse once again","stance":"rec","why":"An 18 minute CNBC piece on ASML.","quote":"an 18 min CNBC piece is quite jolly","text":"'Why The World Relies On ASML For Machines That Print Chips' an 18 min CNBC piece is quite jolly [VIDEO]"},{"id":32333211,"author":"photochemsyn","month":"2022-08","story":"Political Chips","stance":"rec","why":"CNBC tour of ASML clean rooms.","quote":"CNBC toured ASML clean rooms in a very interesting video.","text":"> \"The most important decision was shifting to extreme ultraviolet lithography at a time when Intel thought it was much too expensive and difficult to implement; TSMC, backed by Apple’s commitment to buy the best chips it could make, committed to EUV in 2014, and delivered the first EUV-derived chips in 2019 for the iPhone. Those EUV machines are made by one company — ASML. They’re worth more than Intel too (and Intel is a customer).\" CNBC toured ASML clean rooms in a very interesting video. IIRC, the only thing keeping ASML from exporting these machines to China is some EU/USA export control rule: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32357218,"author":"Cyph0n","month":"2022-08","story":"Italy, Intel close to $5B deal for chip factory","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of ASML and semiconductor fabrication equipment.","quote":"Good video on ASML and semiconductor fabrication equipment in general","text":"All of the above. You need thousands of highly skilled engineers to operate an advanced fab. You need extremely advanced and expensive specialized equipment - look up ASML for a general idea of what this equipment does. You need a huge, purpose-built cleanroom that is many orders of magnitude “cleaner” than an operating room. You need a structure (and location) that has virtually zero vibration or noise. You need an constant, high-volume supply of a bunch of resources including water, power, and sand. And the list goes on. Edit: Good video on ASML and semiconductor fabrication equipment in general: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uxykI30fS54",1],["UiS27feX8o0",1],["p3lsYlod5OU",1],["cQoQE_HDG8g",1],["IkdziSLYzHw",1],["7k1ehaE0bdU",1],["YMqgiXLjvRs",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["baFaEvBywGc",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1],["9NOncx2jU0Q",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1],["0fKBhvDjuy0",1],["qS4H6PEcCCA",1],["PfYYraMgiBA",1],["qhbuKbxJsk8",1]]},"u-x7PdnvCyI":{"wy":"Recommended as a rare, inspiring history of the foundry business and the relationship between Chang and Huang.","q":{"x":"here's a fascinating conversation from 5 years ago between Morris Chang (founder and chairman, TSMC) and Jen-Hsun Huang (CEO, Nvidia)","i":3746181,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":35641370,"author":"swyx","month":"2023-04","story":"Interview podcast with Jensen Huang founder/CEO of Nvidia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspiring conversation between two Taiwanese American semiconductor leaders","quote":"highly recommend his convo with Morris Chang over 15 years ago","text":"jensen is a hero of mine. excited to listen in! highly recommend his convo with Morris Chang over 15 years ago: [VIDEO] as a guy from another small country these two taiwainese american semiconductor titans give me a lot of inspo"},{"id":33312830,"author":"robocat","month":"2022-10","story":"Product Market Fit","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how hard it is to copy a business","quote":"Long interview, but I think it has moments where it reinforces the same concept","text":"Jen-Hsun interviews Morris Chang [VIDEO] Long interview, but I think it has moments where it reinforces the same concept that it is very difficult to copy a business, even if the CEO is directly telling you their secrets. Interesting interview - although exponentially above my pay grade."},{"id":3746181,"author":"ak217","month":"2012-03","story":"Nvidia deeply unhappy with TSMC, claims 22nm essentially worthless","stance":"rec","why":"Historical conversation between TSMC founder and Nvidia CEO","quote":"here's a fascinating conversation from 5 years ago between Morris Chang (founder and chairman, TSMC) and Jen-Hsun Huang (CEO, Nvidia)","text":"Very interesting. Those really are pretty stunning words for a business relationship as big as this. If I understand correctly, Nvidia has nowhere else to go since TSMC is so far ahead of other independent foundries (I'm not sure how independent GF really is - is it conceivable for Nvidia to work with GF?) and it can't very well go to Intel given the amount of competition they are engaged in. Maybe Samsung? Also, I'm not clear on the arguments presented in the slides. The transistor normalized price curves for 20nm and 14nm do eventually cross over the preceding curves, unlike the 40nm curve. And even putting the same transistor count on a smaller process node can result in lower power consumption once the leakage problems are mitigated, so the value is increased. Edit: For historical background, here's a fascinating conversation from 5 years ago between Morris Chang (founder and chairman, TSMC) and Jen-Hsun Huang (CEO, Nvidia): [VIDEO]"},{"id":35719665,"author":"Keyframe","month":"2023-04","story":"TSMC’s 3-Nm Push Faces Tool Struggles","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates Huang's close relationship with Morris Chang","quote":"Morris Chang is a hero of Jen-Hsun Huang","text":"It cuts deeper. Morris Chang is a hero of Jen-Hsun Huang and relationship goes way deeper than customer-vendor relationship. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wSdV1M7n4gQ",1],["ia7IKW0yuG0",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["plVk4NVIUh8",1],["Jv7sLrON7QY",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["Mjx3S3UjmnA",1],["KtQ9nt2ZeGM",1],["T6X2uwlQGQM",1],["ztspvPYybIY",1],["xsSnOQynTHs",1]]},"1Evwgu369Jw":{"wy":"Recommended as a quick, memorable explanation of how to support someone in pain without making it about yourself.","q":{"x":"the video was still useful for me. It's less than 3 minutes long.","i":24948552,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24948552,"author":"rpdillon","month":"2020-10","story":"How to deal with extreme physical pain","stance":"rec","why":"Under three minutes on connection versus problem-solving","quote":"the video was still useful for me. It's less than 3 minutes long.","text":"My employer had me go through training that had this video as part of the curriculum. Though I already understood the ideas, the video was still useful for me. It's less than 3 minutes long. [VIDEO] In short, sometimes people need connection to others; problem-solving mode isn't always the most useful path."},{"id":10976039,"author":"andrewtbham","month":"2016-01","story":"Is it the Wealth Gap that's bad or the Empathy Gap that comes with it?","stance":"rec","why":"Brown's research on shame and vulnerability, relevant to empathy","quote":"If you're interested in empathy, I suggest you watch this brenee brown video.","text":"I'm unsure how this anecdote proves anything about a correlation between empathy and wealth. Whether you are rich or poor, you can have empathy. If you're interested in empathy, I suggest you watch this brenee brown video. The video may seem anecdotal but she has done a lot of research about shame and vulnerability and... having read her books, money is not something that comes up in the research. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6976140,"author":"neilk","month":"2013-12","story":"Ask HN: What book changed your life in 2013?","stance":"rec","why":"Brown's research-based work improves how you support friends","quote":"her books have made me a better friend when my friends are in pain.","text":"Most of what I read is related to startups or economics, but for the short list of change-my-life books: This year I read a lot about what you might loosely call the Paleo movement / anti-carbs and sugar movement. The New Evolution Diet , The Primal Blueprint , books by Gary Taubes, and so on. I think this is going to change how I eat and exercise forever. The Gift of Imperfection and other works by Brené Brown. Lately this is being promoted by Oprah, which isn't usually a good sign. I think it stands apart because it's not theory or poetry; it's based on some solid research on what people living more productive and satisfying lives are actually doing. If nothing else, her books have made me a better friend when my friends are in pain. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18938428,"author":"snthd","month":"2019-01","story":"My dog was killed on a walk with a walker ordered through Wag","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates that support shouldn't turn into making it about yourself","quote":"someone supporting you shouldn't be making it about them","text":"The problem is not that the comparison is valid or invalid, but that someone supporting you shouldn't be making it about them . Brené Brown on Empathy - The RSA [VIDEO]"},{"id":8451510,"author":"justanotherta","month":"2014-10","story":"On Getting Wasted","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as illustration of empathy for sufferers of madness","quote":"","text":"Yes, there are certainly many different types of \"madness\". The \"layer of abstraction\" that the specific variety of madness imparts on the holder is their own reality. The hypothetical accepted objective true reality is irrelevant to them. While this true reality is the standard with which to measure the conduct of people's behavior, if you are to preserve the emotional well being of the suffer of madness it would be helpful to communicate and act in a way which is consistent with their own subjective reality. By delving into their madness you can understand their behavior and actions more than understanding it from an outside perspective. If the goal is to simply enforce external standards this empathy is a waste of resources. But if preserving their emotional well being is a concern empathy can be a useful tool to both understand their perspective and guide you in your interactions with the sufferer. [VIDEO] At the very least I think it would have been nice if the hoarder in this story was taken away to alternate accommodations for the duration of the clean up. Terrible that she had to witness the event."}],"r":[["AHeZHyOnsm4",1],["O_azyt1JhI0",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["l_F9jxsfGCw",1]]},"4HgShra-KnY":{"wy":"Recommended for explaining where the compiler ecosystem lacks standardization for accelerators and why efforts like MLIR and CIRCT matter.","q":{"x":"I watched Chris Lattner's ASPLOS talk and it finally clicked.","i":27017940,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27743756,"author":"mikeurbach","month":"2021-07","story":"Chisel/FIRRTL Hardware Compiler Framework","stance":"rec","why":"Lattner's talk on why CIRCT and unifying hardware IRs","quote":"a much better talk[2] Chris Lattner gave shortly thereafter","text":"I contribute to CIRCT, so I feel like I should chime in here. I personally hope that it can provide exactly the kind of unifying IRs we are all hoping for in the open-source community. The fact that the tools are implemented in C++ may be a win for some, but I think the CIRCT project is compelling for much deeper reasons. The README states the ambition clearly: > By working together, we hope that we can build a new center of gravity to draw contributions from the small (but enthusiastic!) community of people who work on open hardware tooling. There are weekly community meetings that are open to the public, and we have guest speakers from all sorts of interesting projects in the open-source community. Many of those are leading to collaborations and contributions to CIRCT. There hasn't been much (any?) discussion of CIRCT on HN, but rather than present the reasons I think it's so great here, I'll point to a talk[1] I gave earlier this year and a much better talk[2] Chris Lattner gave shortly thereafter, both of which lead up to the \"Why CIRCT?\" question in the second half. Looking back at that SymbiFlow thread, I see familiar faces that are now actively contributing to CIRCT. There are mentions of many different hardware IRs in some of the posts, but at least three have first-class support in CIRCT today: FIRRTL[3], LLHD[4], and Calyx[5]. This is all very recent and experimental, but I would say the results are already promising. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5]"},{"id":27017940,"author":"CalChris","month":"2021-05","story":"“Shared libraries are not a good thing in general”","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the purpose of RISC-V","quote":"I watched Chris Lattner's ASPLOS talk and it finally clicked.","text":"They've tried variations of this. It takes instruction bits to describe these registers: 32 regs is 5 bits, target + src1 + src2 is 15 bits already. They've tried scratch pads. The Mill CPU's Belt is yet another approach. [LINK] [LINK] How to Use 1000 Registers [LINK] 1000 registers would be 10 bits. So they tried register windows. These are all good ideas but they get to compete. So far, I'm a huge AArch64 fan but I now see the purpose of RISC-V. I watched Chris Lattner's ASPLOS talk and it finally clicked. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26909592,"author":"justaguy88","month":"2021-04","story":"Cerebras’ new monster AI chip adds 1.4T transistors","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on compiler ecosystem gaps in standardization for accelerators","quote":"You might like this talk that describes parts of the compiler ecosystem that are lacking standardization for accelerators","text":"You might like this talk that describes parts of the compiler ecosystem that are lacking standardization for accelerators [VIDEO]"}]},"JFaOYYSxSEA":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever Another World comes up, as one of the better GDC talks by the game's creator.","q":{"x":"it's one of the better GDC talks","i":36739178,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36739178,"author":"pengaru","month":"2023-07","story":"Another World Ported to FPGA","stance":"rec","why":"Developer shows his tools and steps through animation VM bytecode.","quote":"it's one of the better GDC talks","text":"The original developer of Another World did a GDC postmortem, it's one of the better GDC talks: [VIDEO] IIRC He shows some of his development tools including how he could modify and step through animation directly line by line in VM bytecode."},{"id":27406987,"author":"slmjkdbtl","month":"2021-06","story":"Another World: Genesis","stance":"rec","why":"The author's GDC talk on Another World.","quote":"also the author's GDC talk","text":"Also check out Fabian Sanglard's articles [0] (there's a lot more on his article listings) about Another World if you haven't yet, also the author's GDC talk [1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21939137,"author":"newnewpdro","month":"2020-01","story":"The Polygons of Another World","stance":"rec","why":"Another World's creator discusses how the game was made.","quote":"Eric Chahi gave a great GDC","text":"Another World's original author Eric Chahi gave a great GDC \"Classic Game Postmortem\" talk about creating the game: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21948047,"author":"JohnBooty","month":"2020-01","story":"The Polygons of Another World: Amiga 500","stance":"rec","why":"Chahi explains polygon choice and shows his rotoscoping and animation tools.","quote":"Very cool video, wherein Chahi demonstrates his rotoscoping system","text":"Here's Eric Chahi explaining his decision to use polygons - he mistakenly thought that the Amiga version of Dragon's Lair used them! [VIDEO] Very cool video, wherein Chahi demonstrates his rotoscoping system, where he could use the Amiga's genlock to trace live video in his (extremely impressive) homemade animation/code tool. The polygons in Another World are a bit different from today's games. In a modern game, game models would contain fully 3D representations of each model. Each polygon vertice would have X, Y, and Z coords. The CPU/GPU then render these out. In Another World, the polygons are 2D representations only, like paper cutouts. The animations are precalculated, and then at runtime they basically just need to be blitted out using some of the clever techniques mentioned in the linked article. You wouldn't really be able to alter the animations at runtime or change the perspective, but that was OK - it was a smart compromise to hit Chahi's stated target of about 20fps. This \"2D polygon\" approach was a brilliant way to overcome the limitations of CPUs at this time. There were fully 3D polygon games at the time, but they run at a fraction of the frame rate as Another World and contain only a fraction of the detail. The 2D polygon world of Another World felt fluid and real (well, relative to the competition at the time) and some convincing pseudo 3D effects could even be faked."}],"r":[["Ja5Q75hf6QI",2],["c2lgEyNaop4",1],["X29lF43mUlo",1],["7xTGNNLPyMI",1],["jK0BkTPUGQY",1],["tzr7hRXcwkw",1],["VFFlO7yBIBM",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1]]},"JurplDfPi3U":{"wy":"Praised as the best crash course on fusion reactor design nuances from an insider, used to temper fusion hype.","q":{"x":"I find it's the best crash course on the nuances of reactor design that I've seen.","i":28705153,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28729656,"author":"willis936","month":"2021-10","story":"How close is nuclear fusion power?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best video on fusion plant design, nails the engineering community's feel","quote":"the best video I've seen on fusion plant design came out in the past few weeks","text":"I haven't had a chance to watch this video yet, but the best video I've seen on fusion plant design came out in the past few weeks. It's titled \"Former fusion scientist on why we won't have fusion by 2040\". [VIDEO] It really nails the feel of the fusion engineering community I've been in. Little things said in passing in this video don't show up in public facing videos (such as a reactor needing to be primarily heated via ECRH) yet are regular topics with experts."},{"id":28705153,"author":"willis936","month":"2021-09","story":"Fusion News Ignites Optimism","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best crash course on reactor design nuances from a fusion insider","quote":"I find it's the best crash course on the nuances of reactor design that I've seen.","text":"I like being optimistic, but there is nothing to be optimistic about when it comes to producing electrical power from laser ICF. It's a weapons program. Also, I spent a few years as an engineer at a stellarator. This video[0] recently came out and I find it's the best crash course on the nuances of reactor design that I've seen. It really nails the thoughts and feelings of fusion researchers. It was especially refreshing to hear in public that ECRH is the only viable heating method for a reactor. If you watch this you might think I'm pessimistic about fusion, but I'm not. Fusion has been sold as this secret Ace up our sleeve that will come in and decarbonize our grid in a matter of decades. It isn't that. That's what's been sold because it's difficult to justify the field when the timelines don't decarbonize the grid in 50 years. The important thing to know is that the world doesn't end in 50 years. Energy demands are ever increasing and the supply is ever diminishing. Fusion science is expensive and time consuming. Humans will have to do the work if they want to stay on the current track beyond the next 100 years. It might seem like a subtle point and a doomed one to make considering how shortsighted we as a society have demonstrated to be. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33950339,"author":"cratermoon","month":"2022-12","story":"U.S. to announce fusion energy ‘breakthrough’","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"The Department of Energy plans to announce Tuesday that scientists have been able for the first time to produce a fusion reaction that creates a net energy gain\" Oh, just like this time in 2013, [LINK] , and this one in 2021 [LINK] Which definition of breakeven are they using this time? [VIDEO]"},{"id":33960112,"author":"cratermoon","month":"2022-12","story":"US scientists boost clean power hopes with fusion energy breakthrough","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"just like this time in 2013, [LINK] ..., and this one in 2021 [LINK] ... Which definition of breakeven are they using this time? [VIDEO]"},{"id":33325613,"author":"cratermoon","month":"2022-10","story":"Fusion Energy Might Power the Grid by the 2030s","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Former fusion scientist on why we won't have fusion power by 2040: [VIDEO] \"Theres a famous saying that nuclear fusion is 30 years away and always will be\""},{"id":29206811,"author":"dcx","month":"2021-11","story":"The good things in the current age in tech","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It just so happens that a short while after I saw this comment, this [1] turned up on my recommendations feed. What would you say are the counterarguments that lead you to believe fusion is on the horizon? (Ideally in more specific terms than \"when a distinguished but elderly scientist\", etc.) Just to be clear, I don't personally have a position on this; I don't know enough about the field. But I'm very interested in understanding developments in energy production, as at the end of the day, this is humanity's only real source of leverage. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29212945,"author":"ergot_vacation","month":"2021-11","story":"Nuclear fusion is close enough to start dreaming","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was hopeful for a bit, then I saw some stuff like this: [VIDEO] and realized nothing has changed. It's the same old shit: reporters being willfully ignorant, business people misleading the public, and on and on. Wake me up when you have plans for a full-scale, fully-functional fusion plant that can actually be built in an economically feasible way. Until then, I don't care."}],"r":[["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["ObhK8lUfIlc",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["C25qzDhGLx8",1],["D28uSzCs7-k",1]]},"YMqgiXLjvRs":{"wy":"HN people link it for Paul Graham's candid views on startups, Aaron Swartz, and investing selection.","q":{"x":"PG definitely gives up lots of great success tips, so worth watching it all.","i":8525792,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":11317674,"author":"tim333","month":"2016-03","story":"I Am Sam Altman, President of Y Combinator.  AMA","stance":"rec","why":"PG discusses missing SendGrid and selection.","quote":"pg talking about what may have been Sendgrid was quite interesting along those lines","text":"I can't answer that but pg talking about what may have been Sendgrid was quite interesting along those lines [VIDEO]"},{"id":8525792,"author":"tjculbertson","month":"2014-10","story":"YC W15 emails are out","stance":"rec","why":"PG gives many startup success tips.","quote":"PG definitely gives up lots of great success tips, so worth watching it all.","text":"I think it was in this video with Jcal [VIDEO] . Not sure at what point in the video, but PG definitely gives up lots of great success tips, so worth watching it all. As far as the black swans, I could be wrong on this, but my understanding is that YC has maybe 10 partners all giving a grade on the apps coming in (apparently started this after they missed SendGrid). Im not sure if the 1400 alums initially flag or what, but even with the 10 partners grading, their odds of finding black swans get worse because black swans don't look like winners to most people - they are essentially counter intuitive, crazy ideas that don't appeal to the majority in the beginning (or maybe the founders are not Stanford drop outs or ex Googlers, which further sways the majority to give higher grade to what is more obvious. Thus pushing down more potential black swans below the interview cut off line. So the more partners you have grading a particular startup, the more partners you have trying to cover their asses and not fuck up. So the safer choices will get more interviews - and safe choices don't usually result in black swans. Not sure if I'm making sense here, but maybe the proof is in the pudding in that some of their biggest wins are Dropbox, Reddit, and Airbnb...all relatively early in YC batches when there were fewer partners making choices."},{"id":8041398,"author":"asadlionpk","month":"2014-07","story":"Aaron Swartz and the YC Community","stance":"rec","why":"Paul Graham's views on Aaron Swartz around 7:42.","quote":"You should check out what pg thinks of Aaron in this recent video","text":"I thought about that too when I watched that documentary. You should check out what pg thinks of Aaron in this recent video: [VIDEO] at around 7:42"},{"id":10613249,"author":"vonklaus","month":"2015-11","story":"Wall Street Talent Is Moving to Silicon Valley","stance":"reference","why":"PG talks about MBAs managing programmers during the interview.","quote":"Here is him talking about MBA's whipping their meek rent-a-programmers during the interview[0]","text":"No way can I parse 10 hours of PG video. Just rewatched the Calacanis interview and some of Charlie Rose. I vividly remember him speaking about the study, but can't remember if it was an interview or just a speaking engagement (possibly Pycon 08). Here is him talking about MBA's whipping their meek rent-a-programmers during the interview[0] which is the best I can do. If anyone remembers what video it is in, or better yet, actually knows the (i believe harvard business school) study, would be super interested. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IkdziSLYzHw",1],["7k1ehaE0bdU",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["baFaEvBywGc",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1],["9NOncx2jU0Q",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1]]},"gPlpphT7n9s":{"wy":"Frequently linked as a short, accessible demonstration of how Shakespeare actually sounded.","q":{"x":"I very much enjoyed this quick introduction to Shakespeare in Original Pronunciation","i":7384296,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":26809225,"author":"gchucky","month":"2021-04","story":"Great Vowel Shift","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates vowel shift changes to pronunciation","quote":"There was a rather fascinating video that went around awhile back","text":"The vowel shift is one of the major reasons why Shakespeare's poetry doesn't rhyme when spoken with modern English. There was a rather fascinating video that went around awhile back of a father and son demonstrating the changes.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26818808,"author":"petercooper","month":"2021-04","story":"Are We Getting Shakespeare’s Rhythms All Wrong?","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to Shakespeare's pronunciation including missed puns","quote":"as an introduction to the realities of the pronunciation of Shakespeare's English","text":"Along similar lines, some years ago I really enjoyed [VIDEO] as an introduction to the realities of the pronunciation of Shakespeare's English. Jump to 5 minutes in for a demonstration of how \"lines\" and \"loins\" were pronounced the same and resulted in a pun that's missed in modern pronunciation."},{"id":7384296,"author":"zhte415","month":"2014-03","story":"Pronunciation errors that made the English language","stance":"rec","why":"Quick intro to original pronunciation and lost puns","quote":"I very much enjoyed this quick introduction to Shakespeare in Original Pronunciation","text":"I very much enjoyed this quick introduction to Shakespeare in Original Pronunciation (the sound of speech at the time) and how many puns and much interpretation is lost in modern accents. From the Open University: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5130738,"author":"keyboardP","month":"2013-01","story":"When Did Americans Lose Their British Accents?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Shakespeare's original pronunciation with an example","quote":"The entire video has some nice information","text":"A bit OT but hopefully interesting nevertheless. This is apparently the original pronunciation for the works of Shakespeare [VIDEO] The entire video has some nice information but if you want to skip to an example of the original pronunciation, you can jump to 2:50."},{"id":43125298,"author":"0tfoaij","month":"2025-02","story":"BritCSS: Fixes CSS to use non-American English","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Shakespearean English for accent comparison","quote":"","text":"The idea that American spelling and pronunciation have a better heritage than British English is a compelling one, especially as the idea that Southern and Appalachian accents are closer to those of the Founding Fathers and Shakespearean English is a nice balance to the perception that these accents sound unintelligent and uneducated, but it's simply not true that one dialect has diverged more than another - both have diverged and in many cases substantially. One of the common reasons given is that British accents like RP (there's a lot to criticise about RP but that's another topic), Cockney (featured elsewhere on the thread and the internet in general, oi m8 you got a loicence for that?), and general loss of rhoticity in BrE (and some AmE) accents that are most represented in American media have diverged substantially, but to me the examples of Shakespearean English in classic pronunciation sound closer to the West Country accents than they do any American accent. Note that there could be some bias here as the speakers are British, but you get features like H-dropping which simply don't exist in AmE. It also wouldn't be fair to say any modern accent sounds even remotely close to this. Shakespearean English: [LINK] [VIDEO] Some good reddit threads on the matter: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Another weird one is spelling, given that etymology and spelling is pretty interesting in general, at least up until the advent of the printing press. Both BrE and AmE have made some questionable"},{"id":42632613,"author":"AndrewDucker","month":"2025-01","story":"Schola Latina Europæa and Universalis","stance":"reference","why":"Sound of Shakespeare's original pronunciation","quote":"","text":"The history of how we know what Latin sounded like is fascinating. Reminds me this video on what Shakespeare's original pronunciation sounded like: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32347022,"author":"joshuaissac","month":"2022-08","story":"Old jokes","stance":"reference","why":"Performance recapturing puns lost in modern accents","quote":"","text":"There are some renditions of Shakespeare's plays with pronunciations from where and when they were written. It recaptures some of the puns and jokes that are not apparent in most modern accents. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28898322,"author":"aasasd","month":"2021-10","story":"Simon Roper: the 23-year-old reconstructing the past for millions of viewers","stance":"reference","why":"Shows puns lost in modern pronunciation","quote":"","text":"See also: about Shakespeare in the Early Modern English pronunciation: [VIDEO] Specifically how some puns don't work with the pronunciation of today, like the ‘hour’."},{"id":24390969,"author":"stan_rogers","month":"2020-09","story":"Winner of French Scrabble Title Does Not Speak French (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Starting point for Ben Crystal's original pronunciation","quote":"A good starting point is","text":"Newfanese is pretty much crystallized Hiberno-English, at least if we're talking the language of the bay wops rather than townies. It has a surprising amount of commonality with African-American vernacular, likely due to transportation and indenture (which is why AAVE, while distinct, barely edges into typical creole patterns). The English of Shakespeare's time, or at least of his plays, has been largely reconstructed by linguist David Crystal, whose son, the actor and director Ben Crystal, is probably the best-known practitioner. There are a number of videos on YouTube of Ben doing Shakespeare in OP. A good starting point is [VIDEO] - and if one had to tie it closely to a modern regional variant, it would be West Country. But that would just be something it would remind you of rather than a template."},{"id":17196377,"author":"robotmay","month":"2018-05","story":"Opening of Romeo and Juliet Recited in the Accent of Shakespeare’s Time [video]","stance":"reference","why":"More original pronunciation Shakespeare from the same speaker","quote":"","text":"I do really wish more Shakespeare was performed in an original accent, as there's an awful lot of rhymes and jokes that don't work in modern pronunciation. Like \"hour to hour\" being pronounced like \"whore to whore\", which suddenly makes that line quite a bit funnier. Here's another video of the same bloke that I've seen in the past: [VIDEO] I do like that he mentioned being asked to perform using his native Welsh accent in the OP's video. Shakespeare was after all the playright for people of every standing, and it feels a bit daft to posh it all up."},{"id":17198341,"author":"drspacemonkey","month":"2018-05","story":"Opening of Romeo and Juliet Recited in the Accent of Shakespeare’s Time [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Additional original pronunciation Shakespeare videos","quote":"","text":"I've seen Ben Crystal do some other Original Pronunciation Shakespeare videos. It's his specialty, and I would love to see one of his \"original practice\" productions live one day. Here's a couple more of his videos: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":16382046,"author":"Izkata","month":"2018-02","story":"The IRS’s Effort to Convert Its Assembly Codebase to Java","stance":"reference","why":"Explains lost puns in modern pronunciation","quote":"","text":"Ah, but with how English has shifted over the centuries, the necessary domain knowledge has slowly been disappearing. Shakespeare's plays now and then [0] are slightly different (and back then, dirtier) because of it. (Jump to 2:30 for an explanation and example, then at 7:58 is the dirty pun that doesn't exist in modern pronunciation.) [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11558678,"author":"danellis","month":"2016-04","story":"William Shakespeare, Playwright and Poet, Is Dead at 52","stance":"reference","why":"Hear early modern English pronunciation","quote":"","text":"Judging by those spellings, we've also been pronouncing it wrongly. A pronunciation like \"shax pear\" does seem to have more of a early modern English ring to it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11296738,"author":"dalke","month":"2016-03","story":"How Far Back in Time Could You Travel and Still Understand English?","stance":"reference","why":"Contrasts modern theatrical and original pronunciation","quote":"","text":"FWIW, the Shakespeare example used modern theatrical pronunciation. [VIDEO] shows the difference between that and the original pronunciation."},{"id":10378791,"author":"_ak","month":"2015-10","story":"Why ‘Mom’ and ‘Dad’ Sound So Similar in So Many Languages","stance":"reference","why":"Linguists' reconstruction of Shakespeare's pronunciation","quote":"","text":"It's not quite like that. American pronunciation change as well. Here's an example of how linguist reconstruct the original pronunciation of Shakespeare plays: [VIDEO] It really has more elements that sound like West Country accent, Irish English, or even Scots/Ulster Scots."},{"id":9774787,"author":"ubernostrum","month":"2015-06","story":"Why we should all learn from the ancient Greeks","stance":"reference","why":"Example of original-pronunciation wordplay and rhymes","quote":"This video has an example","text":"There are companies which do original-pronunciation Shakespeare and make a point of showing wordplay and rhymes that don't exist in modern English pronunciation. This video has an example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8986002,"author":"junto","month":"2015-02","story":"Is Bilingualism Really an Advantage?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Shakespeare in original pronunciation","quote":"","text":"Funnily enough, some would argue that Shakespeare in modern English also isn't real Shakespeare! Check out the original prenounciation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6315185,"author":"brownbat","month":"2013-09","story":"English has been my pain for 15 years","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Shakespearean pronunciation","quote":"","text":"Most of English's \"problems\" come from a sort of natural drift that all languages experience all the time. There's no way to freeze a language in its \"most useful\" form, because a billion different speakers are simultaneously pulling it in different directions. It either ends up as a sort of democratic consensus, or eventually fractures into mutually unintelligible dialects. 1. Shakespearean pronunciation: [VIDEO] 2. Problems that would be caused by simplifying spelling: [LINK] 3. Power of Babel: [LINK] 4. Toastmasters speech about \"Manglish\": [LINK] (Toastmasters has its own version of English, full of over the top gestures. Also recommended, search for Singlish on Youtube.)"},{"id":5131598,"author":"benihana","month":"2013-01","story":"When Did Americans Lose Their British Accents?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just for future reference, if you right click on a video on youtube, you can click copy link at current time to lazy link to 2m50s so you don't have to say \"jump to this time.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":5133495,"author":"ithkuil","month":"2013-01","story":"When Did Americans Lose Their British Accents?","stance":"reference","why":"Gives original spelling of film in Shakespeare's work","quote":"","text":"Sure. For example [VIDEO] 00:04:36, states that film is spelled philome in Shakespeare's work. Unfortunately a google search of \"philome\" yields only pages about shakespeare pronounciation, especially by these two guys; I don't know what that means. However, I think that thinking about the evolution of a language as a linear progression is misleading. Is certainly possible that modern Irish pronunciation stems from what was spoken at that time, and the modern \"mainstream\" english pronunciation evolved later from a slighly different root, and epenthesis acted only in one of the two branches. Film derives from west germanic filminjan, which in turn comes from proto germanic fello(m), and proto indoeuropean pel-, with cognates in lating and greek, pella and pellis respectivly. So it's possible that epenthesis was applied on top of filminjan in irish scottish accents (possibly due to pressure of the gaelic substrate), while the dialect which originated RP didn't have this change. I'm sure somebody with better knowledge of the matter can be more precise about that, however, it's strange how little (and often more misleading than uncertain) information is there about one the most widely spoken languages. (Sorry, if I had more time, I would have written a shorter comment :-) )"}],"r":[["LRhbcDzbGSU",1],["N8elxpSu9pw",1],["y71lli8MS8s",1],["63vqob-MljQ",1],["eRkgK4jfi6M",1],["HQYsFshbkYw",1]]},"lhe286ky-9A":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a brief summary of the problem and the proposed Anti-Corruption Act solution when arguing for getting money out of politics.","q":{"x":"I find these videos to be helpful in briefly understanding the problem and their proposed solution","i":29271008,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29271008,"author":"auslegung","month":"2021-11","story":"Congress on Drugs","stance":"rec","why":"Brief explanation of the proposed solution.","quote":"I find these videos to be helpful in briefly understanding the problem and their proposed solution","text":"Incredibly frustrating. Represent.us is trying to do something about this. I find these videos to be helpful in briefly understanding the problem and their proposed solution 1: problem - [LINK] 2: their solution - [VIDEO]"},{"id":17545993,"author":"gremlinsinc","month":"2018-07","story":"Jeff Bezos Becomes the Richest Man in Modern History, Topping $150B","stance":"rec","why":"Anti-corruption acts to remove money from politics.","quote":"The key is passing anti-corruption acts that get money out of politics","text":"The key is passing anti-corruption acts that get money out of politics: [VIDEO] If it's illegal for lobbyists, corporations, etc to donate to politicians, then we can make politics more fair/ethical. This is a bipartisan group working to get these passed. Anti-corruption benefits everyone."},{"id":12868416,"author":"gremlinsinc","month":"2016-11","story":"Why Are US Presidential Elections So Close?","stance":"rec","why":"Anti-corruption bill advocacy.","quote":"we need to pass the anti-corruption bill asap","text":"As an EX democrat, I'd say the Democrat party is broken too, the amount of election fraud, and corruption in the party makes it look like the Mafia. The whole system is broken and we need to pass the anti-corruption bill asap, if we're to have a chance to take back our country from wall street. [VIDEO] EDIT: I say ex loosely.. I voted mostly dem. down the line, but wrote in Bernie Sanders, I'm not fond of Jill Stein something about her doesn't seem right. But I couldn't in good conscience do a protest vote for Trump, and I can never support Clinton, the most corrupt politician of the past 40 years."},{"id":14406271,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-05","story":"1Password Travel Mode: Protect your data when crossing borders","stance":"reference","why":"Group doing anti-corruption work.","quote":"Groups like The Anti-Corruption Act ( ) are doing a lot","text":"Keep in mind Marriage Equality in America came via the supreme court, not legislation (unlike New Zealand, Canada, et. al.) I'm not saying people shouldn't be active. Groups like The Anti-Corruption Act ( [VIDEO] ) are doing a lot, not to mention the group that pushed Maine's ranked voting amendment. But the vote itself is not very useful. There are other forms of activism that are more worthwhile; those that seek to slowly and fundamentally change the system. Focusing on the left right paradigm will ultimately lead people to being angry at two parties that are essentially the same."}],"r":[["5tu32CCA_Ig",1],["23X14HS4gLk",1]]},"oOlDewpCfZQ":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as the canonical demonstration that popular songs reuse one chord progression, especially in threads on music analysis and AI-generated music.","q":{"x":"the \"4 Chords\" medley by Axis of Awesome is a great demonstration of its ubiquity","i":31110242,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31110242,"author":"Sharlin","month":"2022-04","story":"What chords do you need?","stance":"rec","why":"Great demonstration of the ubiquity of one progression","quote":"the \"4 Chords\" medley by Axis of Awesome is a great demonstration of its ubiquity","text":"Heh, I just this afternoon listened to a radio show about the famously popular vi-IV-I-V/I-V-vi-IV chord progression [1]. If you haven't heard it before, the \"4 Chords\" medley by Axis of Awesome is a great demonstration of its ubiquity [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5378902,"author":"jcr","month":"2013-03","story":"I analyzed the chords to 1300 songs for patterns. Part 3 – Interactive Discovery","stance":"rec","why":"Shows four common chords across many songs","quote":"It shows the use of a set of four common chords in a lot of different songs.","text":"Though a bit more on the \"actual implementation\" side of things, there's an Australian group called \"Axis Of Awesome\" who did an interesting song called \"Four Chords\". It shows the use of a set of four common chords in a lot of different songs. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4104428,"author":"csl","month":"2012-06","story":"I analyzed the chords to 1300 popular songs for patterns. This is what I found.","stance":"rec","why":"Medley of songs sharing the same four chords","quote":"a 47-song medley all using the same chord progression","text":"On a related note (corniest pun of the day), the Australian musical comedy act Axis of Awesome made a song called Four Chords Song , which is basically a 47-song medley all using the same chord progression. [LINK] They've said that the chords are used by some of the most well known songs in the world, and their performance demonstrates this nicely, as in this clip: [VIDEO] The above link is the official video; but they explain their reasoning better in this live performance: [LINK]"},{"id":43725440,"author":"Twirrim","month":"2025-04","story":"I analyzed chord progressions in 680k songs","stance":"reference","why":"Famous demonstration of the four chord trick","quote":"famously demonstrated by the Aussie comedy band Axis of Awesome[2]","text":"By convention in music, we use Roman numerals to signify what chord we should play relative to the root (key). \"I\" refers to the root/tonic/key and we count up from there. [1] So, for example, a common three chord progression in a major scale would be I – IV – V. If we take the key of C, those would be C, F, G, as F and G are the fourth and fifth chords respectively. In the key of G, it'd be G, C and D. In that key, a good example song is \"Sweet Home Alabama\", where almost the entire song is just V - IV - I over and over again. One of the most popular chord progressions, used in an astounding number of pop songs is known as the \"Four Chord Trick\", I – V – VI – IV, famously demonstrated by the Aussie comedy band Axis of Awesome[2] I think I'd agree with the person you're replying to, both in that the original source is flawed due to not including the \"dupes\", despite them being important, and also because key is largely irrelevant, chord progression is much more important. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] ."},{"id":42245016,"author":"ben_w","month":"2024-11","story":"Nvidia Fugatto: \"World's Most Flexible Sound Machine\"","stance":"reference","why":"Shows similarity of most modern hits","quote":"Axis of Awesome demonstrated the similarity of most modern hits with their \"4 Chords","text":"I think there's a lot of different reasons all simultaneously going on. Most human musicians have very little power; that's been going away for a long time, even since \"canned music\" \"robots\" pushed live bands out of cinemas a century ago: [LINK] Most popular music already feels, and to an extent is, fake. Not only because mere recording allows repeated takes until it's inhumanly \"perfect\". When I played an MP3 of Britney Spears to my mum around the turn of the century, she thought it was a robot singing because of the autotune. The Monkees was famously an attempt at a manufactured band whose members just happened to not feel like playing that game and did it for real, Gorillaz is even more obviously manufactured. Parasocial relationships are inherently different from \"real\" relationships, but the performers have to pretend that it's personal when they address a crowd or a camera. Axis of Awesome demonstrated the similarity of most modern hits with their \"4 Chords\": [VIDEO] Those with the power were, possibly still are, the record labels; but if the AI are trained on the works of small musicians that can't afford the copyright cases or the political influence, but also whose works are not under the umbrella of the labels who do have those resources but not the right or short term motivation to intercede on their behalf, the big labels themselves may lose the consumer market to free AI output, while professionals will dismiss both the AI output and the label's output as \"just "},{"id":37346496,"author":"gremlinsinc","month":"2023-09","story":"U.S. Copyright Office RFC: Artificial Intelligence and Copyright","stance":"reference","why":"Songs are far from completely original","quote":"I challenge you to listen to 4 chords of awesome and tell me again about how every song is completely original.","text":"I challenge you to listen to 4 chords of awesome and tell me again about how every song is completely original. How does eragon exist when it's definitely ripped parts from star wars, etc...ai usually doesn't spit out a full plagiarism, but a loosely inspired work which is what most media we consume is. Edit: 4 chords of awesome link is [VIDEO]"},{"id":32563428,"author":"richrichardsson","month":"2022-08","story":"An odd discovery on Spotify","stance":"reference","why":"Same four chords as the Axis of Awesome","quote":"those are the same \"4 chords\" used by the Axis of Awesome","text":"iirc those are the same \"4 chords\" used by the Axis of Awesome [1]. Certainly sounded like that to my ear from memory, but don't really have enough music notation/theory knowledge to know for certain and I may have been mis-remembering. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31214847,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: Teach me something new","stance":"reference","why":"Most popular songs reuse the same chords","quote":"Most popular songs are composed by using mostly the same repeating chord patterns","text":"> Most popular songs are composed by using mostly the same repeating chord patterns [VIDEO] Way too many to list."},{"id":29056472,"author":"NikolaNovak","month":"2021-10","story":"Fundamentals of Piano Practice","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Primarily, it's about payoff / benefit. Imagine you're 40 years old and you've never played an instrument. You have limited ambition - you want to have fun and play a little bit around campfire. How do you start, let's say on a guitar: 1. I show you G, C, D chords and print out a simple, easily understandable chart you can take home. You now can play hundreds of songs after 30 min lesson and a few mins of practice. You can spend time playing around and slowly learning rhythm, strumming techniques and patterns, while singing to your songs and getting a feel for your instrument. A week or three later I show you E-minor and now you can play virtually every pop song made in last 20 years. [1] If you get excited and interested, we throw in Aminor, and eventually F Major and now you know power chords and you're the master of it all. You're having fun, you're playing, you're improving, and you're having FUN. You can focus on good techniques and sounding good. When and if you want more, we can learn basic music theory and pentatonic patterns, and then one day if you're serious and ready for some pain, you can learn some staff notation. Or! 2. I give you some books and tell you to learn notes. You download an app or six to enable you to mindlessly practices notes every day. You spend months studying by rote and can maybe play Twinkle Twinkle Little star, poorly. But it's academic because you gave up a long time before you got there as there was no FUN to be had and you have children a"},{"id":24211488,"author":"sammorrowdrums","month":"2020-08","story":"Ask HN: How to Become a Music Buff?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is a solid answer, but I'd also add - there are very few general music buffs. Maybe I'd count, with years in pro music performance, degree in Jazz and a lifetime of listing to and performing an insanely wide variety of genres... but not a buff for all styles. Computer systems are actually not a bad analogy. How many people do you know that have an in-depth knowledge of Assembly in different systems (including the oldest ones), GPUs, ML, Python, JS, Haskell, C++, Rust, ALGOL, Compilers, Hardware development, etc. Then add into the mix having an in-depth knowledge of the creators of those things, what else they did, failures, influences, their mentors. You always have the choice of breadth versus depth, within your time constraints. I think learning about a single artist or band, looking up the producer, label, members and following up on all those leads to see what else they have done - and see if you like it is a great beginning. Some genres like jazz might seem difficult to get into, and that's fine. No need to delve in until you find a hook that peaks your interest. And then when you find out about a genre (and jazz is an excellent example) - you'll find sub-genres you like, and don't and maybe you are interested in the evolution - like many people love bebop, but don't listen to \"dixieland jazz\" or big band swing, or modern fusion. You will never hear all the music. But depth (in my opinion) breads a lot more passion than breadth just for the sake of it. So rather tha"},{"id":17559807,"author":"gt2","month":"2018-07","story":"What Makes a Hit","stance":"reference","why":"Many hit songs share the same progression","quote":"this video that cycles through a long list of hit songs which have the same progression","text":"Couldn't those guys' track of success be due to cronyism in the music industry? Their songs are formulaic. I'm sure many of these 2 guys' songs are played in this video that cycles through a long list of hit songs which have the same progression (transposed into the same key so the similarities can more easily be noticed) [VIDEO] So perhaps anyone can write the songs, but not everyone has access to the pop stars (or access to these 2 guys). I'm not an expert song writer myself, but I am a musician and lover of music. Based on the fact that there's awesome music noone's ever heard of or that doesn't get as much play as some lesser quality music, I have to echo the common sentiment that the music industry is a fashion show/popularity contest with gatekeepers (still). Not saying it's not all a sham, I think the difference that comes from the human performing is huge, and I love to see and hear that."},{"id":16161872,"author":"melq","month":"2018-01","story":"The brains of jazz and classical pianists work differently","stance":"reference","why":"Example of standard chord progressions in pop","quote":"","text":"Many genres of music have songs that are mostly comprised of a handful of chord sequences, or 'chord progressions'. For instance, you will hear the I - IV - V all the time in western pop. An example of this (with a different chord progression): [VIDEO] So unexpected harmonies are sequences that don;t fit in to these well-known, 'standard' progressions."},{"id":13782965,"author":"samfisher83","month":"2017-03","story":"Amper raises $4M to use AI to write music","stance":"reference","why":"Popular songs share four chords","quote":"people figured out the chord pattern of most popular songs","text":"Even before machine learning people figured out the chord pattern of most popular songs. You can probably program it use those 4 chords. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11671953,"author":"nanodano","month":"2016-05","story":"I'm a good engineer but I suck at building stuff","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Another thing I run in to a lot is people saying \"I know how to program, but I don't know what to build.\" There is an amount of creativity involved with programminng. It's like learning how to physically manipulate a guitar and play notes, but saying \"I don't know what to play.\" That happens a lot too, but musicians have the repetoire of other composers to play if they do not want to write their own music (many don't). With programming, recoding something is called reinventing the wheel and that's frowned upon. Ok, sometimes the analogies are forced, but I hope you get it! It's kind of interesting though, because in order to progress we have to build something new, or build on top of what we have, so the technology is forced to evolve. Music can repeat over and over and people don't get tired of it and you could argue pop music is working against the evolution of music. For anyone who hasn't seen the four chord song... [VIDEO] Wait, what we were originally talking about now?"},{"id":21550716,"author":"braindongle","month":"2019-11","story":"Musical Chord Progression Arpeggiator","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I, V, VI, IV in major MUST NOT be allowed!","text":"Great work, Jake Albaugh. Needs one critical revision, though: I, V, VI, IV in major MUST NOT be allowed! [VIDEO]"},{"id":32556126,"author":"ben_w","month":"2022-08","story":"Stable Diffusion Public Release","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"How much is really being said by the highest dollar-valued modern music? [VIDEO] and [LINK] come to mind for different reasons."}],"r":[["gbYXBJOFgeI",1],["wt-iVFxgFWk",1],["hC3VTgIPoGU",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1],["A8eCGOqgvH4",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1]]},"pIgMTsQXg3Q":{"wy":"Recommended in governance threads for showing how randomness and short terms prevented power concentration.","q":{"x":"This is a good video on this","i":47343227,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47343227,"author":"KK7NIL","month":"2026-03","story":"Britain is ejecting hereditary nobles from Parliament after 700 years","stance":"rec","why":"Explains randomness and short terms in Athenian democracy preventing power concentration.","quote":"This is a good video on this","text":"Perhaps you're joking, but Athenian democracy had a significant amount of randomness, with candidates being chosen randomly from the top vote winners. Terms were also only 1 year for most positions. These, and other systems, helped prevent any one person from monopolizing power. This is a good video on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24855219,"author":"vulcan01","month":"2020-10","story":"Call to arms: Sweden raises defense spending by 40% in 5 years","stance":"rec","why":"Covers Athenian leader selection by lot, with sortition at 8m00s.","quote":"You should watch the whole thing (it's pretty interesting)","text":"The ancient Athenians used something similar to select their leaders: [LINK] EDIT: I remember this from a Historia Civilis video: [VIDEO] You should watch the whole thing (it's pretty interesting) but skip to 8m00s to watch the part about sortition."},{"id":17130219,"author":"drexlspivey","month":"2018-05","story":"Facebook CEO speaks to select members of European Parliament","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the constitution of ancient Athens.","quote":"Historia civilis has a great video on the constitution of ancient Athens.","text":"In ancient Athens only adult male Athenian citizens who had completed their military duties were able to vote, roughly 20% of the population. This seems pretty oligarchic today but it was a massive improvement in the council of elders that used to rule before that. Historia civilis has a great video on the constitution of ancient Athens. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1],["zcqZHYo7ONs",1],["MzRCDLre1b4",1],["42QuXLucH3Q",1]]},"t0mhvd3-60Y":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear introduction to linear types in GHC from the language's lead designer.","q":{"x":"Simon Peyton Jones has a great talk on YouTube [3]","i":22712365,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24636771,"author":"pyrale","month":"2020-09","story":"Haskell GHC 9.0.1-alpha","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to linear types in GHC","quote":"SPJ usually makes great introductions about new things being introduced to GHC.","text":"SPJ usually makes great introductions about new things being introduced to GHC. Here's one about linear types: [VIDEO] > How do people that don't have PhDs bridge the gap in their knowledge from what's commonly taught/used (OOP and it's design patterns) to thinking in terms of monads/monoids/functors/etc. In my own experience, you just practice, and munch new knowledge bits by bits. The crucial point here is that you don't need to know _all_ of haskell to be productive using it. So you learn the basics, start using monads without understanding the Monad abstraction (it's really not something you need to understand to use), and after a while it just clicks. And once you're confortable with this basic knowledge, you learn new things when you need them. As a result, I (and many other paid haskellers) have a job writing haskell without having a PhD, without learning category theory, or without a deep math bagage."},{"id":22712365,"author":"throwaway17_17","month":"2020-03","story":"Programming Models for Distributed Computation","stance":"rec","why":"Practical linear types for Haskell","quote":"Simon Peyton Jones has a great talk on YouTube [3]","text":"Session Types, AFAIU are a specific implementation of a linear logic in some type system and I am not all too familiar with them in practice. However, I would like to think I am well versed in Linear Logic and Substructural Type Theory. I think the works by Wadler are a good place to start, in particular is a sort of review paper, based in intuitionistic logic (which he also covers in said paper), on linear logic. It’s called ‘A Taste of Linear Logic’ (I actually have the paper open in Safari on my iPhone as I type this) and it is written as a sort of primer on the area. Wadler’s bibliographies on the works contained in the pages you linked are a good source as well. If you are at all coming from a place of familiarity with Haskell, a decent overview of the current discussions on Linear Types in functional languages is at [2]. And lastly, if you like videos, Simon Peyton Jones has a great talk on YouTube [3] where he discusses practical linear types for Haskell, but the theory is not Haskell specific. I will warn you that the latter two references make several arguments and then discuss uniqueness typing (which is related but very different) and that is a whole other rabbit hole of sources, but generally a good topic to dig into as well. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18174165,"author":"T-R","month":"2018-10","story":"Rust RAII is better than the Haskell bracket pattern","stance":"rec","why":"Peyton Jones on linear types and exceptions","quote":"Simon Peyton Jones gave an excellent presentation on the topic[2]","text":"Something to keep in mind - linear types are on their way[1], with exactly this usecase in mind. Simon Peyton Jones gave an excellent presentation on the topic[2], briefly discussing exceptions, as well as giving a mention to ResourceT and the phantom type solution in the article (described as channel-passing). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24635958,"author":"T-R","month":"2020-09","story":"Haskell GHC 9.0.1-alpha","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on linear types in GHC","quote":"Simon Peyton Jones talk","text":"My understanding is that the current implementation is only groundwork for possible future performance benefits, since there are no changes to the runtime system or garbage collector, and that their current intended use is mostly for allowing library authors to encode safe session/resource handling in the type system. So, new libraries can make it a type error to fail to properly close a connection or release a shared resource. Simon Peyton Jones talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21664746,"author":"mauricioc","month":"2019-11","story":"Unsoundness in Pin","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Peyton Jones's early guess about Rust","quote":"","text":"Simon Peyton Jones initial intuition [0] was that Rust would be full of soundness holes due to not starting with a full formalization. Derek Dryer, Ralf Jung and others in the RustBelt project [1] did amazing work in formalizing Rust's safety guarantees, showing that Peyton Jones' guess was not accurate. There is nothing wrong about having bugs, of course, but the reaction to the bug in this thread shows that mathematical correctness is not as universally valued as I thought it would be. I agree with the sentiment that this is a bigger issue that shouldn't be discussed in a technical thread about a specific bug. However, after reading this, it is unclear to me whether mathematical correctness is regarded in the Rust project as an explicit goal, an explicit non-goal or an unessential nice-to-have. (I do not mean to insinuate anything with the word \"unclear\", as I believe all three options are valid and appeal to different use cases. Almost all of the popular languages don't care about this, for instance.) [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":18001009,"author":"icc97","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: What topics/subjects are worth learning for a new software engineer?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Peyton Jones on Rust linear types","quote":"","text":"My personal opinion but this is purely because I enjoy it the most is functional programming. So much of the positive changes I've seen of late come through functional programming. For example: * ReasonML - this is what the founder of React is working on now. The ReasonML claims itself to be JavaScript 2030 (I see it as JavaScript + TypeScript + Immutable.JS + Redux). This contains a lot of the concepts from JavaScript so is a reasonable step to make * If ReasonML isn't too scary it leads you down a path to OCaml, F# and Elm * Rust (not strictly a functional programming language, but is ridiculously fast and contains well implemented Linear Types that makes Haskell's Simon Peyton-Jones jealous [0]) - this is one of the most loved programming languages according to the Stack Overflow survey [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3uiEyEKji0M",1]]},"tcdVC4e6EV4":{"wy":"Often pointed to as an accessible first introduction to AI safety and why a superintelligent AI could be dangerous.","q":{"x":"There's a great Computerphile video from 2015 that introduced me to the idea of AI safety awhile ago.","i":33962078,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35633080,"author":"circuit10","month":"2023-04","story":"Stability AI Launches the First of Its StableLM Suite of Language Models","stance":"rec","why":"Why a theoretical superintelligent AI would be dangerous","quote":"for a really interesting video on why a theoretical superintelligent AI would be dangerous","text":"But the concerns about AI taking over the world are valid and important; even if they sound silly at first, there is some very solid reasoning behind it. They’re big matrices, yes, but they’re Turing-complete which means they can theoretically do any computational task See [VIDEO] for a really interesting video on why a theoretical superintelligent AI would be dangerous, and when you factor in that these models could self-improve and approach that level of intelligence it gets worrying…"},{"id":35658241,"author":"circuit10","month":"2023-04","story":"GitHub Copilot emits GPL code","stance":"rec","why":"Why a theoretical superintelligent AI would be dangerous","quote":"for a really interesting video on why a theoretical superintelligent AI would be dangerous","text":"\"those non-problems\" Why is that a non-problem? It's a really important concern that we need to take more seriously I pasted this from another comment I wrote but: The concerns about AI taking over the world are valid and important; even if they sound silly at first, there is some very solid reasoning behind it. See [VIDEO] for a really interesting video on why a theoretical superintelligent AI would be dangerous, and when you factor in that these models could self-improve and approach that level of intelligence it gets worrying…"},{"id":33962078,"author":"johncs","month":"2022-12","story":"Perhaps It Is a Bad Thing That the Leading AI Companies Cannot Control Their AIs","stance":"rec","why":"Introduced commenter to AI safety; useful context for article","quote":"There's a great Computerphile video from 2015 that introduced me to the idea of AI safety awhile ago.","text":"There's a great Computerphile video from 2015 that introduced me to the idea of AI safety awhile ago. It seems like some good context to this article: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11384601,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-03","story":"Why AlphaGo Is Such a Big Deal","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to strong-AI finding solutions against humanity's interest","quote":"I recommend watching the previous video first, which introduces the problem of strong-AI rapidly finding solutions","text":"> exponential leap forward A recent(-ish) Computerphile video has a good overview of the AI-self-improvement problem. [LINK] I recommend watching the previous video first, which introduces the problem of strong-AI rapidly finding solutions (especially with poorly-specified questions) that may not be in humanity's best interest. [VIDEO] Before anybody jumps to overly-sensational conclusions, note that the last video in that series, Rob Miles explains how exponential self-improvement is an extreme point in the space of possible AI development. We don't know how to predict discoveries[1], so we need more research into AI, so we can hopefully make something that isn't exponentially growing beyond our understanding. [LINK] [1] see James Burke's non-teleological view of change"},{"id":40114857,"author":"npoc","month":"2024-04","story":"Bitcoin Block 840000","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's an issue with the concept of a \"black budget\", not the payment mechanism. The government corruption bitcoin stops is really bank corruption, but the central banks (including the fed) work hand in hand with the governments (control them in fact), so it's a kind of joint effort. The corruption works like this: When the Fed was formed in the early 20th century, it came to an agreement with the government to be able to print off unlimited amounts of money, providing it was in the form of loans and under the agreement that it would destroy the money when the loans were repaid. All under the Keynesian guise of \"elasticity of money\". The fly in the ointment is that the banks can charge interest on this money they print - if they didn't it would be a free-for all, because interest is what disincentivises debt. What this led to was the doubling of the money supply every decade for the last 100 years, as the banks are incentivised to lend as much money as possible. [LINK] Each time you double the number of currency units, the total value of the money is diluted across double the number of units and so the value of each unit is halved. If you do this too quickly, people lose confidence in the money and you fall into hyperinflation, but if you throttle it just right, they don't catch on. It helps that the value of consumables (cars, food, toothbrushes etc) goes down by around 5%/year due to technology-driven manufacturing efficiency gains. This means that they can devalue the curr"},{"id":26487855,"author":"ExtraE","month":"2021-03","story":"Moore's Law for Everything","stance":"reference","why":"Source for claim that general AI could kill us","quote":"","text":"I don’t see the world this piece presents panning out. Either we make general intelligence (which then scales almost instantly to be way, way smarter than any human ever and then accidentally kills us [1]) or we don’t and humans still have a role to play in the workforce. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20914418,"author":"throwaway2048","month":"2019-09","story":"Artificial intelligence is changing every aspect of war","stance":"reference","why":"Supporting source for AI being unpredictable and dangerous","quote":"","text":"We understand how people work and think pretty well, their edgecases, and countermeasures to said edgecases. We don't understand edge cases of AI much at all, and the terrain-space of \"possible minds\" they might be is vastly broader than that of a human mind, and utterly outside our experience. An AI might work with absolute 100% flawless precision for 100 years, then decided one day to kill everyone. A lot harder to see that coming from a totally alien intelligence than a human one. AI does not think like humans, have human values, or care about what humans care about, that might make them extremely unpredictable and dangerous. There will be no time for iterative development, or learning from our mistakes, a sufficiently advanced AI knows we would try to stop it, and make sure that we never can. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qv6UVOQ0F44",2],["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"5RpPTRcz1no":{"wy":"HN commenters share it during DOGE and government-cuts threads as a summary of the billionaires' aims, though many only cite it as a reference.","q":{"x":"This video breaks down who those people are as well as what their motivations are","i":43041044,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42911000,"author":"keernan","month":"2025-02","story":"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Billionaires' own words on their plans","quote":"Please, please everyone watch the incredibly disturbing video I link below.","text":"Please, please everyone watch the incredibly disturbing video I link below. The video was created on Nov 14, right after the election. It is about the plans of 6 billionaires (Peter Thiel, Elon Musk, Marc Andressen, Ben Horotwitz, Brian Armstrong, and David Sacks) who refer to themselves as Dark Gothic MAGA. The video regurgitates the words of these 6 men. Don't take anyone's word for it. Listen to their own words about their plans. Here is what billionaire Marc Cuban says in the video: >“… what’s happening in Silicon Valley is insane. It’s not so much a support thing. It’s more like a takeover thing. Trying to put themselves in a position to have as much control as possible. They want Trump to be the CEO of the United States of America and they want to be the Board of Directors that makes him listen to them.” Link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43220153,"author":"rwyinuse","month":"2025-03","story":"A DOGE staffer appears to be posting DOGE work on his public GitHub","stance":"rec","why":"Good summary of the billionaires' goals","quote":"here's a pretty good summary","text":"Yep, here's a pretty good summary: [VIDEO] Indeed I believe that the current administration's end-goal is the collapse of the US as we know it. Thiel and other billionaires need the collapse to create their own cyberpunk states inside current US territory. The remaining territory will be probably taken by Project 2025 guys, who want to rule it as a Christian theocracy / far-right utopia."},{"id":42896221,"author":"metamet","month":"2025-02","story":"The government information crisis is bigger than you think it is","stance":"rec","why":"Succinct summary of the billionaires' plan","quote":"This video is probably the most succinct summary of it I've seen","text":"Musk et al.'s plan has been out in the open for a while. Lots of interviews and opinions from them the last few years, so I wouldn't even consider it an open secret. This video is probably the most succinct summary of it I've seen: [VIDEO] It was made two months ago and has been right on the nose so far in terms of the phases in their process."},{"id":42923402,"author":"khold_stare","month":"2025-02","story":"The young, inexperienced engineers aiding DOGE","stance":"rec","why":"Predicted the government-dismantling steps months earlier","quote":"Watch the whole video (posted months ago predicting all these actions)","text":"This will sound like a conspiracy theory, but this is the playbook of Curtis Yarvin, specifically the \"RAGE\" step - Retire All Government Employees. Some references: Watch the whole video (posted months ago predicting all these actions), but here is the relevant section: [VIDEO] NYT interview: [LINK] Gil Duran did a lot of the reporting on this. [LINK]"},{"id":42941469,"author":"Hikikomori","month":"2025-02","story":"What's happening inside the NIH and NSF","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the figures talking about their ideas and plans","quote":"Video with similar content, shows them actually talking about their ideas and plans","text":"I'm convinced that these people and in particular Jarvin read Snow Crash and want to make it reality. Video with similar content, shows them actually talking about their ideas and plans: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42943406,"author":"CamperBob2","month":"2025-02","story":"We need network societies, not network states (2024)","stance":"rec","why":"Perspective on Balaji Srinivasan within a web of influence","quote":"offers an interesting perspective on Balaji Srinivasan and his place in a larger web of influence","text":"I love articles that give me a magic sentence right at the front that tells me I don't need to read any more I wish this were a laughing matter, but it's not. These people appear to be quite serious and quite capable. There's a coalition in play that includes the Project 2025 authors among others, operating at a scope that a lot of people don't fully appreciate. If this coalition holds together, they may be able to execute what many would consider a rather frightening agenda. Despite its cheesy title, this video ( [VIDEO] ) offers an interesting perspective on Balaji Srinivasan and his place in a larger web of influence alongside people like Yarvin, Musk, and Vance. It's eye-opening. She released it back in November and promptly took a lot of flack from people calling her alarmist and conspiracy-prone, and now it's basically playing out in real time."},{"id":43005380,"author":"leanstartupnoob","month":"2025-02","story":"Teen on Musk's DOGE team graduated from 'The Com'","stance":"rec","why":"Uses clips of tech figures discussing dismantling government","quote":"she plays direct video clips of major tech figures talking about dismantling the government and dividing the world into small nation states.","text":"I would like to see some discussion on the Dark Gothic Maga video (How Tech Billionaires Plan to Destroy America) [VIDEO] On one hand it comes across like a conspiracy video, but on the other hand she plays direct video clips of major tech figures talking about dismantling the government and dividing the world into small nation states. Trump and Musk have also both stated they want to eliminate large amounts of the federal government. I would love to get some perspective from those who have personally interacted with those people over the last 10 years. This video probably falls under what you consider \"activating\", but it seems like we need to have conversations around this type of issue rather than letting rational voices get drowned out by the sea of angry shouting."},{"id":43041044,"author":"rnd0","month":"2025-02","story":"Europe cannot rely on US protection, Pentagon chief tells NATO allies","stance":"rec","why":"Breaks down who the power players are and their motivations","quote":"This video breaks down who those people are as well as what their motivations are","text":"Trump is a puppet. The people pulling his strings, however, should be taken very seriously. This video breaks down who those people are as well as what their motivations are: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43053863,"author":"yxre","month":"2025-02","story":"A Libertarian Island Dream in Honduras Is Now an $11B Nightmare","stance":"rec","why":"Evidence that billionaires aspire to such goals","quote":"it shows a lot of evidence that many tech billionaires are aspiring to that end.","text":"I think this is a pretty common goal. This video is a bit pessimistic, but it shows a lot of evidence that many tech billionaires are aspiring to that end. Most notably Peter Thiel [VIDEO]"},{"id":47506721,"author":"nclin_","month":"2026-03","story":"Country that put backdoors in Cisco routers to spy on world bans foreign routers","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for billionaires feeling oppressed by threats to power","quote":"","text":"Power revels in hypocrisy: Rules protect the in-group but do not bind them, and bind the out-group but do not protect them. It's not just logical, it's affective: There is a real pleasure in domination, and a real fear in any loss of control. It feels good to be strong, to be in control, to be protected but not bound. Domination is hegemony, hegemony is safety. These billionaires genuinely feel themselves to be oppressed if their power is threatened in any way. [1] --- [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44760072,"author":"onepremise","month":"2025-08","story":"Corporation for Public Broadcasting ceasing operations","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Again, it's all part of the plan, which is referred to as the butterfly revolution, by Curtis Yarvin. Leaders that have literally invested in this platform are buying into this nonsense. These guys have polarized the two parties to a point all weaknesses are surfacing. It isn't about democrats vs republicans. It's just working class vs the billionaires. You know the ppl sitting behind Trump at his inauguration. Literally, they want to break apart the US and discredit the constitution. Unless we come together and carve a new narrative that works. These guys may succeed and you can kiss your life, liberty, and pursuit of happiness goodbye, as well bill of rights. Peter Theil, JD Vance, Marc Andreesen, Garry Tan, Srinivasan, and many others, wanting to overthrow democracy and dissolve nation states. This effort is to establish Network States with those that worship them, sycophants and cults. They want to transform the US into an Autocracy. The polarization of the media and political parties is on purpose. They want America to fall. It's not a secret, not a conspiracy theory. It's definitely being rolled out by billionaires. It would be wise for others here to really do your research and understand why we are being polarized to hate each other. Enter the butterfly revolution: 1. Reboot (“full-power start”) Suspend or bypass existing constitutional limits; concentrate absolute sovereignty in one new organization—analogous to Allied occupation powers in post-1945 Japan/Germany. El"},{"id":44704204,"author":"onepremise","month":"2025-07","story":"4k NASA employees opt to leave agency through deferred resignation program","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's all part of the plan, which is referred to as the butterfly revolution, by Curtis Yarvin. Leaders that have literally invested in this platform are buying into this nonsense. Peter Theil, JD Vance, Marc Andreesen, Garry Tan, Srinivasan, and many others, wanting to overthrow democracy and dissolve nation states. This effort is to establish Network States with those that worship them, sycophants and cults. They want to transform the US into an Autocracy. The polarization of the media and political parties is on purpose. They want America to fall. It's not a secret, not a conspiracy theory. It's definitely being rolled out by billionaires. It would be wise for others here to really do your research and understand why we are being polarized to hate each other. Enter butterfly revolution: 1. Reboot (“full-power start”) Suspend or bypass existing constitutional limits; concentrate absolute sovereignty in one new organization—analogous to Allied occupation powers in post-1945 Japan/Germany. Eliminate checks and balances that block rapid change. 2. CEO-Monarch model A single executive (chosen like a corporate CEO) rules; the former president becomes a figurehead “chairman of the board.” Treat the state as a firm run for efficiency, not democratic representation. 3. RAGE strategy “Retire All Government Employees” by mass-firing the civil service and replacing it with loyal appointees. Remove institutional resistance (“the Cathedral”) and ensure obedience. 4. Parallel regime Build"},{"id":42898203,"author":"rokkamokka","month":"2025-02","story":"Reflections on Palantir","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions billionaires' goals of corporation states","quote":"I watched a video recently that briefly mentions Palantir","text":"I watched a video recently that briefly mentions Palantir, though only in passing. I only recall this because the video had some incredibly alarming tidbits about billionaires stating their goals of \"corporation states\" unironically [VIDEO]"},{"id":42904191,"author":"coder4life","month":"2025-02","story":"USDA Inspector General Who Refused to Leave Escorted from Office by Security","stance":"reference","why":"Source on Yarvin and the Butterfly Revolution","quote":"Just learned about that guy Curtis Yarvin","text":"Just learned about that guy Curtis Yarvin and his \"Butterfly Revolution\" today \"DARK GOTHIC MAGA: How Tech Billionaires Plan to Destroy America\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":42905425,"author":"naruhodo","month":"2025-02","story":"CDC data are disappearing","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for tech oligarchs' plans","quote":"","text":"The tech oligarchs want to dismantle democracy, receive a gift of 0.5% of Federal land from Trump and establish their own democracy-free fiefdoms. [1] The social support system in the US is being dismantled and when people can no longer afford to eat, the ensuing riots will provide the necessary trigger to declare martial law and suspend democracy completely. [1] DARK GOTHIC MAGA: How Tech Billionaires Plan to Destroy America [VIDEO]"},{"id":42941270,"author":"Hikikomori","month":"2025-02","story":"The young, inexperienced engineers aiding DOGE","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"[LINK] Numbers are published by U.S. Elections Assistance Commission. Read it if you want. They'll dismantle the federal state alright. But not for the reasons you think or want. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42974907,"author":"dTal","month":"2025-02","story":"AI Startup School with Elon Musk, Satya Nadella, Sam Altman, Andrej Karpathy","stance":"reference","why":"Trending video on Silicon Valley's stance toward democracy","quote":"This video is currently trending","text":"This tacit endorsement by YCombinator of the likes of Elon Musk and Sam Altman must be condemned in the strongest possible terms. Like Sam Altman's abrupt Face-Heel turn from staunch and eloquent Trump critic into gushing bootlicker [0], it is a shocking wakeup call that Silicon Valley monied interests either lack principles entirely, or else adhere to principles fundamentally at odds with liberal democracy [1]. I found myself hesitating to post this, here on news.ycombinator.com. This forum has always discouraged \"politics\". But in the final analysis, you cannot separate politics from everything else. The suffering and death that Donald Trump and Elon Musk are currently in the process of expediting[2] is no less real for being \"political\". And if criticizing YCombinator for endorsing - or at least turning a blind eye to - this death and suffering, is considered \"off topic\", then this will be my last comment on this platform. [0] [LINK] [1] This video is currently trending: [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":42991210,"author":"9283409232","month":"2025-02","story":"Dear Mr. Vice President, Please Take Off Your Apple Watch","stance":"reference","why":"Supporting source on network-state agenda","quote":"","text":"A slew of people including Musk, Vance, Curtis Yarvin, Peter Thiel, Marc Andreessen and a few other VC billionares are trying to break up the US into nation states that they call network-states. Think of Cyberpunk with a heavy dose of Snow Crash. It's a completely authoritarian agenda. While I understand this sounds like conspiracy, Andreessen quite literally has a book called \"The Network State\" and Yarvin has blogged about this extensively. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":42997599,"author":"zapataband2","month":"2025-02","story":"Teen on Musk's DOGE team graduated from 'The Com'","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of long-stated aims","quote":"","text":"Uhh they've been openly talking about dismantling democracy and creating nation-states rules by corporations for literally almost a decade. JD Vance is their man in DC when trump is gone. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43193530,"author":"hjgjhyuhy","month":"2025-02","story":"US Forest Service firings decimate already understaffed agency","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for billionaires' network state aims","quote":"","text":"I doubt the billionaire tech bros now controlling the US mind forest fires, and loss of natural habitats. They want the country to collapse, so they can build their own network states. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5u7euN1HTuU",1],["Kvsvd67XUKw",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["Y9FZ4igNxNA",1],["KdfHVcU8U7U",1]]},"LKJBND_IRdI":{"wy":"Recommended as a convincing counterargument to the widely repeated idea of the uncanny valley.","q":{"x":"here's an amazing video making a convincing argument that \"Uncanny Valley\" isn't a thing","i":25621303,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25621303,"author":"MikeLumos","month":"2021-01","story":"The “Uncanny Valley” and How Artists Have Explored It","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convincing argument that the uncanny valley is not real.","quote":"here's an amazing video making a convincing argument that \"Uncanny Valley\" isn't a thing","text":"As a counterpoint, here's an amazing video making a convincing argument that \"Uncanny Valley\" isn't a thing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29390843,"author":"Errancer","month":"2021-11","story":"Disney Combines CGI with Neural Rendering to Tackle the ‘Uncanny Valley’","stance":"rec","why":"Argues against the existence of the uncanny valley.","quote":"there is an interesting argument to be made against the existence of 'Uncanny Valley'","text":"Slightly off topic but there is an interesting argument to be made against the existence of 'Uncanny Valley' – [VIDEO] ."},{"id":28956652,"author":"kortex","month":"2021-10","story":"Artificial muscles robotic arm with full range of motion [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Argues uncanny valley is an artifact of mixing robot goals.","quote":"The uncanny valley is wrong.","text":"The uncanny valley is wrong. Or rather, it is a result that appears due to the juxtaposition of robots with different goal criteria. Turns out, when you focus just on the devices which try to replicate both human likeness and emotional relate-ability (drop all the WALLe style cute but abstract ones), it's pretty linear based on how well that goal of emotional affect is achieved. [VIDEO]"}]},"SK4ezQrTqFw":{"wy":"HN calls it a favorite startup video because it is packed with candid stories about Facebook, Twitter and Google.","q":{"x":"My favorite, because it's so full of great stories, is the Chris Sacca interview with Jason Calacanis","i":7665375,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7665375,"author":"i4i","month":"2014-04","story":"Ask HN: What are your favorite videos relevant to entrepreneurs or startups? ","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Chris Sacca interview full of great stories","quote":"My favorite, because it's so full of great stories, is the Chris Sacca interview with Jason Calacanis","text":"My favorite, because it's so full of great stories, is the Chris Sacca interview with Jason Calacanis on This Week In Startups. Pt.1 [LINK] Pt.2 [VIDEO]"},{"id":10570165,"author":"DeBraid","month":"2015-11","story":"Marc Andreessen Sold 73 Percent of His Facebook Stock in the Last Two Weeks","stance":"rec","why":"Sacca's stories on Facebook's IPO and Zuckerberg","quote":"Some good stories related to this via Chris Sacca on TWIS","text":"Some good stories related to this via Chris Sacca on TWIS [VIDEO] Essentially Zuckerberg treated the IPO process with contempt, was cavalier and unorthodox, offending many Wall Streeters. 'The Hoodie' legend exploded during this period, as did perception that Facebook was hopeless on mobile AND had poor leadership. Both turned out to be false, as FB post-2012 found a way to monetize mobile and Zuckerberg grew into his role as CEO, now telling jokes in Chinese and managing the press more effectively."},{"id":12420999,"author":"jasode","month":"2016-09","story":"Twitter Is Finally Starting to Wake Up to Reality and Consider Selling Itself","stance":"reference","why":"Sacca's theory on why Google wouldn't want Twitter","quote":"Chris Sacca has an interesting theory on why Larry wouldn't be that interested in Twitter.","text":">For Google, it would be buying an enormous database of personal information, For armchair quarterbacks like us, it's hard for us to mindread Larry Page's ultimate plans. That said, Chris Sacca has an interesting theory on why Larry wouldn't be that interested in Twitter.[1] Chris isn't just a random talking head. He worked at Google to buy datacenters for Larry Page during its explosve growth stage and is also one of Twitter's biggest outside shareholders. He's now a billionaire angel investor. His comments are from 2012 but I believe it's still relevant 4 years later. The tl;dw: Twitter isn't an interesting \"science problem\" for Google to solve. I haven't studied the list of Google's acquisitions extensively but it doesn't seem like they have a pattern of acquiring companies for the databases of user accounts. [1] relevant excerpt is 4m45ss - 10m51s : [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["6VOQnK7O2To",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["ZTC_RxWN_xo",1]]},"dngqR9gcDDw":{"wy":"Commenters say it made market economics click for them and find it an interesting documentary relevant to welfare and UBI debates.","q":{"x":"What really made it click was Milton Friedman's","i":34209667,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34209667,"author":"olalonde","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Concepts that clicked only years after you first encountered them?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made market economics click for the commenter","quote":"What really made it click was Milton Friedman's","text":"It took me a long time to grasp market economics. I knew it worked somehow but I didn't quite understand why. What really made it click was Milton Friedman's \"Free to Choose\" TV series[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41402877,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2024-08","story":"Judge rules $400M algorithmic system illegally denied Medicaid benefits","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting documentary related to UBI and welfare bureaucracy","quote":"to be really interesting","text":"That is also a fantastic comparison point. The same ideal is part of the idea behind UBI replacing the social welfare bureaucracy. I found this documentary \"Freedom to choose\" to be really interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37237450,"author":"mattanimation","month":"2023-08","story":"Thomas Sowell's greatest insight – Konstantin Kisin [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Where commenter first encountered Sowell debating","quote":"I first heard him debate when watching Milton Friedman's","text":"\"There are no solutions, only trade-offs\" 100% based Sowell. I first heard him debate when watching Milton Friedman's \"Free to Choose\" series (found here: [VIDEO] ) and immediately got his books."}],"r":[["kZA9Hnp3aV4",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["iHzzSao6ypE",1],["hFL6qRIJZ_Y",1]]},"er_lLvkklsk":{"wy":"HN people point to it as an amazing demonstration of relational programming and program synthesis that still looks exciting after LLM code generation.","q":{"x":"I work on miniKanren, which is a logic programming language with a complete search that doesn't hit the nontermination issues you mention.","i":14047984,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":27363457,"author":"kanzenryu2","month":"2021-06","story":"William Byrd on logic and relational programming, miniKanren (2014)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing Barliman talk with a standout moment","quote":"You might enjoy this amazing talk.","text":"You might enjoy this amazing talk. I've linked to a part when the next 20 seconds of explanation turns the amazing up to 11. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39183350,"author":"thuuuomas","month":"2024-01","story":"A Tour of the Lisps","stance":"rec","why":"Barliman demo of synthesis still exciting","quote":"the Barliman demo is still pretty exciting even after LLM codegen.","text":"Have you checked out any of the minikanren logic programming environments people have implemented in scheme? The Reasoned Schemer is an accessible introduction to that space if you’re not put off by the socratic-themed dialogue. & the Barliman demo is still pretty exciting even after LLM codegen. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27363028,"author":"will_byrd","month":"2021-06","story":"William Byrd on logic and relational programming, miniKanren (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Barliman talk on relational program synthesis","quote":"The Clojure/conj 2016 talk Greg Rosenblatt and I gave on Barliman","text":"For anyone who finds this topic interesting, you might be interested in the following resources: [LINK] The second edition of `The Reasoned Schemer` (MIT Press, 2018): [LINK] The Clojure/conj 2016 talk Greg Rosenblatt and I gave on Barliman: [VIDEO] `A Unified Approach to Solving Seven Programming Problems (Functional Pearl)`, William E. Byrd, Michael Ballantyne, Gregory Rosenblatt, Matthew Might (ICFP 2017) (Open Access): [LINK] and the related talk: [LINK] and Nada Amin's interactive version of the paper: [LINK] Cheers, --Will"},{"id":14047984,"author":"mballantyne","month":"2017-04","story":"The Power of Prolog","stance":"reference","why":"Program synthesis with miniKanren's complete search","quote":"I work on miniKanren, which is a logic programming language with a complete search that doesn't hit the nontermination issues you mention.","text":"Other search algorithms can take up lots of memory to store progress they've made down different paths. Having only one active state also lets you map unification down to efficient low level cpu operations. If you have multiple states, you either have to copy lots of data when you fork the state or you use a persistent data structure but can't use side effects, so everything is a bit slower. It's a tradeoff though. I work on miniKanren, which is a logic programming language with a complete search that doesn't hit the nontermination issues you mention. The complete search lets us do some pretty cool stuff with program synthesis (though we're not about to put any programmers out of business just yet): [VIDEO] Note that you can implement iterative deepening depth first search on top of prolog if you want a complete search there. Iterative deepening takes a little more time but avoids the memory problems with breath-first. SWI has tools built in to help there: [LINK] And I believe Ciao implements its iterative deepening library with a meta-interpreter: [LINK]"}]},"y5UT04p5f7U":{"wy":"Pointed to as the canonical concise case against pennies whenever coin abolition comes up.","q":{"x":"CGP Grey has an excellent youtube video on the whole issue for the US.","i":3774123,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":23832172,"author":"tacocataco","month":"2020-07","story":"Is It Time to Kill the Penny?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wonderful video on the penny.","quote":"CGP Grey has a wonderful video on the penny, he is my favorite YouTuber.","text":"All I want is for all paper cash to be plastic so I can wash the covid off of it. I support getting rid of the penny, but I feel that plastic cash would make more of a difference. CGP Grey has a wonderful video on the penny, he is my favorite YouTuber. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3774123,"author":"rogerbinns","month":"2012-03","story":"Canada ditches the penny","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the case for abolishing the US penny.","quote":"CGP Grey has an excellent youtube video on the whole issue for the US.","text":"CGP Grey has an excellent youtube video on the whole issue for the US. The US currently makes 4 billion 1 cent coins annually. And they have already been abolished at US military overseas bases. [VIDEO] (Come to think of it, doesn't the US military use a lot more metric system than the general population. If so, this would be a good reason to increase the military since it would appear to be the only way progress happens!)"},{"id":36125098,"author":"TehShrike","month":"2023-05","story":"Paper Money Diehards Refuse to Fold","stance":"reference","why":"Obligatory CGP Grey on abolishing small coins.","quote":"Obligatory CGP Grey","text":"This already happens with cents. Your tax frequently comes out to a fraction of a cent, and it gets rounded to the nearest cent. The US used to have a half-penny coin. It stopped being minted in 1857. The purchasing power of the half-penny at the time was about as much as a quarter has today. It wouldn't be crazy to get rid of all coins worth less than 25¢ and round all purchases to the nearest quarter. Obligatory CGP Grey: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17427357,"author":"slavik81","month":"2018-06","story":"The US Mint lost $69M making pennies last year","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the penny's history and abolition.","quote":"","text":"They've done some of that in the past. The United States had a half-cent coin until 1857. When it was coin was eliminated, it was more valuable than the penny is today[1]. There is also a US $1 coin. They were widely used in Equador when I visited in 2005, because their currency was pegged to the US dollar. I spent a day in Miami on my way back home and I left a few as tips. People were very confused by them. It seemed kind of funny to me that a US coin was more commonly used outside the US than inside. [1] See CPGrey's \"Death to Pennies\". [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HfHgUu_8KgA",1],["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["RfdZTZQvuCo",1],["nBNTAmLRmUg",1]]},"-QMHOsfjHq0":{"wy":"Cited in threads on social media and online identity as a framework of sincerity, authenticity and profilicity.","q":{"x":"it is interesting to see some meta perspective on identity creation","i":33573237,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32906910,"author":"ever1337","month":"2022-09","story":"The tyranny of the supertweeter","stance":"rec","why":"Framework of sincerity, authenticity and profilicity applied to social media.","quote":"has a youtube channel with some good videos on the topics","text":"Professor Hans Georg-Moeller of the University of Macau I think has some really good observations and a systematization of what he calls the \"profilicity mode of identity production\", as contrasted to \"authenticity\" and \"sincerity\". He frequently comments on how social media causes people to view their actions not directly, but on a \"second order\", through the eyes of the \"general peer\" as personified by the quantified likes and comments posts get on social media. I haven't done a great job summarizing here but he has a youtube channel with some good videos on the topics: [VIDEO] [LINK] I think his framework helps a lot in making sense of the way people act online and offline in our current age, and the implications for \"identity production\" are quite interesting."},{"id":27455365,"author":"noobermin","month":"2021-06","story":"Don't create for an imaginary group of people","stance":"rec","why":"Philosophy lecture on authenticity versus curated online profiles.","quote":"of a philosophy teacher who argues that we've entered a new era of","text":"This reminds me of this video[0] of a philosophy teacher who argues that we've entered a new era of \"identity\" where identities are based on curated profiles vs. what it used to be, around so-called \"authenticity.\" One of the aspects of that is our identities are curated now to appeal to the \"average online follower\" (he calls it the \"general peer\") which is an abstraction of the \"typical\" person we cater to to get engagement (likes, follows, etc). [0] Forgot video: [VIDEO] Timestamp when he talks about the general peer [VIDEO]"},{"id":33573237,"author":"gala8y","month":"2022-11","story":"The Digital Sensorium (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Meta perspective on identity shifting from sincerity to profilicity.","quote":"it is interesting to see some meta perspective on identity creation","text":"For starters, re: first story (\"subject: Exoskeletal unease\"), it is interesting to see some meta perspective on identity creation [0] as it shifts from sincerity, through authenticity, to profilicity. Let us just not forget that we can still take a walk and look real person into the eye. [0]. \"The Philosophy Of Identity\" - [VIDEO]"}]},"1Nh_vxpycEA":{"wy":"HN links it as a popular, accessible introduction to indoor CO2 and its effect on thinking, in air quality threads.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott did a great video on it a while back","i":23156286,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23156286,"author":"wh-uws","month":"2020-05","story":"Rising CO2 levels may trigger cognitive impairment, says new study","stance":"rec","why":"Shows CO2 effects on cognition.","quote":"Tom Scott did a great video on it a while back","text":"This one of those things that sounds silly but is actually very easy to test and see it can be a real issue. Tom Scott did a great video on it a while back [VIDEO] Do yourself a favor and get an air quality monitor like an awair [LINK] and monitor the levels. Making sure they are good in whatever indoor areas you spend a good amount of time in is a night and day difference cognitively."},{"id":18997910,"author":"yoodenvranx","month":"2019-01","story":"Show HN: I wanted to measure CO2 in my office, so I build a C++ WebServer/App","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point for researching CO2 effects.","quote":"it's a good palce to start research","text":"For those who are wondering about 'changed my life': Tom Scott uploaded a video about CO2 and its influences a few days ago. it's a good palce to start research: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44827329,"author":"jackcurran","month":"2025-08","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"here are some cool links that I've heard people say completely changed their thinking about air quality","text":"Here is our Kickstarter Project Link. We'd love your support! My best friend and I have spent the past year trying to track the environment in our NYC apartments, cafés, and subway cars. We tried the usual handheld monitors and bulky bricks, but none were truly pocket‑friendly, user friendly, and most importantly, they only tracked a single metric, and poorly at that. So we built our own. Made it a powerhouse of multiple sensors. And miniatured it to fit in your pocket when you want to put it away. Our device (named Halo Air after our favorite game, Halo 3) snaps to the back of any smartphone and streams CO₂, PM1/2.5/10, t‑VOCs, temp, and humidity into our app, a lockscreen widget, and we'll be making the data feed available too. No disposable batteries, no calibration stickers—just one USB‑C charge and go. We just hit Kickstarter yesterday. If you’re curious, we’d love your feedback (good, bad, brutal) and—if it resonates—your support as early backers. The link’s in the first comment to avoid spamming the post. Huge thanks to Reddit for the inspiration threads on sensor accuracy, cross‑calibration, and DIY enclosures; your collective brainpower shaped Halo Air more than you know. If you're interested in the area, here are some cool links that I've heard people say completely changed their thinking about air quality: This Is Your Brain On Stale Air (Youtube) [VIDEO] Elon's X thread about his CO2 Monitor: [LINK] Diary of a CEO: The Air In Your Room Is Making You Stupid & Harmi"},{"id":34572906,"author":"M3L0NM4N","month":"2023-01","story":"AirGradient Open Source Air Quality Monitor for CO2 and PM2.5 Measurements","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Higher levels of CO2 result in less brain function, as shown by this Tom Scott video","text":"I will note though, CO2 measure indoors can be very valuable. Higher levels of CO2 result in less brain function, as shown by this Tom Scott video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33583447,"author":"jobigoud","month":"2022-11","story":"Analyzing Carbon Dioxide levels while attending IETF-115 in London, UK","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It was featured in a Tom Scott video","text":"Kurtis Baute tried to seal himself in a greenhouse with plants and that wasn't enough to compensate the exhaling. It was featured in a Tom Scott video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32056858,"author":"thamer","month":"2022-07","story":"Whose breath are you breathing?","stance":"reference","why":"Subject in airtight chamber shows brain function impact as CO2 rises.","quote":"For a more pop-sci take on this, see this Tom Scott video","text":"Covid aside, a reading of almost 6000 ppm in the bus is extremely high, at this level there is a clear impact on cognitive function. See this study[1] in Nature that measured the effects of CO2 at 1000, 3000, and 5000 PPM. Quote: \"For the Psychomotor Vigilance Test, the probability of achieving an accuracy score of >90% decreased in a dose–response like fashion from 79.5%, 74.7%, 73.4%, to 64.0% for 600, 1200, 2400, and 5000 ppm, respectively\". Another study[2] tried to establish the effect on response time, finding that \"For every 500ppm increase, we saw response times 1.4-1.8% slower, and 2.1-2.4% lower throughput.\" For a more pop-sci take on this, see this Tom Scott video where a subject is placed inside of an airtight chamber where the CO2 gradually increases, with a major impact on their brain function: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":22637886,"author":"derefr","month":"2020-03","story":"Cabin Fever","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I’ve lived in a lot of places in my life, and spent at least some time in seclusion in each one. And I have experienced the “cabin fever” feeling before... but not everywhere I’ve lived. In some environments, it happened easily and immediately; in others, it never did. I’ve tried to think about my environment at each juncture to figure out if the situations where I did experience “cabin fever” had anything in common. Every time I experienced the symptoms described in the article (restlessness, lightheadedness, insomnia, irritability, etc.) I was living in a modern, rather-well-sealed apartment building; or was on a long road-trip in a modern, rather-well-sealed car; or was on a long plane flight; or was staying in a modern hotel room in a high-rise; or I was camping in a tent or camper-trailer. Meanwhile, I’ve never experienced these symptoms while living in an old, drafty brick building (repurposed office building); or while living in a Victorian-era farmhouse; or while staying in a low-rent motel; or while camping under the stars. My conclusion is that “cabin fever” is a feeling you get when your living space is not well-ventilated. Specifically, when there’s no way to create a through-draft of air, so even opening windows won’t force out the air deeper in the home—leading to that air becoming stale, creating a built-up “cloud” of CO2, other gaseous bodily wastes, and exhaled aerosolized water droplets (you know, those things that viruses travel on.) It happens faster when "},{"id":21570080,"author":"techiferous","month":"2019-11","story":"The cognitive costs of air pollution","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"After watching this Tom Scott video","text":"After watching this Tom Scott video [VIDEO] I bought this CO2 monitor: [LINK] It worked quite well and showed that I don't have anything to worry about. Now I'm trying to pass it on to someone else. I'll be willing to sell it for half price if anyone is interested."},{"id":21660951,"author":"darkmighty","month":"2019-11","story":"Rooms can be as bright as the outdoors","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Obligatory Tom Scott video on indoor CO2 levels","text":"Obligatory Tom Scott video on indoor CO2 levels: [VIDEO] (or the studies themselves [LINK] and others) I.e. I think air quality is likely a big factor. When it's windy and I get good airflow through my room it doesn't feel as different from outside. It's probably not only CO2, but also dust, VOCs, funghi, microorganisms, etc."},{"id":18963733,"author":"yoodenvranx","month":"2019-01","story":"On whether changes in bedroom CO2 levels affect sleep quality","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott video on CO2 and its influence.","quote":"Tom Scott uploaded a video about CO2 and its influences","text":"Is it just coincidence that Tom Scott uploaded a video about CO2 and its influences at almost the exact time the gwerns link was submitted? [VIDEO]"},{"id":18978240,"author":"digitalsushi","month":"2019-01","story":"Researchers develop a new houseplant that can clean home air (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Person sits in a sealed cube with plants and CO2 monitors.","quote":"wherein a person sits in a sealed off cube full of plants and carbon dioxide monitors","text":"Floating around this week is this video [VIDEO] wherein a person sits in a sealed off cube full of plants and carbon dioxide monitors, with an EMT on the other side for safety - the person claims that 300 ppm of outside air was fresh, and now 400 ppm is fresh - but that indoors, it can easily climb to over 1000ppm and that there might be a significant cognitive decline associated with it. I was looking for a nice wall receptacle for a hallways that measures carbon dioxide inside my house because I will go for weeks at a time without getting a proper air-out. In northern new england, honestly, it could be months for all I know. Only a few random door openings and the leakiness of the house are my exchange. I don't know how accurate their claim is, and it's certainly a universal solvent to consider stale air is the reason a person is behaving a way. Measuring the levels and being aware of it seems worth 60 bucks, if it's actually a factor in our rationality."},{"id":18997546,"author":"bemmu","month":"2019-01","story":"Show HN: I wanted to measure CO2 in my office, so I build a C++ WebServer/App","stance":"reference","why":"Cites a cognitive decline figure for 1000ppm CO2.","quote":"Higher ppm decreases cognitive function even at pretty low levels","text":"Higher ppm decreases cognitive function even at pretty low levels, so being alerted to open your window might improve your work / study. There's at least this study: [LINK] And this video has the quote \"At 1000ppm there is a 15% decrease in cognitive function\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":18997688,"author":"stackmad","month":"2019-01","story":"Show HN: I wanted to measure CO2 in my office, so I build a C++ WebServer/App","stance":"reference","why":"Body poorly senses elevated CO2.","quote":"the body is pretty bad at recognizing higher than norm CO2 level unless it gets to life threatening levels","text":"Its not that I'm afraid to die here :) Its more about improving the quality of the air. And the body is pretty bad at recognizing higher than norm CO2 level unless it gets to life threatening levels. ( [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["oYp5XuGYqqY",1],["wt-iVFxgFWk",1],["2o8MDCIlOEk",1]]},"9XkHBmE-N34":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear explanation of how information may be encoded on a black hole's surface.","q":{"x":"There's a great PBS Space Time youtube video on this concept","i":18241616,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":27226721,"author":"neom","month":"2021-05","story":"Physicists identify the engine powering black hole energy beams","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a PBS Space Time black hole series","quote":"PBS Space Time to the rescue","text":"PBS Space Time to the rescue: What Happens at the Event Horizon? - [LINK] What’s On The Other Side Of A Black Hole? - [LINK] How Time Becomes Space Inside a Black Hole - [LINK] The Black Hole Information Paradox - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18241616,"author":"ncallaway","month":"2018-10","story":"Are Black Holes Actually Dark Energy Stars?","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of information on black hole surfaces","quote":"There's a great PBS Space Time youtube video on this concept","text":"By my understanding of the theory, yes (according to some unproven theories). The information can be imprinted on the surface of a black hole (and, later are dissipated by imprinting on Hawking Radiation). This is also the principle that led to our theoretical understanding that the maximum amount of information that can be contained in a volume is bounded by the _surface area_ of the volume. This is the Holopgraphic Principle: [LINK] . There's a great PBS Space Time youtube video on this concept, which explains it far better and more accurately that I ever could: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21581106,"author":"aurelian15","month":"2019-11","story":"Hologram Within a Hologram Hints at Solution to Black Hole Information Paradox","stance":"reference","why":"General episode on the black hole information paradox","quote":"Related PBS Space Time episode","text":"Related PBS Space Time episode about the \"Black Hole Information Paradox\" (in general, not about the particular paper discussed in the article): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["OkmNXy7er84",1],["2JAOTJxYqh8",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["AZX6awZq5Z0",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["mht-1c4wc0Q",1],["xRel1JKOEbI",1],["umatbZ0n4mE",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["G0Q4UAiKacw",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["KePNhUJ2reI",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1]]},"9ZX_XCYokQo":{"wy":"Recommended as a definitive Gould interpretation whose playing makes Bach's counterpoint clear.","q":{"x":"Have a look/listen at Glenn Gould's version, which makes the counterpoint really clear [1].","i":18900762,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":18900762,"author":"ternaryoperator","month":"2019-01","story":"Debussy’s Radical Search for Simplicity","stance":"rec","why":"Gould's interpretation makes the counterpoint clear","quote":"Have a look/listen at Glenn Gould's version, which makes the counterpoint really clear [1].","text":"I find Osetinskaya plays so fast that it's difficult to make out the counterpoint. Have a look/listen at Glenn Gould's version, which makes the counterpoint really clear [1]. As you might know, this interpretation was controversial at the time. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16556220,"author":"siglesias","month":"2018-03","story":"Can any composer equal Bach?","stance":"rec","why":"Seminal Gould performance of the D minor concerto under Bernstein","quote":"it's hard to top the interpretations of Glenn Gould","text":"If anyone is interested in Bach's keyboard literature, it's hard to top the interpretations of Glenn Gould, a fascinating musician in his own right. Here, for example, is a seminal performance of the D minor concerto, conducted by Leonard Bernstein: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39432973,"author":"swannodette","month":"2024-02","story":"Bach’s Cantata Calendar","stance":"reference","why":"Keyboard concerto reworked from earlier cantatas","quote":"The profound Harpsichord Concerto in D minor BWV 1052,","text":"I felt that way at first, but after getting around to listening to a few of them you start to realize he repurposed a lot his favorite material from these cantatas into his instrumental works. For example, one of Bach's innovations was the keyboard concerto - where the keyboard played the central solo role. The profound Harpsichord Concerto in D minor BWV 1052, [VIDEO] , is actually reworked from the cantatas BWV 146 & 148. While there's obviously plenty to enjoy w/o dipping into the cantatas, you might miss out on making these kinds of connections which I think really enriches our understanding of the instrumental works."}]},"Ai2HmvAXcU0":{"wy":"HN links it constantly as vivid evidence of how much margin aircraft structures have and that destructive testing is real.","q":{"x":"you might find this really interesting","i":17656210,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17656210,"author":"sjm-lbm","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: How to overcome fear of flying?","stance":"rec","why":"777 wing snaps at far more flex than turbulence causes.","quote":"you might find this really interesting","text":"Since you are posting on HN and I'm guessing you are technically minded, you might find this really interesting: [VIDEO] - it's a video from a documentary about the development of the Boeing 777. Specifically, the video shows a test where they bend/stress the 777 wings to the point where they actually snap. Spoiler alert: they snap at orders of magnitude more flex than you've ever seen. Even for very severe turbulence (like you experienced), it's very comforting for me to know (via that video evidence) that even if I haven't seen turbulence this bad, the people that made the airplane definitely thought about it."},{"id":9085185,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2015-02","story":"The Last of the Typewriter Men","stance":"rec","why":"Modern aircraft structural engineering shown in wing test.","quote":"Check out this video","text":"Planes come to mind. Basically, back in the age of the slide ruler, engineers \"overcompensated\" in durability. This is why older planes are much heavier (so more inefficient and costly to run) but also last forever. PS: Not to crap on the structural engineering of more modern planes, don't get me wrong! Check out this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34620903,"author":"mulmen","month":"2023-02","story":"Easter egg in flight path of last 747 delivery flight","stance":"reference","why":"Famous 777 wing test video.","quote":"The famous video is of a 777 wing.","text":"The famous video is of a 777 wing. I don't believe any similar footage exists for the 747 or if the test was even performed decades before. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21659625,"author":"jvanderbot","month":"2019-11","story":"Boeing 777X’s fuselage split dramatically during September stress test","stance":"reference","why":"Wing failure test broke slightly beyond design target.","quote":"Have you seen the wing failure test?","text":"This sounds fine. It's a catastrophic failure test. They push until it breaks, then it broke, and they were very close to their designed target (within 1%). If they had gone 1% past their designed target, they would have been upset, as they could have made the plane lighter. They were 1% short of design limit, they are upset because they'll have to add reinforcement. Have you seen the wing failure test? I worked for the company that did these tests (they did them before I worked there), and they still talk about how upset the engineers were that it went over spec in strength. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21662594,"author":"zuppy","month":"2019-11","story":"Boeing 777X’s fuselage split dramatically during September stress test","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how wings are tested until they break.","quote":"look how the wing was tested","text":"there is no way to fail gracefully, because what this test does is to test the breaking point. when there is such a stress, there's no middle ground. for example, look how the wing was tested: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20901209,"author":"cyberferret","month":"2019-09","story":"Door blows off Boeing 777X during stress test","stance":"reference","why":"777 wing test breaking at 154% of load.","quote":"Here is another video of a 777 wing test where it broke at 154%","text":"It's a stress test. You would expect things to break during a stress test. > \"They loaded it up well beyond capacity and bent its wings in an extreme manner, in a way almost certain to never happen in the real world.\" Here is another video of a 777 wing test where it broke at 154% of the [s/normal/maximum theoretical/] load - a bit beyond what was expected by the engineering team. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20902687,"author":"mkl","month":"2019-09","story":"Door blows off Boeing 777X during stress test","stance":"reference","why":"Source for 154% meaning 1.54 times the limit.","quote":"so it's 1.54 times the design limit.","text":"It's not a matter of interpretation or tricky language. The video says \"of\" [1] so it's 1.54 times the design limit. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19826771,"author":"cesarb","month":"2019-05","story":"Boeing’s Own Test Pilots Lacked Key Details of 737 Max Flight-Control System","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that destructive aircraft testing exists.","quote":"Actually, there is","text":"> There is no such thing as 'destructive aircraft testing' Actually, there is: [VIDEO] \"This Boeing 777 wing was tested to destruction, finally breaking at one fifty four percent of the designed limit load.\""},{"id":8407176,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2014-10","story":"Aboard a Cargo Colossus: Maersk’s New Container Ships","stance":"reference","why":"777 wing tested to destruction at 154% limit load.","quote":"This Boeing 777 wing was tested to destruction, finally breaking at one fifty four percent (154%) of the designed limit load.","text":"One of the wing tests: [VIDEO] \"This Boeing 777 wing was tested to destruction, finally breaking at one fifty four percent (154%) of the designed limit load.\""},{"id":6004160,"author":"beachstartup","month":"2013-07","story":"Video shows plane's moment of impact at SFO","stance":"reference","why":"777 wings deflected with cables in stress test.","quote":"video of boeing stress-testing 777 wings by deflecting them up with cables","text":"[VIDEO] video of boeing stress-testing 777 wings by deflecting them up with cables, against a stationary fuselage first thing that came to mind after seeing the video of the crashed plane do a cartwheel on its wing."}],"r":[["3-son3EJTrU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["HxSHIpEQRjs",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1]]},"GZadCj8O1-0":{"wy":"HN links it in piracy and copyright-lawsuit threads as a funny short demonstration of how inflated industry damage figures are.","q":{"x":"Rob Reid did a funny 5min TED Talk on this","i":16678736,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":34697698,"author":"omoikane","month":"2023-02","story":"Getty Images Is Suing Stable Diffusion for a Staggering $1.8T","stance":"rec","why":"Funny TED talk on copyright math","quote":"It's a funny TED talk on","text":"Related: \"The 8 billion iPod\" by Rob Reid - [VIDEO] It's a funny TED talk on \"copyright math\"."},{"id":16678736,"author":"DavidNielsen","month":"2018-03","story":"Kim Dotcom wins battle in ongoing fight against U.S. extradition","stance":"rec","why":"Funny five-minute talk on absurd piracy damage figures","quote":"Rob Reid did a funny 5min TED Talk on this","text":"Rob Reid did a funny 5min TED Talk on this called the 8 Billion dollar iPod: [VIDEO] He used the same premise when he wrote the comedy sci-fi book Year Zero, the story of naive aliens accidentally pirating all of Earths music and the legal consequences thereof, i.e. owning the citizens of Earth literally all the money in the universe leading to the ruination of their delightfully hedonistic utopia. A quick and hilarious read."},{"id":46802406,"author":"ShroudedNight","month":"2026-01","story":"Lennart Poettering, Christian Brauner founded a new company","stance":"reference","why":"Explains inflated industry piracy loss claims","quote":"","text":"Also worth remembering that around 2010, the music and film industry associations of America were claiming entitlement to $50 billion dollars annually in piracy-related losses beyond what could be accounted for in direct lost revenue (which _might_ have been as much as 10 billion, or 1/6th of their claim): [VIDEO] These guys pathologically have had a chip on their shoulder since Napster."},{"id":39224215,"author":"turquoisevar","month":"2024-02","story":"Gary Bowser and gaming's most infamous piracy case","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the fantasy accounting in piracy cases","quote":"goes into the fantasy accounting in cases such as this","text":"Always reminds me of this TED talk[1] that goes into the fantasy accounting in cases such as this. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4173503,"author":"Selfcommit","month":"2012-06","story":"Mega-victory: Kim Dotcom search warrants “invalid,” mansion raid “illegal”","stance":"reference","why":"Rob Reid's TED talk on copyright math","quote":"","text":"The affiliate program was for people pushing content, not specifically copyrighted content. Mega made money on site visits and account premiums This is no different then Apple making a fortune off the ipod. The original iPod did not have a great store attached to it, it was simply a device for playing MP3s. Wonder where all those MP3s came from? Holding Mega accountable for the users actions is like saying Apple should pay the record companies for songs that were pirated on the ipods they sold, and continue to sell. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12136436,"author":"LeonM","month":"2016-07","story":"Alleged founder of world’s largest BitTorrent distribution site arrested","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I can't keep myself from giggling and thinking about this","text":"> “Vaulin is charged with running today’s most visited illegal file-sharing website, responsible for unlawfully distributing well over $1 billion of copyrighted materials,” Assistant Attorney General Caldwell said in the statement. I can't keep myself from giggling and thinking about this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["iRkZcTg1JWU",1]]},"SqayDnQ2wmw":{"wy":"Offered as an entry point into Aphex Twin and IDM, and as a striking audiovisual piece in demoscene and neural-net visual discussions.","q":{"x":"check out the music video for T69 Collapse by Aphex Twin (photosensitive epillepsy warning)","i":37928444,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37928444,"author":"Cthulhu_","month":"2023-10","story":"What is the Demoscene? An obscure but influential art form","stance":"rec","why":"Audiovisual art music video worth checking out, with photosensitivity warning.","quote":"check out the music video for T69 Collapse by Aphex Twin (photosensitive epillepsy warning)","text":"Because without the technical challenge element, it's \"just\" audiovisual art, but check out the music video for T69 Collapse by Aphex Twin (photosensitive epillepsy warning): [VIDEO] . More information / interview with the visual artist: [LINK]"},{"id":33531789,"author":"sph","month":"2022-11","story":"How Aphex Twin Made Selected Ambient Works 85-92","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as an entry point into Aphex Twin and IDM.","quote":"These were my ways into the genre: - Aphex Twin - T69 Collapse ( )","text":"I have loved Daft Punk for a long time, but one day I wanted to learn more about Aphex Twin and IDM in general and I must say it's an acquired taste. It's like getting into whiskeys. There are still some tracks that are impenetrable, but I've been enjoy this album and the works of Squarepusher a lot. Squarepusher has such a wide range of sounds it might be a better gateway drug. I love the unapologetic electronic, artificial, synthetic feel of these two artists. These were my ways into the genre: - Aphex Twin - T69 Collapse ( [VIDEO] ) (as someone said \"it's like jacking into cyberspace, but the RAR is corrupted\") - This album - Squarepusher - Terminal Slam ( [LINK] ) - Squarepusher - Hello Everything (album) In general many genres outside of pop are an acquired taste. If they were easy to get into, they would be called pop. You just need to find a way in, even if you decide it's not for you."},{"id":27795363,"author":"j4yav","month":"2021-07","story":"AIeyes: Walking simulator where the world is seen through a realtime neural net","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It reminded me a lot of this Aphex Twin video","text":"It reminded me a lot of this Aphex Twin video: [VIDEO] For now I prefer the human (curated?) one but you could imagine this improving to be really cool."},{"id":22735669,"author":"Cthulhu_","month":"2020-03","story":"A song that was constructed to reduce anxiety","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Great video and music, but anything but anxiety reducing.","text":"Wouldn't put Aphex Twin on as a playlist though, since he also has [VIDEO] . Great video and music, but anything but anxiety reducing."}],"r":[["AtPA6nIBs5g",1],["xUezfuy8Qpc",1],["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["ia8Q51ouA_s",1],["zBgCRJluWTc",1],["jpNU72dny2s",1],["mb3uK-_QkOo",1],["RTB5XhjbgZA",1],["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["gp4U5aH_T6A",1],["8k2AbqTBxao",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["w70Xc9CStoE",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1]]},"nnl6nY8YKHs":{"wy":"Offered as a less technical introduction to Bostrom's simulation paper.","q":{"x":"for a less technical introduction, watch the video of Bostrom talking about it","i":20666739,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20666739,"author":"Veedrac","month":"2019-08","story":"Are We Living in a Computer Simulation? Let’s Not Find Out","stance":"rec","why":"Less technical introduction to Bostrom's simulation paper.","quote":"for a less technical introduction, watch the video of Bostrom talking about it","text":"For people actually interested in the topic rather than painfully bad pop-sci reenactments, you should read the simulation paper[1], or, for a less technical introduction, watch the video of Bostrom talking about it[2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13762885,"author":"darekkay","month":"2017-03","story":"Did the Oscars Just Prove That We Are Living in a Computer Simulation?","stance":"rec","why":"Bostrom interview introducing simulation theory.","quote":"If you're interested in simulation theories, I recommend reading \"The Simulation Argument\" by Nick Bostrom [0] or watching his interview on Youtube [1].","text":"If you're interested in simulation theories, I recommend reading \"The Simulation Argument\" by Nick Bostrom [0] or watching his interview on Youtube [1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11822609,"author":"okket","month":"2016-06","story":"Odds are we're living in a simulation, says Elon Musk","stance":"reference","why":"Bostrom presents the simulation argument.","quote":"The \"Simulation Argument\" by Nick Bostrom","text":"The \"Simulation Argument\" by Nick Bostrom [VIDEO] Even if it is true, it changes nothing. Since this is the world we have to deal with and there is no indication we can influence the (supposed) simulation. If our world/universe is a simulation, it is simply too good. I also recommend to read \"The Big Picture\" by Sean Carrol, chapter 11 \"Is It Okay to Doubt Everything?\" deals with these kinds of thought experiments (including Bostrom). The 2016 Isaac Asimov Memorial Debate was about \"Is the Universe a Simulation?\": [LINK]"}],"r":[["l9ZqXlHl65g",1],["oVfHeWTKjag",1],["pYXy-A4siMw",1],["ctS8FzqcRug",1],["ZeecOKBus3Q",1],["L5pUA3LsEaw",1],["BzhbAK1pdPM",1]]},"E9KJ_f7REGw":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an impressive hobbyist project and as an example of open-source engine control enabling freevalve retrofits.","m":[{"id":35456726,"author":"bmelton","month":"2023-04","story":"“Inside-out Wankel” rotary engine delivers 5X the power of a diesel","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Probably the coolest hobby automotive project in years: freevalve on a Miata.","quote":"","text":"Agreed, but there's also Koenigsegg, which has managed to eliminate the need for cams, and complete heads from ICEs, replacing them with their 'freevalve' tech (which basically replaces the timing chain with computers so that instead of having one or two modes that a camshaft can opt into, you can precisely dial in bespoke timings for every RPM, or every RPM and speed, or ever RPM / speed / gear combination. They've also practically reinvented the transmission to invent a new transmission that eliminates moving gearsets and gives you a dual-clutchish transmission with a 6 speed manual AND a 9 speed automatic in the same device. IIRC, the Freevalve tech is like a 30% efficiency hack for any ICE you put it on, and it's a part of how Koenigsegg are managing to squeeze 600 brake horsepower out of just a 2L, 3-cylinder engine. Wesley Kagan reimplemented their freevalve technology on his Mazda Miata, and it's probably the coolest hobby automotive project I've seen in years. [VIDEO] Here's a really cool explainer on the transmission - [LINK]"},{"id":48754554,"author":"pseudoralph","month":"2026-07","story":"Internal Combustion Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Example of retrofitting an air-actuated valve system onto a Miata.","quote":"","text":"Fiat (and other's I'm sure) have the [MultiAir system]( [LINK] ) which doesn't use cams to actuate valves And here's an example of someone that retrofitted a Miata to use a similar [air-actuated valve system]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":47867961,"author":"tadfisher","month":"2026-04","story":"Alberta Startup Sells No-Tech Tractors for Half Price","stance":"reference","why":"Example of open-source ECU enabling Koenigsegg-style freevalve on a Miata.","quote":"","text":"There's some nuance here. If you care about fuel consumption or emissions, then EFI is the current best way to reduce both, and that requires \"computers and software\" to operate on the timescales required. I put scare quotes around those terms because you can do EFI on an Arduino, which is at least an order of magnitude more powerful than what automakers shipped in the 80s. In any case, EFI gives you more control over the engine and vastly simplifies the overall product. I don't know if you've seen the mechanical fuel-injection pumps used by tractor diesels; they are basically tiny engines unto themselves, with their own little block and camshaft [0]. There is an entire world of diesel performance modding with a subset of it dedicated to modifying the Bosh P1700 mechanical fuel-injection pump to change timings, handle higher RPMs, and run higher pressures. I would not call it, or its carburetor cousin in the gasoline world, \"simple\" compared to computer-controlled fuel delivery. An open-source ECU project, on the other hand, enabled a hacker to implement Koenigsegg's Freevalve tech on a Miata [1]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26999851,"author":"LeonM","month":"2021-04","story":"Internal Combustion Engine","stance":"reference","why":"Hobbyist built own freevalve system and got it running on a Miata.","quote":"","text":"Swedish hypercar builder Koenigsegg has made a system like this, they call it FreeValve. Their upcoming car called the Gemera will feature FreeValve, its 2.0L 4-cylinder is said to generate over 600HP. Though it must be said that this is a 1.5 million dollar car, so don't expect this kind of performance per displacement from other brands. A guy on Youtube made his own version of freevalve, and got it to work on a Mazda Miata: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v_UyVmITiYQ",1],["Erp8IAUouus",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["6bqBsHSwPgw",1]]},"YQZ2UeOTO3I":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference in threads about pharma payments, medical corruption, and declining trust in healthcare.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching it","i":9042590,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9042590,"author":"DigitalSea","month":"2015-02","story":"The FDA buries evidence of fraud in medical trials. My students and I dug it up","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Oliver segment on pharma marketing to doctors.","quote":"I highly recommend watching it","text":"Both honestly and sadly, I think nobody is actually legitimately surprised by this. The pharmaceutical industry is a profit machine that will do anything to ensure that it remains profitable. If anyone hasn't seen John Oliver's latest video, it's on the whole marketing to doctors thing and touches upon the pharmaceutical industry and I highly recommend watching it: [VIDEO] - he sums it up perfectly."},{"id":15048863,"author":"ddebernardy","month":"2017-08","story":"Opioid makers made payments to one in 12 U.S. doctors","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"John Oliver ran a piece on the topic and the industry's many other dubious practices over 2 years ago","text":"Is this really news? John Oliver ran a piece on the topic and the industry's many other dubious practices over 2 years ago, and I'm quite sure he wasn't the first to try to raise awareness. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10597294,"author":"brightball","month":"2015-11","story":"Doctors want ban on prescription drug, device advertisements","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's a part of me that wants to agree completely...and there is another part that has concerns about the messenger. The end result of that would be that the gatekeeper to knowing about these drugs or devices are the doctor's themselves meaning those companies just have to work harder to advertise to the doctors to recommend their drug/device. From Last Week Tonight w/ John Oliver: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9175493,"author":"RaleyField","month":"2015-03","story":"Java Runtime updater now installs ask.com toolbar on Macs","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on healthcare corruption cited for declining trust.","quote":"I'll just leave this relevant episode of","text":"> Anti-vaccine movement comes from declining levels of trust in authorities. I'll just leave this relevant episode of [Last Week Tonight]( [VIDEO] ). While anti-vaxxers are clearly wrong let's acknowledge that health care, at least in the US, has been partially corrupted. The thought that medical workers might be offering unsound advice in this one area when it's known they behaved unethically in that other area suddenly does not appear so unreasonable."},{"id":9081824,"author":"ForHackernews","month":"2015-02","story":"Lenovo's Response to Its Dangerous Adware Is Astonishingly Clueless","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think many sex workers with personal standards would be insulted by your comparison with physicians, who seem to be happy to take money (not your money, I guess) to push drugs that may or may not actually help you[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"d2ogGZXmepY":{"wy":"Cited as a thorough, candid review of the Bolt, particularly for its uncomfortable seats and uncool image.","q":{"x":"Really really great review from Doug DeMuro here","i":15530230,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15530230,"author":"whalesalad","month":"2017-10","story":"Chevy Bolt: the First Practical, Mass-Market Electric Vehicle","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep review of the Bolt's quirks and oddities","quote":"Really really great review from Doug DeMuro here","text":"Note that this is the Bolt not the Volt. Even as a petrol head who owns a V8 and a Subaru STI, I really like what GM has done with this vehicle. Really really great review from Doug DeMuro here: [VIDEO] Doug has a great style - really digs deep into all the quirks and oddities of the cars he reviews."},{"id":19811134,"author":"drewg123","month":"2019-05","story":"Tesla Model 3 Effect – Chevy Dealers Discount 2019 Bolt by Almost $10k","stance":"reference","why":"Reviewer noticed the same narrow, uncomfortable seats","quote":"Doug DeMuro noticed the same thing in his review","text":"I think they did it to avoid competing with themselves. Before we bought our Telsa in 2017, we test drove a Bolt. The sales people tried really hard to steer us toward a Volt (which was more expensive). It seemed clear they were not really interested in selling the Bolt. Speaking of the design: On the drive, we noticed that the seats in the Bolt were narrow and uncomfortable. The odd thing was that they had plenty of room to make the seats a few inches wider. Doug DeMuro noticed the same thing in his review: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17912275,"author":"xedeon","month":"2018-09","story":"Mercedes Readies First Tesla Rival in $12B Attack","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You can walk into a dealership and buy a Chevy Bolt right now because there is plenty of surplus inventory. People are just not buying them for many reasons. [VIDEO] >GM's electric offering was built from the ground up to be an electric car, and has won tons of awards for actually being a good car. The Bolt actually used many of the Chevy Sonic hardware/parts so I would hardly call it being built from the ground up. I mean, they look pretty much the same. It's like the Tesla roadster 1.0 which used Lotus parts."},{"id":15825575,"author":"drewg123","month":"2017-12","story":"GM says it will put fleets of self-driving cars in cities in 2019","stance":"reference","why":"Reviewer also noted the narrow seats","quote":"At least one reviewer agrees","text":"We looked at a Bolt. We mostly liked it, except the seats are incredibly uncomfortable. I'm not even close to obese, and the seat bottoms were just too narrow to be comfortable. It is odd, as there is plenty of space to either side of the seat with the doors closed .. they could have made them wider, but chose not to. At least one reviewer agrees: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15610054,"author":"maxsilver","month":"2017-11","story":"Tesla posts big loss, cuts production of Models X and S to catch up on Model 3","stance":"reference","why":"Reviewer calls it not sexy or cool, otherwise fine","quote":"","text":"> It's a shitty car, and non-electric reviewers have loathed it. It's a perfectly good car, and most non-electric reviewers have said its only major fault is being \"not sexy, not cool\". Doug DeMuro, for example - [VIDEO] > the bolt weighs more than the (short range) model 3. Sure, but only barely . The short-range Model 3 weighs 3,549lbs, and the Bolt weighs 3,563lbs. Your complaining about a difference of 14 pounds (less than 1%). The Bolt also delivers 18 extra driving miles of range, which explains a small portion of the weight difference. > the lead aero guy called it a nightmare Of course he did, they sacrificed aerodynamics for comfort. Some of the big complaints with Chevy's last electric car (Volt) was that it was too low to the ground and too short -- all great things for aerodynamics, but all things that actual humans complained about. So they made the Bolt a proper hatchback -- taller and sit higher -- to make it nicer for the occupants. Chevy, shockingly, listened to their customers and made some changes they requested . The Bolt is perfectly fine car. Obviously it's not as sexy or luxurious as a Model 3. But the Bolt is not in any way a \"shitty car\" -- by most accounts, it's fast, comfortable and reliable. In fact, the Bolt is arguably the best electric car you can actually purchase in the US today -- and likely will be for the next year or so until Tesla solves their production struggles."}],"r":[["giYbq4HmfGA",1],["IxuU5L2MEII",1]]},"hWUiLJnEYJI":{"wy":"Used in techno-history threads as evidence of Kraftwerk's role in the genre's origins and as an example of original work.","q":{"x":"Still one of my favourite clips on youtube: the very first video recording of Kraftwerk live, from 1970!","i":23094980,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23094980,"author":"awiesenhofer","month":"2020-05","story":"Florian Schneider has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"First video recording of Kraftwerk live in 1970, with striking audience reactions.","quote":"Still one of my favourite clips on youtube: the very first video recording of Kraftwerk live, from 1970!","text":"Still one of my favourite clips on youtube: the very first video recording of Kraftwerk live, from 1970! [VIDEO] (Those audience reactions!)"},{"id":49335663,"author":"yencabulator","month":"2026-08","story":"Patterns and problems in emerging multi-agent systems","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as an example of original creative work, not a remix.","quote":"You tell me if you think it is just a remix of something earlier","text":"\"A lot of creative work is remixes\" in no way conflicts with \"That's pretty low-value creativity.\" Hiphop made using samples a fashion . You can think of it as choosing to restrict one's in order to explore something deeper, like a painter deciding to paint with only two pigments to exercise their skills at shading at patterning. Or you can think of it as a cheap way to make demo tracks in your bedroom. If you want to find actually creative things, look outside of mainstream, Disney, etc. I'll leave you with this link. You tell me if you think it is just a remix of something earlier: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35599011,"author":"aa-jv","month":"2023-04","story":"The Battle over Techno’s Origins","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence techno originated with Kraftwerk in Dusseldorf.","quote":"Techno was started in Düsseldorf by Kraftwerk.","text":"Techno was started in Düsseldorf by Kraftwerk. [VIDEO] People found this very inspiring and created their own variants. The Detroit variant came along. The Chicago variant came along. The UK variant came along. But it all goes back to Kraftwerk."},{"id":26979634,"author":"Dowwie","month":"2021-04","story":"History of Techno","stance":"reference","why":"Kraftwerk's first recorded live performance in 1970.","quote":"Kraftwerk's first live recorded performance was in 1970","text":"1978 Kraftwerk live performance of 2 songs: [LINK] Kraftwerk's first live recorded performance was in 1970: [VIDEO] How many people in the 1970 audience sensed that they were listening to something as significant as what it became?"},{"id":17488812,"author":"kowdermeister","month":"2018-07","story":"Keep on moving: the bizarre dance epidemic of summer 1518","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I feel some details might have lost in 500 years :) The council probably liked the decadence of the happening and they added some fuel. It was probably unreal to be at a \"festival\" you never seen before so I can understand. Just like experiencing a techo party for the first time no one seen before. [VIDEO]"}]},"ygB4EZ7ggig":{"wy":"Cited in UFO threads as a short, first-hand pilot account worth hearing, even without papers or data.","q":{"x":"Can you just... please... at least just watch the damn 7-minute video","i":30622783,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30622783,"author":"pmarreck","month":"2022-03","story":"Flight characteristics of anomalous unidentified aerial vehicles (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short pilot testimony worth hearing despite lack of papers.","quote":"Can you just... please... at least just watch the damn 7-minute video","text":"Can you just... please... at least just watch the damn 7-minute video (shit, if you watch it at 1.5 speed it's only 5 mins!), because as it turns out, not all truths can be reached via scientific papers, with some truths you have to actually listen to people talking about their subjective experiences (more pedestrian cases in point: kids claiming they are trans or gay despite no obvious mechanistic explanation for it, women claiming they were sexually assaulted, guys claiming that blueballs exist (did you know there's no actual evidence for that and that the predominant explanation is \"testicular torsion\", which is insane, as that condition is an extremely rare medical emergency, according to my nurse friend?), anyone reporting anything as an eyewitness to a police or legal authority... etc. etc. etc.) I'm not trying to sell you anything. I'm not making any money on this (and neither are these folks), and 60 Minutes is a (mostly) reputable information source with a long history. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28754987,"author":"dandotway","month":"2021-10","story":"The Obsessive Life of the Fisherman Who Discovered the Loch Ness Monster (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot interviews on the 2004 tic tac sighting.","quote":"four separate Navy pilots came forward to say they all saw that white tic tac in 2004","text":"Ahh, so if the monster exists then it must, like the porpoises, only rarely travel to Loch Ness from the ocean, so that few sightings per century can be expected. I thought all alien craft sightings were BS until four separate Navy pilots came forward to say they all saw that white tic tac in 2004. And three of the four pilots have done television interviews, most recently this 60 Minutes one: [VIDEO] If not for the link between Loch Ness and the ocean I'd think the existence of the monster could probably be ruled out as attention seeking liars. But now that we know porpoises sometimes make the rare trip from the ocean, we can't 100% rule out the monster never does. Does Dochfour Weir have 24/7 thermal and sonar monitoring and recording? Would Dochfour Weir capture on video and count every single porpoise that passes through at 3am, 365 days per year?"},{"id":27349789,"author":"throwaway29303","month":"2021-06","story":"The UFO Sightings Don't Impress This Physicist","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"there's brief footage of the object[0]","text":"For one thing, first-person accounts, which are notoriously inaccurate to begin with, don’t provide enough information for an empirical investigation. Scientists can’t accurately gauge distances or velocity from a pilot’s testimony: “It looked close” or “It was moving really fast” is too vague. What a scientist needs are precise measurements from multiple viewpoints provided by devices that register various wavelengths (visual, infrared, radar). That kind of data might tell us if an object’s motion required engines or materials that we Earthlings don’t possess. It's true that first-person accounts are sometimes inaccurate, but in the Nimitz case, we have, I believe, eight pilots in total who actually saw the object. The first incursion by David Fravor and his co-pilot Alex Dietrich and a second plane; and the second incursion - by other pilots which I don't know the names of - on which there's brief footage of the object[0]. Not only there's first-person accounts there's also devices which registered these objects. Nimitz caught these objects, in fact, they not only caught several of them, the incursions were made in order to check out what was going on. I wish these \"devices can be faulty,\" (knowing that people actually looked at the object in loco ) \"people can be bad first-person accounts\" memes actually died once and for all in this particular case. It feels like people don't actually go out and do their due diligence. I feel people either aren't listening or paying atten"},{"id":27316541,"author":"creedleshrump","month":"2021-05","story":"Radar data confirms: USS Omaha was surrounded by swarm of UFOs","stance":"reference","why":"Navy pilot Alex Dietrich recounts seeing the tic tac object.","quote":"Lt. Cmdr. Alex Dietrich was with Fravor and also saw the tic tac","text":"Lt. Cmdr. Alex Dietrich was with Fravor and also saw the tic tac: [VIDEO] Also would be surprised if military pilots were allowed to carry their cell phones with them, nor would it likely be advisable to take a phone video while you're piloting a fighter jet."}],"r":[["yTkzNHF6rMs",1],["ZBtMbBPzqHY",1]]},"2Z43y94Dfzk":{"wy":"Referenced in discussions of early GUIs and Alto history as a rare first-hand demonstration by the creator.","q":{"x":"In 2017 Tesler recorded a demo with the Computer History Museum on their restored Alto","i":40326265,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":27459085,"author":"Rochus","month":"2021-06","story":"Building the First GUIs","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting presentation on early editors like Gypsy.","quote":"I assume you know these interesting presentations","text":"Bravo was modal, Gypsy was non-modal, Smalltalk-74 onwards had multi-windows (including pop-up menus) and a metaphorical desktop. I assume you know these interesting presentations: [VIDEO] , [LINK] EDIT: see also e.g. [LINK]"},{"id":42889153,"author":"Someone","month":"2025-01","story":"Xerox Alto Source Code (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Comparison point for how Alto's GUI differed from Mac's.","quote":"(For comparison, look at","text":"In hindsight, yes, but I’m not sure it looked like it at the time. Surely, it took Apple years to start making ‘real’ money on selling Macs. They needed their Apple 2 line to pay the bills for years. Also, it wasn’t as much tech (bitmapped displays and computer mice existed elsewhere) as a vision that Apple took from Xerox, and they improved upon it/changed it quite a bit. Examples: - Alto’s GUI is verb-object, Mac is object-verb - Alto doesn’t have overlapping windows. - did Alto have cross-process copy-paste? - simplifying the mouse down to a single button may have been necessary to gain acceptance. (For comparison, look at [VIDEO] )"},{"id":40326265,"author":"musicale","month":"2024-05","story":"Preliminary Macintosh Business Plan (12 July 1981) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Larry Tesler's Alto demo at the Computer History Museum.","quote":"In 2017 Tesler recorded a demo with the Computer History Museum on their restored Alto","text":"> took inspiration from Xerox PARC Apple also took several people from Xerox PARC, including Larry Tesler (cut and paste, Object Pascal, Apple Lisa, Newton, etc.) who had demonstrated the Alto to Steve Jobs. In 2017 Tesler recorded a demo with the Computer History Museum on their restored Alto: [VIDEO] And of course Charles Simonyi went from PARC (Bravo) to Microsoft (Word)."},{"id":36117744,"author":"nequo","month":"2023-05","story":"No_color","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Alto GUI text editor with light background.","quote":"the Xerox Alto had a light background too, back in the 1970s.","text":"The “natural” background for text is light though. Hard to find books, newspapers, or magazines that put a dark background behind text of any significant length. Accordingly, the Xerox Alto had a light background too, back in the 1970s. Demonstrated here with Smalltalk: [LINK] And here with a GUI based text editor: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Qg0mHFcB510",1],["OsFEV35tWsg",1]]},"6_yU9eJ0NxA":{"wy":"Used as a friendly example of high-dimensional geometry showing up in a simple problem, and of ball volume shrinking inside a hypercube.","q":{"x":"There's a nice video about this from Numberphile and 3blue1brown","i":36010687,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36010687,"author":"jstx1","month":"2023-05","story":"Counterintuitive Properties of High Dimensional Space","stance":"rec","why":"Nice video on ball volume shrinking within a hypercube.","quote":"There's a nice video about this from Numberphile and 3blue1brown","text":"You can and as the dimensions go up the volume of the inscribed ball as a percentage of the volume of the hypercube shrinks down a lot. There's a nice video about this from Numberphile and 3blue1brown - [VIDEO]"},{"id":29972677,"author":"gorloth","month":"2022-01","story":"A high-dimensional sphere spilling out of a high-dimensional cube","stance":"reference","why":"Darts puzzle solved with volumes of 4+ dimensional spheres.","quote":"Higher dimensional geometry can show up in lower dimensional problems.","text":"Higher dimensional geometry can show up in lower dimensional problems. This numberphile video ( [VIDEO] ) involves a puzzle about throwing darts at a dart board which is solved by using the volumes of 4+ dimensional spheres."},{"id":29341418,"author":"jhncls","month":"2021-11","story":"Euler’s number pops up in situations that involve optimality","stance":"reference","why":"e shows up in a darts game.","quote":"this weird number pops up in a game of darts.","text":"In a unique Numberphile video featuring Grant Sanderson (3blue1brown), this weird number pops up in a game of darts. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28598697,"author":"jstx1","month":"2021-09","story":"A mathematician's guided tour through higher dimensions","stance":"reference","why":"Example of approaching higher dimensions without spatial intuition.","quote":"Darts in Higher Dimensions, 3blue1brown and Numberphile","text":"I think it's easier to approach higher dimensions without talking about spatial dimensions at all. For example - Darts in Higher Dimensions, 3blue1brown and Numberphile - [VIDEO] Or even more trivially, you can think of a table where every row is some entity and every column is some attribute associated to it. For example, make a spreadsheet where each row is a person and the columns are age, height, weight, salary, and years to retirement - then you can think of each person as a point in 5-d space. And some properties are intuitively obvious - for example as you keep adding more columns it becomes more difficult to find people who are similar to each other. It's a pretty accessible way to introduce high dimensions without talking about tessaracts."}],"r":[["h7apO7q16V0",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["sD0NjbwqlYw",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1]]},"7aXbh9VUB3U":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as the clearest explanation of the PS/2 protocol, usually when keyboard interfaces come up.","q":{"x":"There's a good video on how PS/2 works here","i":26919402,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26919402,"author":"zackbloom","month":"2021-04","story":"The last generation of typewriter repairmen (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the PS/2 protocol works","quote":"There's a good video on how PS/2 works here","text":"There's a good video on how PS/2 works here: [VIDEO] It's pretty straight-forward. There are also pre-made kits for digitizing typewriters if that interests you."},{"id":28966374,"author":"RJIb8RBYxzAMX9u","month":"2021-10","story":"IBM PS/2 Keyboard Modification","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how PS/2 keyboards work","quote":"Ben Eater's made a few videos discussing how PS/2[0] and USB keyboards[1] work.","text":"I'd imagine it would be very difficult, and would require a relatively powerful MCU. Ben Eater's made a few videos discussing how PS/2[0] and USB keyboards[1] work. USB's a vastly more complicated protocol compared to PS/2. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":28384377,"author":"bear8642","month":"2021-09","story":"It's Time for Operating Systems to Rediscover Hardware","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"think might be at end of this one < [VIDEO] > though there's a few he's done recently on USB spec"},{"id":27806253,"author":"thristian","month":"2021-07","story":"How does USB device discovery work? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Part of Ben Eater's keyboard protocol video series","quote":"He investigates the PS/2 keyboard protocol","text":"Piggy-backing this comment to add some context for the OP video: This \"USB device discovery\" video is actually the latest in a series of videos where Ben Eater is building a simple 8-bit computer on breadboards. He hooked up a PS/2 keyboard as an input device, and then got sucked into a tangent of investigating and explaining keyboard protocols. - He investigates the PS/2 keyboard protocol: [VIDEO] - Somebody on Patreon asked about USB keyboards having higher latency than PS/2, so he made a video about the basic USB protocol: [LINK] - He made the OP video about USB keyboards being limited to holding six keys at once."}]},"7lm3K8zVOdY":{"wy":"HN cites it as Nubank's account of building a bank on Datomic and Clojure, and for what Datomic's temporal model enables.","q":{"x":"It's worthwhile watching Nubank's talk on how they manage these things with Datomic on the backend","i":9852036,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9852036,"author":"sgrove","month":"2015-07","story":"Om Next – David Nolen [video]","stance":"rec","why":"How Nubank manages client data syncing with Datomic on the backend.","quote":"It's worthwhile watching Nubank's talk on how they manage these things with Datomic on the backend","text":"It's worthwhile watching Nubank's talk on how they manage these things with Datomic on the backend [0] - providing a \"complete\" (filtered) database to the client, http caches for queries based on tx-time, and syncing mobiles data via transactions-since. Takes care of said concerns nicely - I'm just implementing this stuff today however, so I'm not sure how well it'll work in practice. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":40377042,"author":"stuarthalloway","month":"2024-05","story":"Jepsen: Datomic Pro 1.0.7075","stance":"reference","why":"Nubank founders' Clojure/conj 2014 talk on Datomic.","quote":"Ed Wible is a founder of Nubank and chose Datomic.","text":"Ed Wible is a founder of Nubank and chose Datomic. He and Lucas Cavalcanti gave a talk on it at Clojure/conj 2014. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28428408,"author":"Chyzwar","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: What could a modern database do that PostgreSQL and MySQL can't","stance":"reference","why":"Datomic temporal query capabilities.","quote":"Datomic temporal capabilities[5].","text":"1. High performance read/write of Scylla/Cassandra with high availability[1]. It has some limitations for OLTP workloads and require careful planning. Postgres without Citus is not really HA and even then companies would often invent custom sharding/cluster management system. 2. Powerful graph query language like Cypher. It might not perform well in real life, but my personal experience left me amazed[2]. There is a number of issues with SQL that could be addressed[3], but current standard is way too much prevalent. 3. Zero impedance mismatch between database and application representation. In database like Smalltalk GemStone it is really seamless experience for developer to write database code and application code[4]. To some extent, MongoDB success can be attributed to this aspect. 4. Datomic temporal capabilities[5]. It is hard to maintain temporal tables in postgres. There are some use cases where you really want query in point of time. Strictly not an OLTP feature, but I can see this be usefully in many scenarios. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [VIDEO] Edited: independence -> impedance"},{"id":17809291,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2018-08","story":"Idempotence: What is it and why should I care?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on Datomic use at a Brazilian bank.","quote":"I found the talk","text":"I do remember Datomic and I think it's a great tool but I fell out of love with the Clojure ecosystem and JVM-based languages as a whole and don't think I'll be getting back into it/them. I do remember wanting to check out Datomic (I believe after seeing a talk on how it was being used at a bank in southern america?[0]), but I found it unreasonably hard to find and download/experiment with the community edition -- compare this to something like Postgres which is much more obvious, more F/OSS compliant (I understand that they need to make money) and Datomic doesn't really look that appealing to me these days. At this point in my learning of software craftmanship I can't do non-statically type-checked/inferenced languages anymore -- I almost never use JS without Typescript for example. Typed clojure was in relatively early stages when I was last actively using clojure, and I'm sure it's not bad (probably way more mature now), but it's a staple in other languages like Common Lisp (the declare form IIRC). The prevailing mood the clojure community seemed to be against static type checking and I just don't think I can jive with that anymore. Thinking this way right now Datomic wouldn't be a good fit for me pesonally but I believe that it is probably high quality paradigm. [EDIT] - I found the talk: [VIDEO] [0]: [LINK]"},{"id":10248227,"author":"onetom","month":"2015-09","story":"AWS US East is experiencing high error rates on several services","stance":"reference","why":"Nubank building a bank on Datomic.","quote":"it's a competitive advantage for those guys' who are building a bank on top of it in brazil","text":"nadam said 10 seconds, not minutes. we are using the starter version combined with dynamodb in production and we found the payment structure very clear; no obfuscation whatsoever, unlike a microsoft or adobe pricing matrix ;) (it's made by the same guy who made the very open source clojure programming language, btw, and he is very much against obfuscation) anyway, it's a competitive advantage for those guys' who are building a bank on top of it in brazil ( [VIDEO] ). we feel we can avoid writing a lot of authorization and audit log code by using datomic, maybe u can save such work too."},{"id":8828612,"author":"dmix","month":"2015-01","story":"Clojure 2014 Year in Review","stance":"reference","why":"Nubank building a bank in Brazil with Datomic and Clojure.","quote":"building a bank from scratch in Brazil with Datomic and Clojure","text":"Not mentioned in the article but most interesting to me this year is how these guys are \"building a bank from scratch in Brazil with Datomic and Clojure\": [VIDEO] The future looks bright for the language, especially with applications like this."}],"r":[["kKySEUrP7LA",1],["NWSZ4c9yqW8",1],["YX40hbAHx3s",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["skos4B5x7qE",1],["a9xAKttWgP4",1]]},"AYshVbcEmUc":{"wy":"Cited in repair and recycling threads as Apple's showcase of its iPhone disassembly robot, and sometimes mocked as marketing for what is really shredding.","q":{"x":"see the Liam[0] robot that takes an iPhone down to constituent parts","i":16027815,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15361934,"author":"jxramos","month":"2017-09","story":"A “right to repair” movement tools up","stance":"rec","why":"Liam robot disassembling iPhones for recycling","quote":"Very cool!","text":"This article taught me about Liam [VIDEO] \"It is also investing in technology that makes it easier to recycle its products, such as Liam, a robot for disassembling iPhones.\" Very cool!"},{"id":16027815,"author":"eltoozero","month":"2017-12","story":"A Message to Our Customers about iPhone Batteries and Performance","stance":"reference","why":"Liam robot breaks iPhones into raw material parts","quote":"see the Liam[0] robot that takes an iPhone down to constituent parts","text":"> The throwaway economy Apple is thinking about this, see the Liam[0] robot that takes an iPhone down to constituent parts, and consider that the aluminum, steel, and glass are all quick turnaround raw materials. It's a start. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14931003,"author":"abalone","month":"2017-08","story":"Why Apple and other tech companies are fighting to keep devices hard to repair","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's robot for disassembling iPhones","quote":"Apple even has a custom-designed robot to disassemble iPhones to achieve maximum recyclability.[1]","text":"This is a super disingenuous article from the Verge. It's based on a highly biased \"report\" by the Repair Association trade group led by iFixit, whose business is impacted by this approach. They are now attempting to make an environmental argument. Pay close attention and you'll note that their premise, that user-repairable devices are better for the environment, is entirely unsupported . Where do you think most user-removed parts end up? In landfill. Whereas devices traded in to Apple can be recycled properly. Apple even has a custom-designed robot to disassemble iPhones to achieve maximum recyclability.[1] They also offer zero support for the claim that Apple's approach shortens the lifetime of devices. Apple offers a battery replacement program![2] You can extend your iPhone's life! And they recycle the old battery properly. iFixit just doesn't like that they can't sell you the kits to do it yourself. Apple's obvious motive, which this sensational article fails to dispute, is to make their devices smaller, watertight, and generally better. That is why they pack and glue the interiors with custom parts and seals. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11537075,"author":"sp332","month":"2016-04","story":"Almost Nothing About the ‘Apple Harvests Gold from iPhones’ Story Is True","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's Liam robot recovering materials from iPhones","quote":"Apple announced a robotics project last month called \"Liam\".","text":"Apple announced a robotics project last month called \"Liam\". [VIDEO] It recovers \"cobalt and lithium\", \"gold and copper\", \"silver and platinum\". The number \"2,204 pounds of gold\" comes from page 17 of this PDF [LINK] which prominently displays Liam on the cover page."},{"id":24669404,"author":"rasz","month":"2020-10","story":"Apple says electronics recycler “illegally” reselling over 100k iPhones, iPads","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple redefines recycling to Will it blend","text":"Yes, reselling is recycling. Apple redefines recycling to Will it blend [VIDEO] 100K devices back in the supply stream means Apple lost on at least $30Mil of pure profit."},{"id":24669467,"author":"rasz","month":"2020-10","story":"Apple says electronics recycler “illegally” reselling over 100k iPhones, iPads","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it goes into a blender","text":"Its the other way around. Apple determined it was detrimental to their bottom line to repair and resell devices older than Iphone X. Devices with cracked screens, requiring battery/ charging port/ case/ button replacement, of fully working traded in [LINK] ones. What do you think happens to perfectly working iphone 8 plus worth ~$300 after its been traded in? Its not being resold [LINK] , it goes into a blender [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NURiiQatBXA",1],["SEDuVF7kiPU",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1]]},"RJh9yTIBY48":{"wy":"It gets linked in threads about salt and taste as a quirky, memorable curiosity.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite random youtube videos","i":37530762,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37530762,"author":"kadoban","month":"2023-09","story":"Salt Taste Is Surprisingly Mysterious","stance":"rec","why":"Tasting lithium to cesium chloride salts","quote":"One of my favorite random youtube videos","text":"One of my favorite random youtube videos: [VIDEO] \"What salt tastes the best? Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs and more\""},{"id":39275415,"author":"elil17","month":"2024-02","story":"Lady Tasting Brine","stance":"reference","why":"Tasting alkali metal chloride salts","quote":"Reminds me of this insane video","text":"Reminds me of this insane video (tasting chloride salts of Lithium, Sodium, Potassium, Rubidium, and Caesium): [VIDEO] FWIW no one has ever noticed my use of 50% NaCl/50% KCl mixture (\"Lite Salt\") in place of NaCl. I think when you actually put that mixture on food it would be almost impossible to notice the difference."},{"id":21794682,"author":"osamagirl69","month":"2019-12","story":"Ramen restaurant prevalence is associated with stroke mortality in Japan","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This guy tries all of the alkali metal salts!","text":"Why not limit your scope to just sodium and potassium? This guy tries all of the alkali metal salts! [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ulCdoCfw-bY",1],["JTfhYyTuT44",1]]},"U88jj6PSD7w":{"wy":"HN shares it as a short, provocative counterpoint in threads on purpose, goals and the pursuit of happiness.","q":{"x":"I'll leave you with this 2 minute video from Slovenian philosopher Slavoj Žižek","i":12381293,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12381293,"author":"gwilkes","month":"2016-08","story":"Hunter S. Thompson on Finding Your Purpose (1958)","stance":"rec","why":"Short Zizek take questioning happiness as the goal","quote":"I'll leave you with this 2 minute video from Slovenian philosopher Slavoj Žižek","text":"Andrew, you're fine. I'm going to guess that you're American as America is the most happy-obsessed culture in the world in my experience. Happiness as virtue is not universal, maybe you need some more time abroad. I know you said you visited 100 countries but visiting 100 countries and living in another country for many years is quite a different thing. Or maybe you just need different friends or perhaps a meditation practice, I don't know. I'll leave you with this 2 minute video from Slovenian philosopher Slavoj Žižek, \"Why Be Happy When You Could Be Interesting?\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":34339558,"author":"knowhy","month":"2023-01","story":"The Daily Habits of Happiness Experts","stance":"reference","why":"Zizek sees happiness as a conformist category","quote":"Slavoj Žižek [1] sees happiness as a conformist category.","text":"Slavoj Žižek [1] sees happiness as a conformist category. He argues that: > We don't really want what we think we desire. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15781449,"author":"ak39","month":"2017-11","story":"Ask HN: What are your long-term goals?","stance":"reference","why":"Zizek on happiness as an unethical category","quote":"Happiness is an unethical \"cut-ay-gory\"","text":"I see your Mr Peterson (whom I love), and I raise you with the inimitable and sporadically brilliant Slavoj Zizek - whom I fucking hate so much that I love too. Happiness is an unethical \"cut-ay-gory\": [VIDEO] (For those you aren't aware of his tick, don't watch, just listen)"},{"id":7432465,"author":"dsaravel","month":"2014-03","story":"What if you didn't need money or attention?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See what I mean here","text":"I don't fell that being happy is worth it. If I needed no money and no attention, perhaps happiness shouldn't also be the next goal to achieve. See what I mean here: [VIDEO]"}]},"aqqEYz38ens":{"wy":"Cited as a cool, concrete visualization of Wi-Fi interference patterns.","q":{"x":"the space is basically riddled with interference patterns","i":16012324,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":37565379,"author":"TechBro8615","month":"2023-09","story":"Your WiFi Can See You","stance":"rec","why":"Earlier similar project that the commenter finds really cool","quote":"I know this stuff is dystopic, but I personally find it really cool.","text":"CNLohr made a similar project three years before that: [VIDEO] I know this stuff is dystopic, but I personally find it really cool. That said, I also disable WiFi and prefer ethernet wherever possible..."},{"id":40769794,"author":"andai","month":"2024-06","story":"CHART: The Hackable Amateur Radio Telescope","stance":"reference","why":"Room map made by moving a sensor with stepper motors","quote":"I saw a post a while ago where someone made a map of their room by moving some sensor","text":"I saw a post a while ago where someone made a map of their room by moving some sensor (measured either noise level or wifi signal) around with stepper motors. Edit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16012324,"author":"tqdm","month":"2017-12","story":"I amplified my home's Wi-Fi with aluminum foil","stance":"reference","why":"Shows high-resolution mapping of wifi interference patterns","quote":"the space is basically riddled with interference patterns","text":"I've also achieved substantial improvement with simple parabolic shaped, ~40 cm wide aluminum foil constructions, but there always seemed a bit luck involved and it needed some tweaking. Not sure whether the foil mainly acted as shielding that reduces the noise floor from other directions. I've read in other forums that fiddling with aluminum foil is mostly luck because additional reflective surfaces also introduce interferrence patterns unless they are precisely aligned. Even without aluminum foil it often helps to move either receiver or sender by fractions of 12.5 cm (the wavelength of 2.4 Ghz radiation) or of 6.0 cm (in case of 5 Ghz) because the space is basically riddled with interference patterns: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SA8ZBJWo73E",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1]]},"d-ruFmgTpqA":{"wy":"HN points to it as a concrete demo of emergency autoland when threads discuss pilot incapacitation and aviation automation.","q":{"x":"The real-life demo is pretty impressive","i":28617522,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28617522,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2021-09","story":"Startup aims to make piloting a jetpack as easy as flying a drone","stance":"rec","why":"Real-life demo of Garmin emergency autoland landing a plane","quote":"The real-life demo is pretty impressive","text":"Yes, they can. It'll cost more than the C152 is worth, but Garmin has a \"press button to land plane\" emergency feature recently approved by the FAA. It'll even announce itself to the relevant air traffic control frequencies. [LINK] > Simultaneously, Autoland analyzes terrain, weather, and nearby airports to determine the optimal airport for landing given the specific aircraft's available fuel and performance characteristics, winds, runway length, and a host of other factors. As the airplane descends, Autoland controls speed, altitude and flight path and manages throttles, flaps, cabin pressurization, and more. > Air traffic controllers are informed of the system's landing destination choice and clear traffic as they would with a human-flown emergency. On final approach, Autoland lowers the gear, flares the airplane, and brings it to a stop with automatic braking. Audible instructions for exiting the airplane then play. The real-life demo is pretty impressive: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46351239,"author":"Animats","month":"2025-12","story":"Autoland saves King Air, everyone reported safe","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Garmin SafeReturn, the system in this real save","quote":"This is Garman SafeReturn, and this is its first real save. Here's a demo.","text":"This is Garman SafeReturn, and this is its first real save. Here's a demo.[1] It's been shipping since about 2020, originally on the Cirrus Vision Jet. There's a lot going on. The system is aware of terrain, weather, and fuel, but not of runway status. So it gives the ground a few minutes to get ready, sending voice emergency messages to ATC. If you watch the flight track, you can see the aircraft circle several times, some distance from the airport, then do a straight-in approach. It sets up for landing, wheels down, flaps down, lands, brakes, and turns of the the engine. It doesn't taxi. Someone from the ground will have to tow or taxi the aircraft off the runway. It's mostly GPS driven, plus a radar altimeter for landing. The system can be triggered by a button in the cockpit, a button in the passenger area, and a system that detects the pilot isn't making any inputs for a long period or the aircraft is unstable and the pilot isn't trying to stabilize it. The pilot can take control back, but if they don't, the airplane will be automatically landed. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31710334,"author":"mlyle","month":"2022-06","story":"US moves closer to recalling Tesla’s self-driving software","stance":"reference","why":"Emergency landing autopilot: push a button and wait","quote":"Push a button and wait to be on the ground.","text":"> There are no aircraft autopilots which can be used for a fully hands-off landing There's no certificated 3C approaches or equipment, but category 3B autopilots will arm at DH, and then continue all the way down and flare and land. For that matter, there's all the emergency landing autopilots showing up in turboprops now. Push a button and wait to be on the ground. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31342377,"author":"haunter","month":"2022-05","story":"Passenger with “no idea how to fly” lands plane after pilot incapacitated","stance":"reference","why":"Shows emergency autoland feature possibly installed on the plane","quote":"Autoland has been a (mostly emergency) feature for years","text":"Autoland has been a (mostly emergency) feature for years [LINK] It might have been installed on the plane [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":29501707,"author":"kayodelycaon","month":"2021-12","story":"Artificial Intelligence in the Cockpit","stance":"reference","why":"Video of Garmin's emergency automatic landing system","quote":"","text":"If you haven't seen it, Garmin has an emergency automatic landing system that's installed in a few planes already: [LINK] Limitations: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29502349,"author":"Animats","month":"2021-12","story":"Artificial Intelligence in the Cockpit","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote source for Garmin Safe Return emergency landing system","quote":"There's already a system that does the whole job of flying, for emergency use only - Garmin Safe Return.","text":"There's already a system that does the whole job of flying, for emergency use only - Garmin Safe Return.[1] In the plane, there's one big red button. If pushed, the plane finds an airport and lands, all by itself. It picks the nearest suitable destination airport, using info about fuel state, weather, and airport status. It starts squawking with the emergency transponder code. It plays emergency messages to ATC. There's also pilot incapacitation detection. If the pilot doesn't do anything for a long time, the system sounds warnings, then takes over. Everybody else has to get out of the way, though, when it declares an emergency. It can't really communicate with ATC. So it's strictly an emergency system, for now. This is really just integrated control of the existing avionics. The existing systems are good enough that you can input waypoints and have them followed, and do an automatic instrument landing on a designated runway. This mostly sets up a flight path. It can't deal with traffic. There are no new sensors. The additional hardware just lets it lower the landing gear, apply the wheel brakes, and shut down the aircraft after landing. After which you're blocking the runway until someone comes out and moves the aircraft. Again, emergency use only. It's intended for the \"sick pilot, healthy airplane\" case - the pilot is out of action, but the hardware is fine. It's not helpful in making hard decisions when the aircraft is having problems. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["FzF1KySHmUA",1],["PZbqAMEwtOE",1],["YsmgPp_nlok",1],["GvtNyOzGogc",1]]},"fQqv0v14KKY":{"wy":"Cited as the reference for baseband compromise, especially the point that owning the baseband can expose the application processor's memory.","q":{"x":"Owning the baseband is sufficient to gain access to the raw RAM of the application processor","i":6916112,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6989444,"author":"andr","month":"2013-12","story":"The NSA Reportedly Has Total Access To The Apple iPhone","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on attacking phone baseband software","quote":"An interesting presentation on the topic","text":"I really see this working remotely, as long as you have control over a cell phone tower or you use a phony portable base station, both of which are within the NSA's reach. The thing is phone baseband software (which is reused on different phone models and controls the phone's I/O including GSM, USB, etc.) has hardly ever been under attack. When the iPhone arrived with its new security model, baseband bugs became one of the major ways to jailbreak a phone. Those bugs have been fixed one by one, but they were mostly on the USB side - the GSM side has been impractical to attack. A carefully crafted GSM packet could in 2008 and probably could now cause a buffer overflow in the baseband and gain access. An interesting presentation on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8769816,"author":"rgbrenner","month":"2014-12","story":"German researchers discover flaw that could let anyone listen to cell calls","stance":"reference","why":"DeepSec talk on baseband attacks","quote":"Edit: Slides from a talk at DeepSec","text":"> Do you know in which part of the phones this is supposed to be in? The baseband chip.. it runs its own RTOS (separate from Android/ios/etc): [LINK] Rather than explaining, I think this link is a pretty good example: a chip from qualcomm that was sold at least through 2012: [LINK] It was demonstrated at BlackHat in 2011 on an iPhone 4 and HTC Dream (android): [LINK] [LINK] Edit: Slides from a talk at DeepSec: [LINK] Edit: video: [VIDEO] edit: I think this is the paper: [LINK]"},{"id":7395554,"author":"brianmwaters_hn","month":"2014-03","story":"Samsung Galaxy Back-door","stance":"reference","why":"Baseband shares memory with the CPU in most phones","quote":"in most phones, the CPU and BB share memory","text":"Yes. Some further research I did since this thread started has shown that in some phones, even the microphone and camera are under the direct control of the baseband processor. In others, it's not. But in most phones, the CPU and BB share memory, which opens the door for the BB to do all sorts of nasty things to the OS on the CPU. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6916112,"author":"ge0rg","month":"2013-12","story":"Telegram - secure, free messaging","stance":"reference","why":"Baseband compromise exposes the application processor's memory","quote":"Owning the baseband is sufficient to gain access to the raw RAM of the application processor","text":"Only open-source apps on open-source operating systems [..] ...running on Open Source hardware. Many modern smartphones employ a baseband processor and an application processor with shared memory. While the app processor might be running an open-source compiled Android, the baseband processor is usually on a closed, not externally verified (and very buggy) RTOS provided by the manufacturer (Qualcomm, MediaTek, TI, ...)[0]. Owning the baseband is sufficient to gain access to the raw RAM of the application processor, to read any IM messages and keys, to manipulate data, code and whatever else you like. And all this is completely undetectable by any \"antivirus\" software running on the app processor. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"m1kHsd3Ocxc":{"wy":"Cited as engineering evidence that Tesla's integrated thermal design is far simpler than Ford's, in EV and quality debates.","q":{"x":"Another fun one is Munro's comparison of the thermal management systems for a Model Y and Ford Mach-E","i":38772222,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38772222,"author":"DennisP","month":"2023-12","story":"Breakdown of faults by car brand: Tesla has replaced Dacia at the bottom","stance":"rec","why":"Munro compares Model Y and Mach-E thermal systems","quote":"Another fun one is Munro's comparison of the thermal management systems for a Model Y and Ford Mach-E","text":"Tesla's manufacturing has come a long way in the past few years. In 2018, Sandy Munro famously tore down the new Model 3 with a lot of criticism for things like their excess of parts, fasteners, and welds. They made a lot of changes, including some he suggested, and now Munro tends to rave about how well they're engineered for ease of manufacture. This is a guy who gets paid by Big 3 auto companies to help improve their manufacturing. A great visual is this video of someone snapping together much of a Model Y like legos, in ten minutes by himself: [LINK] Another fun one is Munro's comparison of the thermal management systems for a Model Y and Ford Mach-E: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38129829,"author":"schiffern","month":"2023-11","story":"Auto execs are coming clean: EVs aren't working","stance":"reference","why":"Details Ford's complicated resistive heating coolant loop","quote":"Ford's system is also Rube Goldberg[2]: they use a water-based PTC heater to warm a small isolated coolant loop","text":"> The problem is that driving EVs in the cold costs a lot more energy. I've got a Ford Mustang Mach E Nowadays this problem isn't \"EVs,\" it's mostly just the Mach E. The Mach E delivers heat in the most inefficient way possible: resistive heat[0]. Modern EVs from other manufacturers use heat pumps, which are much more efficient. There's still some drop in winter range (like gas cars), but it's nowhere near 35% anymore.[1] Ford's system is also Rube Goldberg[2]: they use a water-based PTC heater to warm a small isolated coolant loop (complete with its own separate reservoir!), and then they run a pump to send it through a liquid-to-air heater core. Obviously done for commonality with an ICE heater core, but the unnecessary weight and complexity shows the compromises to shoehorn an electric drivetrain into a ICE (or \"flex\") platform. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34717346,"author":"nebula8804","month":"2023-02","story":"Apple signs union agreement for Glasgow Apple Store staff","stance":"reference","why":"Engineering evidence for Tesla's quality","quote":"just let the engineering speak for itself","text":"Clearly you are not going to be convinced because you came into this conversation with a conclusion and backtracked from that just like the other poster. That is a common problem when discussing both Tesla and Apple. Both companies evoke a lot of emotion and \"foaming at the mouth\" type responses. Both companies have continued track records of producing stellar engineering. But for anyone else reading this try to separate your emotion for \"lord elno\" and just let the engineering speak for itself. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":30555896,"author":"martythemaniak","month":"2022-03","story":"The cold hard truth about electric vehicles in winter","stance":"reference","why":"Munro teardown praising Tesla's integrated thermal design","quote":"It's basically head and shoulders above the competitions","text":"Active battery management is common to all EVs now - only the first gen Leaf and other weird niche vehicles like that don't have it. What Tesla does is an incredibly refined, efficient and integrated hardware coupled with great software. If you watch Munro's teardown of the new Model S, they basically state that they don't think it can really be improved. It's basically head and shoulders above the competitions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28285028,"author":"Isinlor","month":"2021-08","story":"Japan is holding back as the world rushes toward electric cars","stance":"reference","why":"Compares Tesla and Ford cooling system complexity","quote":"Compare cooling in Ford Mustang Mach-E and Tesla Model Y. Tesla just brutally crashes Ford in how much they are able to reduce number of parts","text":"From what I've seen from cars being put apparat by Sandy Munro company the complexity of EV cooling system maps roughly 1 to 1 with ICE. Both need cooling loops basically. There is however a staggering difference between companies. Compare cooling in Ford Mustang Mach-E and Tesla Model Y. Tesla just brutally crashes Ford in how much they are able to reduce number of parts, especially hoses and hose connectors. Tesla has something like 3x less hoses, 3x less parts and half the mass of the coolant in the loop (22kg vs 9 kg). Maybe part of what you are seeing is inexperience and incompetence of ICE car manufacturers. There are also other things that legacy car manufacturers are not willing to risk like megacasting. Something like 50 parts reduction in one cast, and good chunk of the factory floor for Tesla. And apparently the idea was out there long before Tesla but nobody tried it. While Musk loves taking financial risks if they can deliver significant engineering benefits. [VIDEO] Not to mention that while Tesla may be not great at software engineering, ICE car companies and their supply chains are just ridiculously bad at it."},{"id":27950320,"author":"antattack","month":"2021-07","story":"Gas Engines, and the People Behind Them, Are Cast Aside for Electric Vehicles","stance":"reference","why":"Mach-E teardown reveals room for optimization","quote":"As Munro's Mach-E tear-down revealed - there's a lot to be optimized in their latest EV[1]","text":"It's disingenuous for the article to claim does that ICE engineers are replaced by software engineers. Now-days if you want to make a fuel efficient engine you have to use software modeling and simulation. Maybe people who are close to retirement age are still hands on but industry have switched to CAD/CAM decades ago and is not going back. Engineers who are verse in CAD/CAM can switch to designing castings, optimizing cooling and heating, and improve part integration. As Munro's Mach-E tear-down revealed - there's a lot to be optimized in their latest EV[1] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LbH1ZDImaI8",1]]},"ptjedOZEXPM":{"wy":"HN points to it as the primary source on Dalvik's dex bytecode, register-based design and memory-saving choices.","q":{"x":"Dan Bornstein's talk at Google I/O 2008 about the dex format and the original Dalvik interpreter","i":3916699,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":3916699,"author":"yonran","month":"2012-05","story":"Android Ported to C#","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about the dex format and original Dalvik interpreter.","quote":"Dan Bornstein's talk at Google I/O 2008 about the dex format and the original Dalvik interpreter","text":"Here's Dan Bornstein's talk at Google I/O 2008 about the dex format and the original Dalvik interpreter: [VIDEO] I'm not sure how useful it is in comparing the Dalvik JIT to MonoTouch though."},{"id":18082907,"author":"jashmatthews","month":"2018-09","story":"Profiling CPython at Instagram (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Dalvik's register-based bytecode and efficient interpreter.","quote":"Dalvik also uses a register-based bytecode.","text":"There's an experimental branch for Ruby: [LINK] Dalvik also uses a register-based bytecode. The early versions of Android needed a more efficient interpreter. Dan Bornstein's talk: [VIDEO] A classic paper on Register vs Stack VMs: [LINK]"},{"id":13428991,"author":"chubot","month":"2017-01","story":"Dismissing Python Garbage Collection at Instagram","stance":"reference","why":"Dalvik moved reference counts out of objects to run on small memory.","quote":"The Dalvik VM (now replaced by ART) also did this to run on phones with 64 MiB of memory","text":"This is a great post. I like how they walked through all the steps and especially the \"perf\" tool. Ruby has a patch to do the same thing -- increase sharing by moving reference counts out of the object itself: Index here: [LINK] First post in a long series: [LINK] I think these patches or something similar may have made it into Ruby 2.0: [LINK] [LINK] The Dalvik VM (now replaced by ART) also did this to run on phones with 64 MiB of memory: [VIDEO] I think PHP might do it too. It feels like Python should be doing this as well."},{"id":5001997,"author":"ttuominen","month":"2013-01","story":"Ubuntu for phones","stance":"reference","why":"Dex format designed for low space requirements.","quote":"the Android .dex bytecode format has been designed with low space requirements in mind.","text":"I agree: within the Android ecosystem and even within the ARM family, bytecode provides portability: you don't have to compile and distribute apps for different versions of the ARM architecture. It's paradoxical however that to increase the portability of certain types of apps across mobile ecosystems, eg. iOS and Android, and now Ubuntu, your best bet can be to write the portable part in native C or C++, bypassing the Dalvik layer using JNI/NDK. This is apparently the common way to make cross-platform mobile games. On memory consumption: I haven't made any measurements, but the Android .dex bytecode format has been designed with low space requirements in mind. See [VIDEO] . However, all apps run in their own VM process, with lots of common structures shared with them using an initial shared VM process and clever forking. I always thought one of the main original motivations to use Java was because it's so well spread among developers. Since in the early days before the JIT compiler, there must have been a really obvious overhead even with the interpreter hand-coded in assembly. But they must have wanted to use a well known higher level language with good IDE support etc. Ubuntu mobile apparently uses Qt: \"It uses QML to give you a really slick, easy development experience for native apps with engines in C or C++, and JavaScript for UI glue that isn’t performance critical. We also give you full native OpenGL, which the top games companies are using to make incredible games.\" "}]},"upsZZ2s3xv8":{"wy":"It comes up as a beloved example of NFB animation and state-funded Canadian cultural work.","q":{"x":"Wade Hemsworth was a national treasure. So many great folk songs.","i":37414290,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37414290,"author":"fanatic2pope","month":"2023-09","story":"National Film Board of Canada","stance":"rec","why":"Classic NFB animated short featuring Wade Hemsworth's folk song","quote":"Wade Hemsworth was a national treasure. So many great folk songs.","text":"The one that immediately comes to mind for me is the Log Driver's Waltz. [VIDEO] Wade Hemsworth was a national treasure. So many great folk songs. [LINK]"},{"id":33884641,"author":"RichardCA","month":"2022-12","story":"Moog Dancers Prove That TV Was a Lot More Adventurous in the 70s","stance":"reference","why":"Example of NFB animation that aired on PBS","quote":"","text":"For me personally, I spent most of the 70's bored to death in school, but I got to come home and watch shows like \"International Animation Festival\" on Channel 13 (PBS if you grew up in the NYC area). They also had the first run of Monty Python's Flying Circus in the US, which was one of the few things me and my dad enjoyed together. But the bulk the animation shown on PBS came from the NFB - The National Film Board of Canada, which provided the retro aestheics that were later adopted by Boards of Canada. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27560238,"author":"motohagiography","month":"2021-06","story":"America’s weirdest guidebooks were funded by the government","stance":"reference","why":"Example of state-funded effort to build Canadian identity","quote":"The logrider's waltz is another one","text":"These were strange, and when you look at them now, they were part of this effort in the 60's onward to create a national \"Canadian\" culture and identity using little factoids like those in Hinterland Who's Who as a common understanding. The logrider's waltz is another one, [VIDEO] , but you have to imagine what weird places the CBC and TV Ontario would have had to have been in the 50's and 60's, where it was a mix of turtlenecked beatnicks, remittence men, old family connected bureaucrats, with influential infiltrators from french separatist and foreign communist movements, Camp X instructors, and a bunch of political idealists spending relatively unlimited public money on inventing a new national identity. In hindsight, the 1970s, Polka-Dot Door was obviously an acid fueled propaganda machine, and Polkaroo, a giant dotted ambiguously gendered kangaroo friend that nobody else could see was the beginning of intersectionality. The future was imminently revolutionary and operated by a benevolent new intellectual class on systems like a new rumored Chilean technology called Cybersyn, which I am sure we sent agents to infiltrate and steal the blueprints of this amazing technology for ourselves to give us a national advantage ( [LINK] ), and groups of conceptual artists formed networks that connected revolutionary politics with the young people of establishment society, e.g. General Idea ( [LINK] ) All against a backdrop of those earth toned rainbows, the Olympics in Montreal, from"}],"r":[["5JYp9eGC3Cc",1]]},"vzGPq9LSjEw":{"wy":"Cited by commenters skeptical of climate data adjustments, to support claims about NOAA modeled data. The claims are contested.","m":[{"id":28123833,"author":"flat-bird","month":"2021-08","story":"Global Temperature over My Lifetime","stance":"rec","why":"Claims NOAA fabricates station data to cool the past","quote":"The first few minutes of this video provide an explanation.","text":"First, even if fossil fuel companies predicted a rise in temperature due to their activities, they're as helpless to predict cause and effect in a complex system as everyone else. They're probably guilty of dishonesty, but it's wrong to act as if they had special insight and ability to predict the future because of what they sell. Second, most of the alleged warming almost certainly just comes from data tampering by NOAA. NOAA \"models\" an increasing number of its stations (lately around 40%). On those stations, it fabricates data according to a model, and applies a varying fudge factor that cools the past and warms the present in a way that assumes the CO2 hypothesis. The first few minutes of this video provide an explanation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28825564,"author":"HamburgerEmoji","month":"2021-10","story":"Globalwarmingindex.org – Tracking progress to a safe climate","stance":"reference","why":"Claims NOAA models temperatures assuming CO2 hypothesis","quote":"NOAA fiddles around with measurements and generates temperatures according to models that assume the CO2 hypothesis.","text":"NOAA fiddles around with measurements and generates temperatures according to models that assume the CO2 hypothesis. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28467557,"author":"BoHerfIIIJrEsq","month":"2021-09","story":"The truth about global temperature data (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Claims NOAA fabricates data assuming CO2 hypothesis","quote":"Temperature data manipulation by NOAA goes far beyond what people might imagine.","text":"Temperature data manipulation by NOAA goes far beyond what people might imagine. They cool the past and warm the present by fabricating data for stations using a model that assumes the CO2 hypothesis. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28344035,"author":"BoHerfIIIJrEsq","month":"2021-08","story":"Climate change will alter where many crops are grown","stance":"reference","why":"Claims NOAA replaces stations with modeled virtual stations","quote":"","text":"> They insist on this because we can actually quantify almost precisely how this is the fault of human activity. No, we can't. We live in a complex system. It's basically impossible to untangle cause and effect. > The rate of global warming is almost perfectly matched to greenhouse gas emissions. Well, this is an interesting issue. There's a result with very serious implications floating around that I've been waiting for someone to reproduce. The gist of it is that when NOAA measurement stations fail or get retired, they're replaced with virtual stations that generate fabricated data generated by models that assume the CO2 hypothesis. That'd be bad, huh. [VIDEO]"}]},"zR3Igc3Rhfg":{"wy":"Shared as the best visceral demonstration of solar system scale.","q":{"x":"a mini-documentary where people attempt to build a true-to-scale model of the Solar System","i":44269825,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44269825,"author":"silasdb","month":"2025-06","story":"If the moon were only 1 pixel: A tediously accurate solar system model (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Mini-documentary building a true-to-scale solar system model.","quote":"a mini-documentary where people attempt to build a true-to-scale model of the Solar System","text":"This is a great website—it reminds me of To Scale: The Solar System [1], a mini-documentary where people attempt to build a true-to-scale model of the Solar System. It makes me feel like a tiny speck of dust, floating in the vastness of nowhere... [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38825281,"author":"aetherson","month":"2023-12","story":"Sweden Solar System","stance":"reference","why":"Scale model that gives a human sense of sizes.","quote":"Here's I think something that works.","text":"It's walkable, but you can't see the \"sun\" from the \"earth,\" and it's walkable in well more than an hour, at which point I don't think that you're really keeping the scale in mind. We can always look at images or whatever if we want to get an intellectual sense of the scale of things. I think the advantage of a model is that you feel immersed in it, and I don't think you do at this scale. It's challenging! It's hard to find a location and a scale which gives you this human sense of the sizes involved. At most scales, you have to make the planets pinpricks in size. But, especially for the inner system, you can make it work. EDIT: Here's I think something that works. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10239838,"author":"frogpelt","month":"2015-09","story":"Pluto ‘Wows’ in Spectacular New Backlit Panorama","stance":"reference","why":"Scale model of the solar system built in the Black Rock Desert.","quote":"this guy built a scale model in the Black Rock Desert","text":"Also, this guy built a scale model in the Black Rock Desert: [VIDEO] EDIT: This one doesn't include Pluto."}]},"--LTYRTKV_A":{"wy":"HN commenters link it to show how much stress airliner wings are designed to withstand, especially in turbulence and crash threads.","q":{"x":"see how much airplane wings can bend without breaking","i":18314069,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":43093451,"author":"mrguyorama","month":"2025-02","story":"Plane crashes, overturns during landing at Toronto airport","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the force and flex airplane wings are designed to withstand.","quote":"this is the level of force and flex they are designed to withstand","text":"Nah, an engine getting ripped off will not tear off the wing since this is the level of force and flex they are designed to withstand: [VIDEO] The engines are built to rip off cleanly, because when they don't, they have caused [LINK] and [LINK] which were fairly serious and catastrophic accidents at least partially caused by the engines tearing off and damaging the wing in such a serious manner as to cause a stall and crash. The wings stayed on the aircraft in both instances. Interestingly, both accidents were caused by those same sheer pins being damaged in minor and unpredicted ways. I highly doubt the wing is DESIGNED to cleanly separate. Planes are just very not rigid for something going such a high speed, and so tend to turn into confetti when faced with a harder surface, like a runway or a concrete building. Usually the only parts that survive serious crashes are the landing gear struts."},{"id":40821214,"author":"bparsons","month":"2024-06","story":"Is Clear Air Turbulence becoming more common?","stance":"reference","why":"Gives a taste of the stress airliner airframes are built for.","quote":"Commercial airliners are built for extraordinary stress on the airframe.","text":"Commercial airliners are built for extraordinary stress on the airframe. You can get a taste of it here: [VIDEO] Other people will correct me if I am wrong, but I believe the last time a large commercial airliner was lost to turbulence was 1966. [LINK]"},{"id":18314069,"author":"Pyxl101","month":"2018-10","story":"Less Turbulence on Delta Flights?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how far airliner wings can flex without breaking.","quote":"see how much airplane wings can bend without breaking","text":"When I hit turbulence, I just try to keep in mind that airplanes are extremely robust and can handle considerably more turbulence than a typical person might imagine. For example, see how much airplane wings can bend without breaking: [VIDEO]"}]},"-lErGZZgUbY":{"wy":"Used as a source for the Framework CEO's explanation of why the desktop's memory could not be socketed or modular.","q":{"x":"This sort-of-interview of Nirav Patel (ceo of framework) explains in a bit more detail","i":44853681,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45270003,"author":"daemonologist","month":"2025-09","story":"How to make the Framework Desktop run even quieter","stance":"reference","why":"CEO quote on why LPCAMM does not work with Strix Halo.","quote":"here's a quote from the CEO explaining (to some degree) the choice","text":"I agree, but here's a quote from the CEO explaining (to some degree) the choice: “So we did actually ask AMD about this the first time they told us about Strix Halo. It was literally our first question: ‘How do we get modular memory? We are Framework after all.’ And they didn’t say no actually, they did assign one of their technical architects to really really go deep on this—they ran simulations, they ran studies, and they just determined it’s not possible with Strix Halo to do LPCAMM. The signal integrity doesn’t work out because of how that memory’s fanning out over the 256-bit bus.” (from [VIDEO] via [LINK] )"},{"id":44850626,"author":"jeroenhd","month":"2025-08","story":"The Framework Desktop is a beast","stance":"reference","why":"CEO explains why modular memory was not possible.","quote":"they tried and AMD assigned an engineer on getting modular memory to work and decided it's not possible.","text":"According to the Framework CEO on the Linus Tech Tips video about this thing [1], they tried and AMD assigned an engineer on getting modular memory to work and decided it's not possible. Unless there's another company out there shipping this CPU with replaceable memory, I'll believe them. Even with LPCAMM, physics just doesn't work out. Of course, there are plenty of slower desktops you can buy where all the memory can be swapped out. If you want a repairable desktop like that, you're not stuck with Framework in the same way you would be with laptops. You'll probably struggle to get the same memory bandwidth to make full use of AMD's chips, though. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44853681,"author":"komali2","month":"2025-08","story":"The Framework Desktop is a beast","stance":"reference","why":"Framework CEO explains why socketed RAM was not feasible.","quote":"This sort-of-interview of Nirav Patel (ceo of framework) explains in a bit more detail","text":"As stated previously, the sockets reduce signal integrity, which doesn't necessarily make them \"bad,\" but is why Framework wasn't able to used socketed ram to maximize the potential of this CPU. This sort-of-interview of Nirav Patel (ceo of framework) explains in a bit more detail: [VIDEO] Basically, they need to use LPDDR5X memory, which isn't available in socketed form, because of signal integrity reasons. Which means you won't see an improvement if you solder your ram directly, I think mostly because your home soldering job will suffer signal integrity issues, but also because your RAM isn't LPCAMM and isn't spread across a 256 bit bus."},{"id":43176826,"author":"rdedev","month":"2025-02","story":"Framework's first desktop is a strange–but unique–mini ITX gaming PC","stance":"reference","why":"Framework CEO explains memory limitation from the AMD chip.","quote":"The ceo kind of explains why in this video.","text":"[VIDEO] The ceo kind of explains why in this video. In essence it seems to be a limitation of the chip from AMD"},{"id":43179572,"author":"phonon","month":"2025-02","story":"Framework's first desktop is a strange–but unique–mini ITX gaming PC","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Framework laptop CPU details.","quote":"","text":"The two CPU models are i3-1215U and i5-1334U [1] which are 15 W parts with 2 P cores. They should be OK. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"15HTd4Um1m4":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference whenever a company issues a hollow public apology.","m":[{"id":40904433,"author":"ryandrake","month":"2024-07","story":"Boeing to plead guilty to criminal fraud charge stemming from 737 MAX crashes","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"LOL To these guys, “taking responsibility” means simply making a sad face and issuing a press release saying “we’re sorry.”[1]. Not actually taking responsibility and going to prison. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38971825,"author":"fransje26","month":"2024-01","story":"Cloudflare CEO responds to viral termination video","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"We've done a lot of bad things, and we'll try to improve in the future.\" Standard procedure. And they will change nothing in the future. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39063409,"author":"calzone5116","month":"2024-01","story":"Fujitsu bugs that sent innocent people to prison were known \"from the start\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"We did have bugs and errors in the system and we did help the Post Office in their prosecutions of the sub-postmasters. For that we are truly sorry.\" Every time some CEO issues an apology i can't help but not take it seriously because it reminds of following scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38348118,"author":"jay-barronville","month":"2023-11","story":"I deeply regret my participation in the board's actions","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Maybe if he says “I’m sorry” South Park-style [1], they’ll reunite? In all seriousness though, there’s really no coming back from this. He made a risky move and he should stand behind it. OpenAI’s trajectory is pretty much screwed. Maybe they won’t disappear, but their days of dominating the market are obviously numbered. And of course, well done, Satya, for turning a train wreck into a win for Microsoft. (And doing so before market open!) [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32047216,"author":"rob_c","month":"2022-07","story":"Uber broke laws, duped police and built lobbying operation, leak reveals","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"But don't worry they're a changed company in the valley now and they're so sorry for past behaviour (nothing to do with being caught)... [VIDEO]"},{"id":25277415,"author":"ChuckNorris89","month":"2020-12","story":"Microsoft apologises for feature criticised as workplace surveillance","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of the \"We're sorry\" BP corporate apology scene from South Park","text":"Reminds me of the \"We're sorry\" BP corporate apology scene from South Park that aged like fine wine. Edit: found it [VIDEO]"},{"id":24157751,"author":"qayxc","month":"2020-08","story":"Why the Mauritius oil spill is so serious","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Maritime law has been super weak historically. There are basically no repercussions at all unless people get hurt. Even if the company itself gets fined, they'll simply declare bankruptcy, close up shop and sell their assets to a new company (often incidentally run by the same people). Sad how relevant this still is: [VIDEO]"}]},"25J3u3P-HHg":{"wy":"Used as shorthand for the arrogant, dismissive tech support person in jokes and comparisons.","m":[{"id":28329470,"author":"softwaredoug","month":"2021-08","story":"My love-hate affair with technology","stance":"reference","why":"SNL sketch of the condescending computer guy","quote":"","text":"There's something here between the lines I think I empathize with. That there's been a cultural shift from the tinkerer PC ethos, with decentralized vendors, respect for being \"open\" (whether open source or standard). This way of thinking seemed to be the default for those of us brought up into computing in the 80s up until the mid 2000s. Even on Microsoft products, the Evil Empire of these times, the amount of customizability and range of hardware and software supported in Windows shows how baked in these assumptions were. Of course, the average user struggled during these times given the endlessly complexity and configurability of technology. A lot of work with tech required someone speaking technobabble to get everything to work -- Someone like the old SNL sketch \"Nick Burns, Your Company's Computer Guy\" ( [VIDEO] ). Users don't care about the idealism behind the original PC movement, maybe to their detriment. They just want it to work. Those of us getting in the way to espouse ideal of open source this and that just seem to be getting in their way of the thing that \"just works\" regardless of what MegaCorp they have to turn their personal lives over to."},{"id":41542199,"author":"esafak","month":"2024-09","story":"The Bastard Operator from Hell (1999)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"SNL parody","text":"Back when computer expertise was hard to come by, so admins could lord over users. The Internet has made these people extinct. SNL parody: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33933363,"author":"mberning","month":"2022-12","story":"No Hello: A New Wave","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The living embodiment of Nick Burns.","text":"Usually the biggest office asshole and last person you want to talk to for anything. The living embodiment of Nick Burns. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33493608,"author":"lob_it","month":"2022-11","story":"Was everyone stupid back then?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Nick burns would disagree :)","text":"Nick burns would disagree :) [VIDEO] Technology natives skipped social to enjoy innovation without the emotional fodder. They were left on their own with nobody to coddle them. Its 2022.. Illiteracy is obvious"}]},"35DSdw7dHjs":{"wy":"HN posts it as an enduring, still-relevant artistic depiction of corporate power when discussing finance, tech lobbying and ethics.","q":{"x":"For an artistic, but accurate image of the world we live in see this scene from Network (1976)","i":28862021,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41769251,"author":"Terr_","month":"2024-10","story":"Silicon Valley, the new lobbying monster","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"which has aged pretty well for a movie 50 years old","text":"> \"All Watched over by Machines of Loving Grace\" This reminds me of the tycoon's thunderously semi-religious speech from The Network [0], which has aged pretty well for a movie 50 years old. > The world is a business, Mr. Beale. It has been since man crawled out of the slime. And our children will live, Mr. Beale, to see that... perfect world... in which there's no war or famine, oppression or brutality. One vast and ecumenical holding company, for whom all men will work to serve a common profit, in which all men will hold a share of stock. All necessities provided, all anxieties tranquilized, all boredom amused. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38368451,"author":"Terr_","month":"2023-11","story":"Seven Signs of Ethical Collapse","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the memorable (and ambiguously villainous) speech from The Network (1976)","text":"Somehow that makes me think of the memorable (and ambiguously villainous) speech from The Network (1976) [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33697689,"author":"deck4rd","month":"2022-11","story":"China Got Our Kids Hooked on ‘Digital Fentanyl’","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"is as relevant as ever","text":"[VIDEO] is as relevant as ever > Am I getting through to you, Mr. Beale? You get up on your little twenty-one inch screen and howl about America and democracy. There is no America. There is no democracy. There is only IBM and ITT and AT&T and DuPont, Dow, Union Carbide, and Exxon. Those are the nations of the world today. What do you think the Russians talk about in their councils of state? Karl Marx? They get out their linear programming charts, statistical decision theories, minimax solutions, and compute the price-cost probabilities of their transactions and investments, just like we do. We no longer live in a world of nations and ideologies, Mr. Beale. The world is a college of corporations, inexorably determined by the immutable by-laws of business. The world is a business, Mr. Beale. It has been since man crawled out of the slime. And our children will live, Mr. Beale, to see that perfect world in which there's no war or famine, oppression or brutality. One vast and ecumenical holding company, for whom all men will work to serve a common profit, in which all men will hold a share of stock. All necessities provided, all anxieties tranquilized, all boredom amused. And I have chosen you, Mr. Beale, to preach this evangel."},{"id":28862021,"author":"lsiq","month":"2021-10","story":"G7 finance officials endorse principles for central bank digital currencies","stance":"reference","why":"Network scene depicting the world as corporations","quote":"For an artistic, but accurate image of the world we live in see this scene from Network (1976)","text":"I will take the risk and give an explanation. The are two first principles that few fully understand: The first is that we are creatures of commerce - that is how we sustain ourselves. The economy is the total FLOW of all concurrent commercial transactions, and this is conducted via cash & money. For an artistic, but accurate image of the world we live in see this scene from Network (1976): [VIDEO] . The second first principle is that you are a sovereign spirit in this universe, and so is everybody else. There are no behavioral rules in the natural reality, only in the social reality. The rules, symbols, ideologies, religions, and institutions exist to manipulate other people's behavior and concentrate cash flows in the great system that is the economy. But in the pure state of nature, it really is only you. The cash flows concentrate as the more capital you start with the more you can acquire. The precious few people who reach the \"Center of Cash Flows\" ultimately do so with force; they kill people over money, embezzle public funds, and so on because they are sovereign in their selfish minds. They are not \"above the law,\" the law is simply for other people, not them. That is and has always been the true nature of \"government.\" But we have governments now that are sophisticated enough that we don't even know their true nature. It's like a laundromat owned by the Triads. You don't ever see the Triads, you don't know who owns the place, you simply go in and put your quarters in"}]},"7DTlWPgX6zs":{"wy":"Used mostly as a citation of C++'s historical baggage and complexity when HN criticizes or jokes about the language.","m":[{"id":28506387,"author":"db48x","month":"2021-09","story":"Transmission torrent client ported to C++","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Several CPPCON talks in recent years have reinforced that for me as well.","text":"Several CPPCON talks in recent years have reinforced that for me as well. [VIDEO] , for example. Edit: I originally linked to the wrong video ( [LINK] ), which was a different talk by the same person."},{"id":29488096,"author":"belter","month":"2021-12","story":"Bjarne Stroustrup: “I Did It for You All” (2007)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Next Halloween I will dress up as std::variant","text":"What a tour de force! Thanks for the link. It beats my previous favorite C++ presentation: \"CppCon 2018: Nicolai Josuttis “The Nightmare of Initialization in C++” [VIDEO] Next Halloween I will dress up as std::variant"},{"id":24390526,"author":"hackingthenews","month":"2020-09","story":"C++20 has been approved","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Just gonna drop this here","text":"Agreed. Just gonna drop this here: Nicolai Josuttis “The Nightmare of Initialization in C++” [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21513392,"author":"michaelg7x","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: How to approach learning C, C++, or Java on free time?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"What a mess (C++ initialisation, not the book)!","text":"I think this depends to some extent on the type of job you're going for, which might seem trite but a graphics role is going to be different from a systems programming role. I've been writing Java for a long time and tired after a while of the JVM \"getting in the way\", and the lack of bare-metal acceleration available. Eventually designing and working on zero-GC systems led me to wonder why we didn't just write it in a compile-to-native language in the first place. I skipped both C and C++ and started hacking on x86 and amd64 assembly. It turned out that approaching C after that was fairly straightforward, with the dark corners of the standard libraries forming the still fairly gentle but steepest parts of the learning curve. I've been writing a toy project in it for a while and it's been a lot of fun. Implementing what I understsand to be Modern C's Best Practices have left me with something fairly usable :-O I've also looked at what you'll probably hear called Modern C++, but IMO it's and order of magnitude harder to get started on. I was watching Nicolai Josuttis' \"The Nightmare Of Initialization in C++\" the other day[1], which chimes with the first chapters of Scott Meyer's \"Effective Modern C++\". What a mess (C++ initialisation, not the book)! Java's potentially a better bet in the wider market as the number of job listings could be higher, but others may disagree. That said, Java is now a vast ecosystem and you'll need to learn at least one of its various build tools. I"},{"id":20991229,"author":"kinjba11","month":"2019-09","story":"Why Go and Not Rust?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"C++ has more historical baggage than any other mainstream language.","text":"C++ has more historical baggage than any other mainstream language. Consider something as simple as initialization: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18835108,"author":"forrestthewoods","month":"2019-01","story":"Initialization in C++ is Seriously Bonkers","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"C++ initialization is such a convoluted mess that CppCon was able to have an entire hour dedicated to \"The Nightmare of Initialization in C++\".","text":"C++ initialization is such a convoluted mess that CppCon was able to have an entire hour dedicated to \"The Nightmare of Initialization in C++\". [VIDEO] C++ has a rigorous standard. There is no rule that wasn't added for a reason. Nor are there lines of code with which you can specify which rule it must follow. The path to hell is paved with good intentions. Just because each step is logical and defensible doesn't mean the end result isn't fire and brimstone. I believe that good things are more than the sum of their parts. You often hear this with respect to creative works like movies or games. I think it also applies to source code, language design, and much more. The flipside is that bad things are less than the sum of their parts. I've shipped games that fall into this category. C++ initialization has a LOT of parts. And I would strongly argue the end result is less than the sum of those parts."}]},"8Vq993Td6ys":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence that Jobs originally intended web apps to be the iPhone's app model.","q":{"x":"Jobs initially wanted people to build web apps","i":33058539,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":39408385,"author":"midasuni","month":"2024-02","story":"It’s Official, Apple Kills Web Apps in the EU","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs's original web-app stance contradicts the claim about Apple's aims.","quote":"Not what Jobs said","text":"> Apple just made their true target crystal clear: they have always been aiming at web capabilities: Not what Jobs said [VIDEO] Originally there wasn’t an SDK at all."},{"id":33058539,"author":"chx","month":"2022-10","story":"Stadia died because no one trusts Google","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs initially wanted people to build web apps on iPhone.","quote":"Jobs initially wanted people to build web apps","text":"Jobs initially wanted people to build web apps [VIDEO] and the app store only became available for the second generation iPhone (which was called the iPhone 3G). The next year, the 3GS added HSDPA support which made online browsing pretty smooth. Combined with apps, it was finally ready for massive success."},{"id":24211671,"author":"RubberShoes","month":"2020-08","story":"Apple becomes first U.S. company to reach a $2T market cap","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs initially pushing web apps for the iPhone.","quote":"Steve was hellbent on WebApps","text":"What's funny is how much friction and persuasion it took to get them to actually do the App Store. Steve was hellbent on WebApps and I hope the history books recognize how much influence jailbreakers had in shifting the discussion to native apps [VIDEO]"}]},"AaA7kPfC5Hk":{"wy":"Cited as proof that a large jet airliner can safely do a barrel roll and as a famous test-pilot stunt.","q":{"x":"there's film of Tex Johnston's Dash-80 barrel roll","i":34618487,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34618487,"author":"fsckboy","month":"2023-02","story":"Easter egg in flight path of last 747 delivery flight","stance":"reference","why":"Film of Tex Johnston's Dash-80 barrel roll in 1955","quote":"there's film of Tex Johnston's Dash-80 barrel roll","text":"there's film of Tex Johnston's Dash-80 barrel roll [VIDEO] many years later, Boeing Chief Test Pilot John Cashman stated that just before he piloted the maiden flight of the Boeing 777 on Jun. 12, 1994, his last instructions from then Boeing President Phil Condit were “No rolls”. -- anecdote from [LINK] I wonder why \"the suits\" at Boeing are so against the barrel rolls? The pilots all agree it's not that big a deal, not dangerous, and anybody watching is going to love it. this article [LINK] says that the bigger the plane, the more dangerous because it will roll more slowly, and during parts of the roll particularly 90 degrees off \"flat\" there is no lift and you're going to be falling. I'm reminded of the tragic crash of a B-52 illustrating a similar circumstance, and this was the pilot's last flight before retiring [LINK]"},{"id":17742979,"author":"rl3","month":"2018-08","story":"Man Who Stole Plane Near Seattle Raises Troubling Security Question","stance":"reference","why":"Tex Johnston barrel-rolled a Boeing prototype","quote":"Tex Johnston for one","text":"> Who else can ever say they did a freaking barrel roll in a commercial airliner. Tex Johnston for one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10035627,"author":"cpncrunch","month":"2015-08","story":"60 years ago: The famous Boeing 707 prototype barrel roll over Lake Washington","stance":"reference","why":"Roll appears with little heading or pitch change","quote":"No significant heading or pitch changes that I can see","text":"[VIDEO] See around 1:56. No significant heading or pitch changes that I can see, although it is difficult to tell because the camera is so far away."},{"id":4989528,"author":"pedrocr","month":"2012-12","story":"The Real Hazards of E-Devices on Planes","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of the 1955 707 prototype barrel roll","quote":"if a 707 could do it safely in 1955[1] modern airliners should do just fine.","text":"Barrel rolls are low G maneuvers so properly executed they shouldn't cause any of those things. My memory had the barrel roll as a 1G maneuver but wikipedia tells me it gets up to 2-3g (probably to enter/exit the maneuver). Were you thinking of an aileron roll? I mean, if a 707 could do it safely in 1955[1] modern airliners should do just fine. (not that I support routine barrel rolls on airliners...) [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"JREkqCvLzSo":{"wy":"Cited as a joke or illustration in threads about Texas geography and speech.","m":[{"id":16337849,"author":"look_lookatme","month":"2018-02","story":"Talk Like a Texan: How Texans Use “Down,” “Out,” “Over,” and “Up”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Texas, almost formally, redefines the cardinal directions","text":"One BIG thing worth noting is Texas, almost formally, redefines the cardinal directions [1], so it's even harder to parse what up/down/out/over are. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37598778,"author":"RajT88","month":"2023-09","story":"Ship of Fools","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory","text":"Even that breaks down in different parts of Texas. Go to Austin and see what you think. Obligatory: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15192902,"author":"sremani","month":"2017-09","story":"Amazon to Build Second HQ in North America","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Also, Whole Foods is head-quartered in Austin","text":"Anybody who says People's Republic of Austin (ref: Bernie movie: [VIDEO] ) is not on progressive wing is kidding. Also, Whole Foods is head-quartered in Austin, so there could be \"synergy\" there."}]},"OPcV9uIY5i4":{"wy":"Cited as the classic example of arbitrary code execution inside a game, with later runs injecting Pong, Snake and Flappy Bird.","q":{"x":"it involves executing the contents of the controller registers as code, which requires frame-precise inputs","i":26363242,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26363242,"author":"duskwuff","month":"2021-03","story":"FizzBuzz Mario World: Learning Assembly Language and Having Some Fun","stance":"reference","why":"TAS-only arbitrary code execution exploit in Super Mario World.","quote":"it involves executing the contents of the controller registers as code, which requires frame-precise inputs","text":"Somewhat disappointed. I was expecting them to use the well-known arbitrary code execution exploit in this game to load their own implementation of FizzBuzz, cf. [VIDEO] The method used by Masterjun3 is TAS-only (it involves executing the contents of the controller registers as code, which requires frame-precise inputs), but there are some other methods which are more viable for a real-time attack."},{"id":19780376,"author":"politician","month":"2019-04","story":"Indie Game Removed from Switch After Dev Reveals It Has a Hidden Code Editor","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's wild because Super Mario Bros contains a built-in code editor (via arbitrary code execution easter eggs), and Nintendo is OK with that. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16316045,"author":"crankylinuxuser","month":"2018-02","story":"Apple's Failure to Check Kill Syscall Privilege “Not a Security Issue”","stance":"reference","why":"Exploit makes Super Mario World run Pong and Snake via controller inputs.","quote":"The result is that the exploiter then proceeds to make 2 new games inside SMW: Pong and Snake.","text":"[VIDEO] Video is about exploiting Super Mario world, on SNES. The result is that the exploiter then proceeds to make 2 new games inside SMW: Pong and Snake. Those games don't exist inside SMW. They were uploaded using controller inputs. All it takes is 1 hack."},{"id":14693986,"author":"7373737373","month":"2017-07","story":"Block-breaking game in vim 8.0","stance":"reference","why":"Example of arbitrary code execution inside Super Mario World.","quote":"arbitrary code execution in Super Mario World","text":"One of the most brilliant applications of this concept is the Pokémon Yellow Total Control Hack: [LINK] Similarly, arbitrary code execution in Super Mario World: [VIDEO] It's like transforming a chess game into Mikado just by playing it."},{"id":11772461,"author":"Buge","month":"2016-05","story":"Flappy Bird Clone Code Injected into Super Mario World for SNES by Hand","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier automated-input version of Super Mario World arbitrary code execution.","quote":"","text":"He's pointing out that although this type of thing has already been done before by automated inputs (such as this video [VIDEO] ), this is the first time a human has done it."},{"id":9409448,"author":"Scuds","month":"2015-04","story":"Pokemon Yellow Total Control Hack","stance":"reference","why":"Tool-assisted Super Mario World run injecting arbitrary code via controller inputs.","quote":"using programmed controllers to poke arbitrary code into memory and execute it","text":"Similar deal with super mario world, using programmed controllers to poke arbitrary code into memory and execute it. [VIDEO]"}]},"PtgKmzgIh4c":{"wy":"HN cites it as public evidence that Twitter ran GraalVM in production and saw significant performance improvements.","q":{"x":"Twitter has some talks on this where they claim signifiant performance improvements","i":20196468,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21853205,"author":"walkingolof","month":"2019-12","story":"The Road to Scala 3","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Twitter running Scala on GraalVM in production.","quote":"Twitter is a huge Scala shop and was one of the first to run Scala workloads on GraalVM in production.","text":">If you are a startup then Scala isn't a good fit. FAANG don't need to use Scala because.... Not a FANG, but close, Twitter is a huge Scala shop and was one of the first to run Scala workloads on GraalVM in production. Twitter’s Quest for a Wholly Graal Runtime by Chris Thalinger: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20196468,"author":"luu","month":"2019-06","story":"A Race of Two Compilers: GraalVM JIT versus HotSpot JIT C2 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Twitter talk claiming significant performance gains from Graal.","quote":"Twitter has some talks on this where they claim signifiant performance improvements","text":"It seems pretty good. Twitter has some talks on this where they claim signifiant performance improvements: [VIDEO] . I've heard that the Twitter JVM team has a road show where they've talked to some other large Scala users about the performance improvements. Initially, people are highly skeptical of the claims, but after trying Graal on their internal workloads, they generally see similar results. Here's a paper which has some explanations for why you might expect Graal to improve Scala performance: [LINK]"},{"id":19888334,"author":"tom_mellior","month":"2019-05","story":"GraalVM 19.0","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence Twitter publicly discussed using GraalVM.","quote":"Considering that Twitter has been talking about this in public for some time","text":"> They are not \"advertising a commercial license\". [LINK] \"GraalVM Enterprise is available for purchase and is free on Oracle Cloud.\" > They are saying NOT to use the Enterprise version in production [citation needed] > The only reference I've ever seen to Twitter using GraalVM in production is in this discussion thread. Considering that Twitter has been talking about this in public for some time, this statement is unlikely to make the point you were trying to make: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"aCbfMkh940Q":{"wy":"Posted as a joke reply wherever a drastic, scorched-earth solution fits.","m":[{"id":44930523,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2025-08","story":"Do things that don't scale, and then don't scale","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"That’s how bad things have gotten, and a lot of it is because it’s profitable to fan the flames of rancor. I don’t see it improving. I think there was a post, a couple of days ago, where some folks concluded that social media is unfixable. “Take off and nuke the entire site from orbit. It’s the only way to be sure.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":41077348,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2024-07","story":"AI crawlers need to be more respectful","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"One must assume most user IP edge proxies are compromised hosts. If someone paid for that list they were almost certainly conned, as the black hats regularly publish that content on their forums. These folks want as many users as possible in order to hide their nuisance traffic origin in the traffic noise. Allowing users known to have an active RAT or their \"proxy friends\" on a commercial site is not helping anyone.... especially the victims. [VIDEO] Worth studying the problem from time to time when you get bored of the antics. These folks are generally uninterested in positively contributing to any community, but rather show up to cause trouble for fun and profit. User API quotas are popular for a reason. =3"},{"id":40395118,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2024-05","story":"Wuffs: Wrangling Untrusted File Formats Safely","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The complex formats of tiff and tga specifications makes it nearly impossible to span all the edge-cases with unit-tests. A VM can be in a known-state snapshot, process pre/post signature logged/compared with a scripted debugger, and binary input/output stripped of non-compliant metadata/blobs at each stage of the pipeline if the process behaves as expected. I've yet to find a better method than Honeypots to sustainably mitigate the complex leaky dependency mess on traditional architectures. It has been my experience that \"all software is terrible, but some of it is useful\". It may just be my bias, but I see code smell getting worse in recent decades... Have a nice day, =3 [VIDEO]"},{"id":33216193,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: Are Whois Abuse Reports Useful?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The best response is to rate limit nuisance traffic to 1kbps, and setup fail2ban firewall rules to trip a ban on scans/bot-blind-probes. In general, abuse reports are often useful for kicking users from services involved in illegal activities. If you don't get a response, than black-hole the entire IP blocks and their routes. i.e. play possum, and they will eventually get bored enough to find a softer target. The other users are collateral damage, but if they aren't a customer... than who gives a toss. =) [VIDEO]"},{"id":21185878,"author":"Porthos9K","month":"2019-10","story":"Laugh HN: We need this new tag for fun things to bring happiness to HN:)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Ellen Ripley would approve.","text":"Ellen Ripley would approve. \"I say we take off and nuke the entire site from orbit. It's the only way to be sure.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11675757,"author":"mjolk","month":"2016-05","story":"Lawyer lawyer lawyer LAWYER lawyer LAWYER","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Nuke em from orbit\" is a quote from the movie Aliens","text":"\"Nuke em from orbit\" is a quote from the movie Aliens: [VIDEO] I think (hope) it's being used in that playful way."}]},"arss9V3A1VA":{"wy":"Cited in threads about San Francisco shoplifting and crime policy as an example of brazen theft.","m":[{"id":29021285,"author":"908B64B197","month":"2021-10","story":"End Amazon’s Surveillance Partnership with Police","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"What's the point of having a Ring Doorbell if property crimes aren't prosecuted? If incidents like this [0] don't get any conviction, I have little faith that Ring footage of vandalism and package theft will get anything done. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28067773,"author":"908B64B197","month":"2021-08","story":"The slow collapse of Amazon’s drone delivery dream","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Then what does law enforcement will do with the perpetrators? If they don't investigate this [0] will they really go after package thieves? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27241214,"author":"stevecalifornia","month":"2021-05","story":"San Francisco’s Shoplifting Surge","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it's just sickening to watch.","text":"There are many, many videos like this: [VIDEO] Warning, there is nothing NSFW, but it's just sickening to watch."},{"id":27241948,"author":"tablespoon","month":"2021-05","story":"San Francisco’s Shoplifting Surge","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"That's brazen.","text":"> here are many, many videos like this: [VIDEO] That's brazen. Some of the shoplifters were even wearing masks, but pulled them down to their chins."},{"id":27272693,"author":"premium-komodo","month":"2021-05","story":"I Was Robbed in San Francisco While the Cameras Rolled","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Is it possible that, despite good intentions, the policies being implemented there actually have very harmful effects?","text":"Yes, that seems to be how it is in SF. [VIDEO] Is it possible that, despite good intentions, the policies being implemented there actually have very harmful effects?"}]},"dTRKCXC0JFg":{"wy":"Commenters post it as a joke reply when someone misuses a word or term.","m":[{"id":48347663,"author":"tzs","month":"2026-05","story":"The Website Specification","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Nearly 80% of the commentators who have mentioned slop appear to be people who have the same relationship with the the word \"slop\" that Vizzini had with \"inconceivable\" [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46284787,"author":"stack_framer","month":"2025-12","story":"Rob Reiner has died","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I can't believe people voted me down for this! It's a direct quote from the movie, after Vizzini uses the word \"inconceivable\" you warthog-faced buffoons: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45553813,"author":"cadamsdotcom","month":"2025-10","story":"Crypto Sees More Than $6B in Liquidations","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> _literal_ blood on the street You keep using that word. I don’t think it means what you think it means. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29905662,"author":"marcus_holmes","month":"2022-01","story":"Disambiguating the ‘Observable Universe’","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> And the Hubble constant is decreasing: the relative velocities of celestial objects are (on average) growing more slowly than the distances between them. You keep using that word. I do not think it means what you think it means. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"hOSYiT2iG08":{"wy":"HN quotes it as a joke in threads about made-up words and vocabulary.","m":[{"id":29008460,"author":"jdshaffer","month":"2021-10","story":"This word does not exist","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I shall return interfrastically!","text":"Reminds me of the \"Black Adder\" episode where he meet the pompous inventor of the Dictionary and purposely sets about using made up words just to annoy him. \"I shall return interfrastically!\" (Relevant Clip Here: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":20235194,"author":"klipt","month":"2019-06","story":"Estimate the size of your English vocabulary (2014)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm anaspeptic, frasmotic, even compunctuous to have caused you such pericombobulations.","text":"\"I'm anaspeptic, frasmotic, even compunctuous to have caused you such pericombobulations.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":5010950,"author":"derleth","month":"2013-01","story":"It's Gibberish, But Italian Pop Song Still Means Something ","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here's Hugh Laurie as the Prince of Wales (the total idiot)","text":"> Folks seem unbelievably harsh on accents they know not to be the native one, which is every time a famous actor does an accent Either Hugh Laurie isn't famous, or you don't have any idea he's British. ;) Every time he plays House, he's doing a fake American accent. He just does it really, amazingly well. Here's Hugh Laurie as the Prince of Wales (the total idiot) in a short segment from the series \"Blackadder the Third\": [VIDEO]"}]},"iQdDRrcAOjA":{"wy":"Invoked as a joke or analogy whenever contractors or employees work for a morally dubious or doomed organization.","m":[{"id":35225206,"author":"ethbr0","month":"2023-03","story":"UBS Agrees to Buy Credit Suisse in Historic Deal to End Crisis","stance":"reference","why":"Clerks scene about independent contractors on the Death Star II","quote":"it's a reference to a debate in Clerks about independent contractors working on the Death Star II","text":"Because there's a part of HN too young to appreciate your comment, it's a reference to a debate in Clerks about independent contractors working on the Death Star II: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44170482,"author":"toyg","month":"2025-06","story":"Cinematography of “Andor”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"any of the other derivatives of the infamous Clerks dialogue","text":"This is the sort of reference I winked at - or any of the other derivatives of the infamous Clerks dialogue: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30847306,"author":"function_seven","month":"2022-03","story":"$625M worth of ETH drained on Axie Infinity's Ronin Network","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is like the contractors working on the Death Star when it was blown up.[1]","text":"This is like the contractors working on the Death Star when it was blown up.[1] They knew what they were working on. They knew the dangers. Can't cry for them when they're blown to smithereens. I've been asked on two separate occasions to work on some crypto startup idea. Aside from my skepticism that they were even worthwhile projects, I declined because hell no I'm not writing code that touches other people's money . [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18962934,"author":"dehrmann","month":"2019-01","story":"Bed that saved me from the Taliban","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of Clerks and the Death Star contractors","text":"Reminds me of Clerks and the Death Star contractors, though this line of thinking has scary implications for working in the World Trade Center, any federal building, or Youtube. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14053893,"author":"mabbo","month":"2017-04","story":"Uber said to use “sophisticated” software to defraud drivers, passengers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm reminded of the scene from 'Clerks' discussing Contractors[0]","text":"Both driver and passenger think they know the full truth of the matter for the financial transaction they're agreeing to, but they don't. There's implicit dishonesty in that, and when you combine dishonesty with money we call it 'fraud' usually. But let's set aside the question of whether it was legal. Was it moral? Software like this doesn't fall from the sky- management approved it, software teams wrote it, maintain it and system tests probably exist to validate it works... how do those developers feel okay about this? How do they not feel like they're cheating people out of money? When your Mom hears about it, and asks if you were part of it will you spend 20 minutes giving a long-winded answer about how it was actually not a bad thing? That's a bad sign, man. I'm reminded of the scene from 'Clerks' discussing Contractors[0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7257616,"author":"toyg","month":"2014-02","story":"Snowden Documents Reveal Surveillance and Pressure Tactics Aimed at WikiLeaks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Would you be a contractor building the Death Star?","text":">The people doing the work for GCHQ and the NSA are just like us. That's the scariest part: it's the banality of evil, updated for the XXI century. Would you turn down a safe and well-paid government job? Would you be a contractor building the Death Star? (for the record, this is not a new question: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":5988150,"author":"malandrew","month":"2013-07","story":"Students Question the NSA at Recruiting Session [audio]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'll refer to the discussion of Death Star politics and the impact on contractors","text":"I hate to invoke Godwin's Law because it never helps any discussion, so instead I'll refer to the discussion of Death Star politics and the impact on contractors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5415358,"author":"seanc","month":"2013-03","story":"See No Evil: The Case of Alfred Anaya","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this is barely humorous, and actually has a point","text":"I know humorous links are discouraged on Hacker News, but this is barely humorous, and actually has a point: [VIDEO] As an independent contractor he owed it to himself to think through his choices and protect himself more carefully than he did. That said, he did get hosed by a completely crazy and corrupt system. It's just that I think he should have known better."}]},"oH41gGBVpkE":{"wy":"Linked in memorial threads as his last video.","m":[{"id":33581776,"author":"St_Alfonzo","month":"2022-11","story":"Zeal OS is a modernized fork of the Temple Operating System","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"His last Video. Aug. 11, 2018.","text":"> \"It's good to be King. Wait. Maybe. I think maybe I'm just a bizarre little person who walks back and forth.\" [VIDEO] His last Video. Aug. 11, 2018."},{"id":23113422,"author":"wolfhumble","month":"2020-05","story":"TempleOS","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"He passed away August 11, 2018, just the same day he posted his last video on YouTube","text":"Oh, I wasn't aware of his passing . . . According to his Wikipedia page #1) He passed away August 11, 2018, just the same day he posted his last video on YouTube (the same as included on his TempleOS home page): [VIDEO] His Wikipedia #1) page says the following about his passing: \"Investigators could not determine whether his death was suicide or accidental, although the train engineer believed his death to be a suicide. The police report stated that Davis was walking with his back toward the train and that he turned around before the moment of impact\". Bless his soul! #1) [LINK]"},{"id":19707172,"author":"hugh4life","month":"2019-04","story":"Working as a librarian gave me PTSD symptoms","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The last place TempleOS's Terry A Davis did a video before he died was outside a library.","text":"The last place TempleOS's Terry A Davis did a video before he died was outside a library. [VIDEO] \"I really like this library. So uh, come here use their computer and smoke out here. They must have been freaking out because such an impure person was in their presence.\""},{"id":17895625,"author":"esaym","month":"2018-09","story":"Terry Davis of TempleOS has died","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"From his last video(Aug 11th 2018)","text":"The current theory is he was hit by a train: From his last video(Aug 11th 2018): [VIDEO] We see he is at the Dalles county library. ​ Later in the evening (Aug 11th, 2018), The Dalles Chronicle reports an unknown man was hit by a train: [LINK] ​ Reports mention Terminal street and either 1st or 2nd street. ​ A map: [LINK] Also the Terry Davis facebook page is more than likely not Terry. It appears that the twitter handle \"TheTemple\" created it and fooled his relatives into accepting the friend requests. The family has no reason to make any of this public, I doubt they realize the extent of his internet following. But \"TheTemple\" could see their private posts and pictures mentioning Terry's death from the friended \"Terry A Davis\" facebook account (which he then subsequently memorialized). A video here: [LINK]"},{"id":17912919,"author":"forapurpose","month":"2018-09","story":"Terry Davis has died","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"From his last video(Aug 11th 2018)","text":"The post linked to by the parent is just an unfounded rumor created by someone on Reddit, with very little basis: I'm going to call it. From his last video(Aug 11th 2018): [VIDEO] We see he is at the Dalles county library. ​Later in the evening (Aug 11th, 2018), The Dalles Chronicle reports an unknown man was hit by a train: [LINK]"},{"id":17944659,"author":"Born_Again","month":"2018-09","story":"Terry Davis Confirmation: Man killed by train had tech following","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It saddens me that people are more comfortable watching videos of gore than a person suffering from a mental illness. It saddens me that America lacks mental health awareness. > I can't believe this is real. About 1% of Americans suffer from schizophrenia [0] and for many of those, this video shows what they and their family face on a daily basis. You and the people around you probably don't ever experience what happens in those gore videos, but we are surrounded by people who suffer from schizophrenia and other mental disorders. There is currently no cure for schizophrenia and the best treatment is therapy and antipsychotic medication. If the person who is suffering from schizophrenia decides to pass on the medication and therapy (for reasons related to the disorder or because the side effects are worse than the benefits), it can be difficult to prevent situations like the one in the video of Terry from happening. Imagine you are in a room with 100 other people. It is likely that someone in that room has experienced paranoia or groundless thinking similar to what you saw in the video. Both them and their family have to learn how to deal with the symptoms and often do not get enough support. I encourage everyone to educate themselves on mental health and consider donating to an organization that supports people with mental illness [1]. Terry uploaded a video the day before he died [2] and also removed all of his previously uploaded videos from YouTube. That leads me to believ"}]},"oHg5SJYRHA0":{"wy":"Used as a joke link when someone posts a suspicious or unexpected URL.","m":[{"id":21332288,"author":"helb","month":"2019-10","story":"Firefox 70","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"youtube-dl + mpv mentioned by Angeo34 does exactly that","text":"I don't know what iina is, but youtube-dl + mpv mentioned by Angeo34 does exactly that (stream videos without pre-downloading them). Youtube-dl just gets the stream URL and mpv plays it. And it's easy to use, just: mpv [VIDEO]"},{"id":19225982,"author":"EGreg","month":"2019-02","story":"Mark Zuckerberg Promised a Clear History Tool Almost a Year Ago","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"When Facebook releases a tool to clear your history, how will you know it’s really cleared? It will probably work as well as these instructions you can already use to remove your digital fingerprint from Facebook and several other sites: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1598540,"author":"relix","month":"2010-08","story":"Pushing Our (Tweet) Button","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Correct me if I'm wrong, but I didn't find an option to disable using Twitter's own shortening service? As an extreme example, I find [LINK] less informative than [VIDEO] . If your site is still only at 3 characters ID, unlike Youtube, having a custom shortened domain from which users can tell what it is, generally, what they're clicking on, is a much better user experience than using a universal shortener which says nothing about the content."}]},"qoIvd3zzu4Y":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference for obsessing over trivial details like card design, fonts and CV templates.","m":[{"id":6030262,"author":"glurgh","month":"2013-07","story":"Ask HN: Advice on Business Cards","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] I can't think of a single time where I closed or did not close a deal on the basis of business cards, as a freelancer. Whether they were nice or I forgot them at home."},{"id":5751351,"author":"kstenerud","month":"2013-05","story":"Stop Using Arial & Helvetica","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The point being, if it takes 3x magnification and careful examination (after having been coached) to spot any difference, it's hardly worth worrying over, let alone admonishing use of one over the other. It just becomes another case of [VIDEO]"},{"id":4756204,"author":"amix","month":"2012-11","story":"The Best","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think this kind of obsession (about things that don't matter) was best portrayed in American Psycho's business card scene","text":"I think this kind of obsession (about things that don't matter) was best portrayed in American Psycho's business card scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1876046,"author":"barrkel","month":"2010-11","story":"Wearing a mask = being more confident.","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I wouldn't quite go as far as the business cards scene in American Psycho","text":"I don't think that's an accurate analogy. It doesn't matter what your digital cables are made of, so long as noise doesn't go above the threshold. Cheap copier paper, on the other hand, looks and feels like crap. The only reason you print stuff on paper is to communicate with another human being, and the paper is part of the message. I wouldn't quite go as far as the business cards scene in American Psycho ( [VIDEO] ) though."},{"id":1714951,"author":"pak","month":"2010-09","story":"LaTeX CV Template","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This thread is reminding me a bit too much of the business card scene from American Psycho","text":"This thread is reminding me a bit too much of the business card scene from American Psycho: [VIDEO] but uh, nice CV's guys."}]},"wmVkJvieaOA":{"wy":"HN cites it when arguing that debating bad-faith arguers hands them the framing and exposure.","q":{"x":"See \"Never Play Defense\" from the alt-right playbook","i":36332993,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":40908676,"author":"cassepipe","month":"2024-07","story":"The Right Kind of Stubborn","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Could we say that biologically/culturally receptive to performed dominance and being dominant has nothing to do with rationally understand the world. I think this is the whole point of the jock/geek binary opposition in culture even though, as all oppositionnal pairs in culture, they are often porquenolosdossed : Some people can perform dominance and do master rationality quite well and some can do neither. Maybe we don't notice it either because they don't fit the cultural mental map or because they are not part of our social milieus (too high or too low status) ? [VIDEO]"},{"id":38294348,"author":"Terr_","month":"2023-11","story":"The impossibility of rationally analyzing partisan news","stance":"reference","why":"Example of short, quippy, wrong messaging beating nuance.","quote":"","text":"Alas, it seems that in practice humans are often motivated by something \"short, quippy, and wrong\" [0] over something with more details like demarcating areas of ambiguity. So the \"better\" sources have an uphill fight to be influential. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36332993,"author":"asmor","month":"2023-06","story":"Reddit subs with millions of followers plan to extend the blackout indefinitely","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to argue engaging with bad-faith arguers is a losing game.","quote":"See \"Never Play Defense\" from the alt-right playbook","text":"This seems very naive to me. There are enough people yelling \"OK Groomer\" at me on certain platforms that I know the signal to noise ratio is bad enough that engaging is at best a waste of time and at worst throwing more soundbites than I can debunk or bystanders can dead so I lose the debate (for any bystander) by default. See \"Never Play Defense\" from the alt-right playbook: [VIDEO] And as an aside - HN is not guilt free on this topic too. There's a general tendency for comments to go right past the topic even outside this. For instance, every remote work thread turns into a generalized remote vs onsite debate, without the specifics of the article being weighed in. Even worse, HN also has a tendency to devolve into calling authors of posts deranged (or deranged, but with nicer choice of words) more for their identity and politics rather than the words they wrote, as was the case with Alyssa Rosenzweig's article being posted a few weeks ago. It simply isn't viable to assume good faith for everyone. I often (unwiesely) get into fights with transphobes that I shouldn't, and I had maybe 2 insightful well-reflected discussions over the past 10 years. Sometimes they'll complement me on not fitting their expectation of an \"SJW\", but then go on to call me a slur anyway. And when I say slur, I am very positive that this is the right classification. Unambiguously."},{"id":23939436,"author":"thaumaturgy","month":"2020-07","story":"The Four Quadrants of Conformism","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Isn't it funny how things like this are never written by the boring types? It's always those wild, maverick, enlightened types seeking to describe themselves and, along the way, describe others, but mostly to describe themselves in flattering terms, with just a light veneer of modesty. (The self-assessed MBTI INTJs are just fantastically entertaining at this.) \"All great ideas come from us,\" beams the self-described aggressively-independent-minded, \"and if we aren't allowed to champion horrible ideas, why, the world just won't be able to get on without us.\" There are so many coarse assertions in this argument, without any solid foundations or evidence or even thoughtful observation. Right from the first sentence: > One of the most revealing ways to classify people is by the degree and aggressiveness of their conformism. \"Arbitrary\" ways. It's spelled \"arbitrary\". There are a plethora of categorical little boxes that people can try to fit other people into, and some of those have value sometimes, but they often also cause people to see other people as only their boxes. [1] > Imagine a Cartesian coordinate system... Imagine never having seen /r/PoliticalCompassMemes [2]. As gross as it is, this kind of quadrant-categorization isn't new. > There are more passive people than aggressive ones, and far more conventional-minded people than independent-minded ones. So the passively conventional-minded are the largest group, and the aggressively independent-minded the smallest. This is"},{"id":22928343,"author":"some_furry","month":"2020-04","story":"FAQ Off: An Open Source Project to Defeat Harassment Mobs and Disinformation","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing the alt-right already controls the narrative.","quote":"The alt-right already takes control of the narrative.","text":"> All these tools for \"taking control of the narrative\" will be used by your enemies. The alt-right already takes control of the narrative. [VIDEO] Even if this platform ever \"took off\" enough to be used by bad people, the only thing that would happen is we'd have a level playing field."},{"id":19525532,"author":"KozmoNau7","month":"2019-03","story":"By Working Together, We Can Win Against Hate","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for argument that debating bad-faith actors only gives them exposure.","quote":"","text":"I completely disagree. These people are already on openly visible platforms, such as 4chan and 8chan, where the Christchurch terrorist was radicalized. By giving their hate and propaganda exposure on mainstream (social) media, you give them a platform to spread their ideology to more people. You have to realize that these people do not care about the truth, they do not care about debates, all they care about is being heard and seen. Debating them does not work, it only gives them more exposure. [LINK] The only way to deal with people who use these sorts of tactics is to deny them the \"debate\", deny them a platform and deplatform them from whichever platforms they already have. Shut off their access to the people they are trying to radicalize. [VIDEO] Obviously this does go somewhat against the \"rules\" for polite conversation and debate that we've all been taught. But there is no debate to be had with fascists, everything they say and do eventually boils down to \"we will kill you\". That is not an ideology you can reason with."}]},"xSVqLHghLpw":{"wy":"HN commenters post it as a joke whenever a thread involves correlation, causation, or unfalsifiable protective measures.","m":[{"id":47690117,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2026-04","story":"US cities are axing Flock Safety surveillance technology","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Crime's been descending from the COVID blip for a while, everywhere, Flock or otherwise. My city saw zero murders in Q1; 2021 saw ~15 by now. In other words: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40575393,"author":"rvnx","month":"2024-06","story":"'Extremely impressive': melanoma jab trial results excite doctors","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"reminds me of this Simpsons episode","text":"Such claims of protection, reminds me of this Simpsons episode: [VIDEO] It's true that the COVID-19 initial variants were very dangerous, and that the vaccines worked against these variants. However, the virus in 2024 is different from the one in 2019, but the side-effects, you are \"guaranteed\" to pay them. You really have to think twice."},{"id":39478181,"author":"Mordisquitos","month":"2024-02","story":"Nobody ever gets credit for fixing problems that never happened (2001) [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The issue is how do you provide the evidence for that. In other terms, how do you differentiate your effect from that of Homer Simpson's Bear Patrol and Lisa's tiger-repellant rock [0]? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39520496,"author":"eesmith","month":"2024-02","story":"Sky High Sabotage: Major Airlines Are Using TSA to Shut Down Competitor","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And here is a clip of the relevant scene, from the episode","text":"And here is a clip of the relevant scene, from the episode \"Much Apu About Nothing\" (1996), [VIDEO] Quoting from its IMDB entry at [LINK] : Lisa : Dad, what if I were to tell you that this rock keeps away tigers. Homer : Uh-huh, and how does it work? Lisa : It doesn't work. It's just a stupid rock. Homer : I see. Lisa : But you don't see any tigers around, do you? Homer : Lisa, I'd like to buy your rock."},{"id":33856265,"author":"itsokimbatman","month":"2022-12","story":"The War on General Purpose Computing","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Lisa Simpson has a rock she would like to sell you","text":"Lisa Simpson has a rock she would like to sell you: [VIDEO] I mean, yeah, I get it. I worry about general purpose computing going away too. I remember Stallman and Doctorow talking about this in the early 2000s and saw many iterations of this same thread pop up on Slashdot, HN, Digg, Reddit, etc. over the years. I dunno man, I guess I’m just skeptical over the dooming at this point. I think a bigger issue is less about whether we will be able to run whatever we want on our computer, and more that the internet has just become a cesspool of ads, clickbait, ragebait, and app sandboxed content that’s not discoverable by search engines. I think you’ll always have a fairly reasonable choice of computers to run whatever software you want on. But todays internet just isn’t very fun anymore."},{"id":34180263,"author":"heavyset_go","month":"2022-12","story":"Privacy Is OK","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Part of me is convinced that some people like that would be easily persuaded into putting cameras in their bedrooms if they were told it would keep them safe from burglars/murderers/terrorists/pedophiles/bears/tigers[1]/or whatever boogeyman they particularly don't like. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30266074,"author":"drzoltar","month":"2022-02","story":"Heuristics that almost always work","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Homer: Not a bear in sight. The Bear Patrol must be working like a charm. Lisa: That’s specious reasoning, Dad. Homer: Thank you, dear. Lisa: By your logic I could claim that this rock keeps tigers away. Homer: Oh, how does it work? Lisa: It doesn’t work. Homer: Uh-huh. Lisa: It’s just a stupid rock. Homer: Uh-huh. Lisa: But I don’t see any tigers around, do you? Homer: Lisa, I want to buy your rock. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29063329,"author":"aaronbrethorst","month":"2021-11","story":"The Patriot Act Has Threatened Freedom for 20 Years","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Homer : Well, there's not a bear in sight. The Bear Patrol is sure doing its job. Lisa : That's specious reasoning, Dad. Homer : Thank you, sweetie. Lisa : Dad, what if I were to tell you that this rock keeps away tigers. Homer : Uh-huh, and how does it work? Lisa : It doesn't work. It's just a stupid rock. Homer : I see. Lisa : But you don't see any tigers around, do you? Homer : Lisa, I'd like to buy your rock. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27339781,"author":"quickthrowman","month":"2021-05","story":"Improving ventilation will help curb SARS-CoV-2","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The UN hasn’t prevented war, nuclear weapons have. If your opponent has second-strike capability, starting a war is not a great idea. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20520670,"author":"philwelch","month":"2019-07","story":"How Turkey Purged Its Intellectuals","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"...so postwar Britain was at serious risk of being taken over by fascists? Probably something more like [VIDEO]"},{"id":20253509,"author":"eesmith","month":"2019-06","story":"Anecdotes Aren't Data","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And perhaps this rock keeps tigers away","text":"And perhaps this rock keeps tigers away - [VIDEO] . A lot of the 'terrorist plots' the government has found seem more contrived than meaningful. Eg, cases where if the government had done nothing, then it seems likely that nothing still would have happened. Certainly we know that funding was taken away from monitoring and investigating white supremacist terrorism networks in the US in order to look for external ones connected to Moslem. And we know that more people and damage has occurred from white supremacist terrorism. So, from an equally specious argument, perhaps some of GW Bush's policies lead to more deaths from terrorism."}]},"y39D4529FM4":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the famous demonstration of what happens to CPUs without thermal protection, especially the Athlon burning out.","q":{"x":"There's a famous TomsHardware video about that","i":35027256,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":44422660,"author":"kvemkon","month":"2025-06","story":"Blackwell: Nvidia's GPU","stance":"reference","why":"Famous Athlon cooler-removal demonstration.","quote":"the famous video [1] for Athlon 1400 and 1200","text":"Perhaps, I've formulated not exact enough. I was remembering what has been demonstrated in the famous video [1] for Athlon 1400 and 1200. Yes, the went not in flame but just in smoke. Back then the user was allowed to install a cooler direct onto the die though AMD CPU had no thermal protection (just thermal diode for emergency power-off the whole system by BIOS?). I have no confirmation for the following but I've considered that the pre-installed and hard and risky to remove the pretty massive heatspreader plate works not only as die physical protection but also as a minimal needed buffer for heat dissipation in case of rapid cooler removal. [1] \"What happens when the CPU cooler is removed?\" (Tom's Hardware Guide, 2001) [VIDEO] [LINK] P.S. Another rather philosophical question I have since a ventilator (not just some heatspreader) was required for the first time for CPU to operate as advertised. Can one consider that CPUs are actually factory overclocked since then? Even though not to the absolute limit, e.g. Celeron 333A (@66) up to 500 (@100) manually."},{"id":35027256,"author":"userbinator","month":"2023-03","story":"Fix Intel CPU Throttling on Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Famous demo of CPU overheating without thermal protection.","quote":"There's a famous TomsHardware video about that","text":"That CPU will degrade over time when run at that level of heat. If Intel warrants their CPUs to be at TjMax 24/7, it's a good sign that it shouldn't be a problem. I have not heard of overheating killing CPUs since the days when AMD's didn't have any thermal protection[1], and I've cleaned out machines which were heavily clogged with dust and thermally throttling all the time for many years (the service prompted by their owners complaining about their computers being slow.) In one memorable case the push-in heatsink pins must've been originally not fully inserted, since they came out at some point and the heatsink was not even touching the CPU anymore, yet the CPU kept running for years in that state. [1] There's a famous TomsHardware video about that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11495820,"author":"TazeTSchnitzel","month":"2016-04","story":"Skylake's Linux power management is dreadful you shouldn't buy until it's fixed","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Yes, otherwise you end up with this","text":"Yes, otherwise you end up with this: [VIDEO] ;) (I'm well aware it's not a realistic experiment, but it's a fun demonstration nonetheless.)"}]},"z_wImaGRkNY":{"wy":"HN mentions it only in passing as a joke about AirPods.","m":[{"id":13340235,"author":"slantyyz","month":"2017-01","story":"Apple cuts Tim Cook's pay 15% for missing sales goals","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Conan made fun of the airpods","text":">> I think the \"perception\" you are talking about is just from gossipy tech blogs and people on Internet forums who have made a hobby out of getting upset about things like this. Well a lot of these things have made the rounds on late night talk shows, which are watched by normal people (on TV and on viral YouTube clips). Colbert made fun of the book [1]. Conan made fun of the airpods [2] and even has an Apple Spoofs playlist [3]. Kimmel made fun of Apple fans [4] a few months ago. While Conan doesn't get big TV ratings, his Youtube videos do get a lot of views. I'm guessing the reason why Fallon doesn't do as many spoofs is because he is in some iPhone ads. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":20952447,"author":"vaxman","month":"2019-09","story":"What is the new Apple U1 chip?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"or Conan's AirPods commercial","text":"or Conan's AirPods commercial [VIDEO] remember that \"Quora\" predicted Apple would be putting U1s in AirPods too"},{"id":12523838,"author":"chris_wot","month":"2016-09","story":"When Nintendo got rid of the headphone jack","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The difference here is that the primary purpose of an iPhone is to make phone calls. And uses multimedia, like music. Leading to this: [VIDEO]"}]}});
